\par\par \b \uc2 IMF says  \highlight22\uc2 Japan intervention \highlight\uc2  appropriate.   \b0\par\par\uc2 383 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 25 Februar 2004\par 07:04\par \uc2 Reuters News\par \uc2 LBA\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 2004 Reuters Limited   \par \par \uc2 TOKYO, Feb 25 (Reuters) - International Monetary Fund (IMF) Managing Director Horst Koehler endorsed on Wednesday \b \highlight22\uc2 Japan\b0 \highlight\uc2 's \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign exchange intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2 , saying it was appropriate given a lack of policy tools to reflate the economy.   \par \par \uc2 But Koehler, speaking during a visit to Tokyo where he met top government officials, added that he viewed the policy as temporary and not aimed at fixing \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  rates.   \par \par \uc2 "It is pragmatic, and helps stabilise the financial system and battle deflation," he told a news conference.   \par \par \uc2 "But I understand it is temporary, among limited options, and is not based on a fixed \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  rate target."   \par \par \uc2 Tokyo sold a record 20 trillion yen ($184.8 billion) last year to curb the yen's rise.   \par \par \uc2 It also sold a monthly record of over seven trillion yen in January alone and is believed to have \b \highlight22\uc2 intervened\b0 \highlight\uc2  intermittently since then.   \par \par \uc2 Some market participants suspect yen-selling \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  has grown to around 10 trillion yen so far this year.   \par \par \b \highlight22\uc2 Currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  policy is controlled by the \b \highlight22\uc2 Ministry of Finance\b0 \highlight\uc2 , with the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  acting as the government's agent in the \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  market.   \par \par \uc2 Koehler, who met with Finance Minister Sadakazu Tanigaki among others on Tuesday, said he believed Tokyo's massive \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  was not aimed at boosting exports.   \par \par \uc2 "My understanding, also confirmed in discussions with the finance minister, is that the option was taken because of the limited number of other options to avoid spiralling deflation, to secure the integrity of the financial system, and it worked," he said.   \par \par \uc2 On Japan's monetary policy, Koehler said the Bank of Japan should continue its current "quantitative" easing policy of flooding the markets with liquidity to keep short-term interest rates at zero.   \par \par \uc2 He lauded the efforts of BOJ Governor Toshihiko Fukui, saying that he supported the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2 's "increasingly proactive approach", which he said has helped improve the economic outlook.   \par \par \uc2 Koehler said growth in Japan this calendar year would likely be significantly stronger than previously envisaged, forecasting real growth of three percent or better. The IMF's own forecast for Japan in 2004 is 2.2 percent growth.   \par \par \uc2 "Strong net exports plus the strong pick-up in investment are good indicators that the recovery has a good chance to be sustained," he said. ($1=108.21 yen).    \par \par \uc2 Dokument LBA0000020040225e02p000lx\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw172\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2580\pichgoal600
