\par\par \b \uc2 UPDATE 2-IMF head understands Japan's "pragmatic" yen policy.  \b0\par\par\uc2 By Tamawa Kadoya  \par \uc2 580 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 25 Februar 2004\par 07:55\par \uc2 Reuters News\par \uc2 LBA\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 2004 Reuters Limited  \par \par \uc2  TOKYO, Feb 25 (Reuters) - \b \highlight22\uc2 Japan\b0 \highlight\uc2 's controversial policy of \b \highlight22\uc2 intervening\b0 \highlight\uc2  heavily in the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  market has helped the economy and been useful in fighting deflation, the head of the International Monetary Fund (IMF) said on Wednesday.   \par \par \uc2  However, this \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  - with the Bank of \b \highlight22\uc2 Japan\b0 \highlight\uc2  buying dollars to slow a rise in the yen - should be seen as a temporary measure, IMF Managing Director Horst Koehler said.   \par \par \uc2  Speaking on a visit to Tokyo where he has met top government officials, Koehler said \b \highlight22\uc2 Japan\b0 \highlight\uc2 's \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  was appropriate, given the lack of other policy tools available to reflate the economy.   \par \par \uc2  "It is pragmatic, and helps stabilise the financial system and battle deflation," he told a news conference.   \par \par \uc2  "But I understand it is temporary, among limited options, and is not based on a fixed \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  rate target."   \par \par \uc2  Koehler, who met with Finance Minister Sadakazu Tanigaki among others on Tuesday, said he believed Tokyo's massive \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  was not aimed at boosting exports.   \par \par \uc2  "My understanding, also confirmed in discussions with the finance minister, is that the option was taken because of the limited number of other options to avoid spiralling deflation, to secure the integrity of the financial system, and it worked," he said.   \par \par \uc2  Tokyo sold a record 20 trillion yen ($185 billion) last year to curb the yen's rise and many analysts believe this was aimed at preserving the competitiveness of exporters, who have been the driving force behind Japan's economic recovery.   \par \par \uc2  It also sold a monthly record of over seven trillion yen in January and some market participants suspect yen-selling \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  has grown to 10 trillion yen so far this year.   \par \par \b \highlight22\uc2 Currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  policy is controlled by the \b \highlight22\uc2 Ministry of Finance\b0 \highlight\uc2 , with the Bank of Japan (BOJ) acting as its agent in the market.   \par \par \uc2  PRAISE FOR FUKUI   \par \par \uc2  On Japan's monetary policy, Koehler said the BOJ should continue its "quantitative easing" policy of flooding the markets with liquidity to keep short-term interest rates near zero.   \par \par \uc2  He applauded the efforts of BOJ Governor Toshihiko Fukui, with whom he also held talks, saying he supported the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2 's "increasingly proactive approach", which he said had helped improve the economic outlook.   \par \par \uc2  The IMF has until recently been sharply critical of Japan's monetary policy, saying more could be done to tackle a five-year decline in prices which has hobbled the economy.   \par \par \uc2  The BOJ unexpectedly eased its monetary policy further in January despite upgrading its assessment of the economy, saying it was to put the economy on a solid footing.   \par \par \uc2  Deflation remained a concern, Koehler said, but growth in Japan this calendar year would probably be significantly stronger than previously envisaged. Real growth would be closer to "three percent or possibly even beyond that", he said.   \par \par \uc2  The IMF's own forecast for 2004 is 2.2 percent growth.   \par \par \uc2  "Strong net exports plus the strong pick-up in investment are good indicators that the recovery has a good chance to be sustained," he said.   \par \par \uc2  Koehler reiterated the IMF's long-standing view that Japan should fix its huge fiscal deficit over the medium term.   \par \par \uc2  He welcomed the government's goal of achieving a primary surplus by the early 2010s, saying it was a feasible target.   \par \par \uc2  Japan's long-term government debt is set to rise above 140 percent of gross domestic product in the fiscal year starting in April. ($1=108.21 yen) (Additional reporting by Jonathan Standing).   \par \par \uc2 Dokument LBA0000020040225e02p000hd\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw184\pich22\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2760\pichgoal330
