\par\par \b \uc2 Dollar Soars On Speculation Of ECB  \highlight22\uc2 Intervention \highlight\uc2  On Euro  \b0\par\par\uc2 By Tom Barkley and Jamie McGeever  \par \uc2 Of DOW JONES NEWSWIRES  \par \uc2 646 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 25 Februar 2004\par 17:29\par \uc2 Dow Jones Capital Markets Report\par \uc2 CM\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 2004 Dow Jones & Company, Inc.  \par \par \uc2 NEW YORK (Dow Jones)--The dollar has soared against European \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2  Wednesday on renewed speculation that euro-zone policy makers could take action to counter the strength of the common European \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2 .  \par \par \uc2 Traders said the move was triggered in part by a report from the Market News service, which cited unnamed sources from the European \b \highlight22\uc2 Central Bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  as saying the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  was prepared to \b \highlight22\uc2 intervene\b0 \highlight\uc2  in \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  markets in concert with the Bank of \b \highlight22\uc2 Japan\b0 \highlight\uc2  to weaken the euro. The report also said the ECB would discuss cutting interest rates at its next meeting in March.  \par \par \uc2 Others in the market downplayed the report however, which seemed out of line with recent official comments from ECB officials. Bank of France Governor Christian Noyer said earlier Wednesday that the dollar's decline and euro's appreciation are "natural movements" for the \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  markets.  \par \par \uc2 An ECB spokesperson had no comment on the news report or the market speculation.  \par \par \uc2 Other factors were conspiring to send the dollar higher in any case. An option that expired at 10 a.m. EST (1500 GMT) - when the dollar first spiked - was believed to have set off a bout of dollar buying, as well.  \par \par \b \highlight22\uc2 Currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2  have become increasingly volatile in recent sessions amid uncertainly about whether the dollar's downtrend is still intact, and speculators may be simply taking the opportunity to unload short-dollar positions built up during Tuesday's selloff in the U.S. \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  after the move stalled out against the euro overnight around $1.27.  \par \par \uc2 Neal Kimberly, head of \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  sales at Bank of Tokyo Mitsubishi in London, said the report speculating on ECB \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  was likely just the excuse dealers were looking for to unwind overextended positions built up overnight. "It's just position squeezing from a market that got caught long euro and sterling," Kimberly said.  \par \par \uc2 Enrico Caruso, senior dealer at Tempest Asset Management, a \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  hedge fund based in Irvine, Calif., also said talk of a possible ECB rate cut flying around the market was the ideal catalyst to square positions. "The market simply got caught near the topside again," Caruso said, predicting a probe to the downside around $1.24-$1.2450.  \par \par \uc2 On the upside, Kimberly said bids from longer-term investors dry up on approaches to $1.2650.  \par \par \uc2 Marc Chandler, chief \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  strategist at HSBC Bank in New York, agreed that investors were using the report as an excuse to exit positions, saying that he didn't see the story "breaking any new ground."  \par \par \uc2 Euro-zone officials have already established that they wouldn't rule out \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  as a policy tool, said Chandler. He thinks a rate cut is unlikely at the ECB's March 4 meeting.  \par \par \uc2 At around 11:30 AM EST (1630 GMT), the euro was trading around $1.2507, down sharply from $1.2685 late Tuesday in New York, and also well off against the yen at Y136.20 compared with Y137.20.  \par \par \uc2 The dollar was up against the yen at Y108.84 from Y108.15 late Tuesday, and stronger against the Swiss franc at CHF1.2577 from CHF1.2415. Sterling was at $1.8800, down from $1.8910 late Tuesday in New York.  \par \par \uc2 The dollar's slow climb back from the lows overnight was also helped by comments by German Chancellor Gerhard Schroeder that the ECB should consider a rate cut to counter the euro's rise.  \par \par \uc2 Schroeder is meeting with U.S. President George Bush later this week, and he is expected to bring up the topic of the euro's exchange rate against the dollar.  \par \par \uc2 -By Tom Barkley, Dow Jones Newswires; 201-938-4385  \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par     tom.barkley@dowjones.com \par    -By Jamie McGeever, Dow Jones Newswires; 201 938-2096; \par     jamie.mcgeever@dowjones.com \par    \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 [ 02-25-04 1129ET ]  \f28 \par \par \uc2 Dokument CM00000020040225e02p0002k\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw200\pich25\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal3000\pichgoal375
