\par\par \b \uc2 Dollar Climbs on Fears of European  \highlight22\uc2 Intervention \highlight\b0\par\par\uc2 By Tom Barkley  \par \uc2 Of DOW JONES NEWSWIRES  \par \uc2 900 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 25 Februar 2004\par 22:57\par \uc2 Dow Jones Business News\par \uc2 DJON\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 2004 Dow Jones & Company, Inc.  \par \par \uc2 NEW YORK -- The dollar posted a strong recovery Wednesday, as fears of official European action to counter euro strength sent short-term investors bailing out of the single \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2 .  \par \par \uc2 The dollar had been on the rebound from a selloff the previous day when a news report suggesting possible \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  or an interest-rate cut by the European \b \highlight22\uc2 Central Bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  helped turn the move into a full-blown rally.  \par \par \uc2 Market News International cited unnamed sources from the European \b \highlight22\uc2 Central Bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  as saying the bank was prepared to \b \highlight22\uc2 intervene\b0 \highlight\uc2  in \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  markets in concert with the Bank of \b \highlight22\uc2 Japan\b0 \highlight\uc2  to weaken the euro. The report also said the European \b \highlight22\uc2 Central Bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  would discuss cutting interest rates at its next meeting on March 4.  \par \par \uc2 Several market participants downplayed the report which seemed out of line with recent official comments from European \b \highlight22\uc2 Central Bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  officials. Bank of France Governor Christian Noyer said Wednesday that the dollar's decline and euro's appreciation are "natural movements" for the \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign-exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  markets. A European \b \highlight22\uc2 Central Bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  spokesperson had no comment on the news report or the market speculation.  \par \par \uc2 Marc Chandler, chief \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  strategist at HSBC Bank in New York, said the story wasn't "breaking any new ground." He said investors were using the dollar's turnaround as an opportunity to exit short-dollar positions that had been placed as recently as Tuesday.  \par \par \uc2 With the euro's overnight ascent faltering around $1.27, speculators have essentially taken their bets back off the table for a renewal of the European \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2 's climb toward highs above $1.29. "Investors were scrambling for an excuse for the euro to slide after everybody got caught leaning the other way, thinking that the uptrend had resumed," said Mr. Chandler.  \par \par \uc2 Some observers think a European \b \highlight22\uc2 Central Bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  rate cut may well be in the works, although that appears to be very much a minority view. Michael Hume, chief European economist at Lehman Brothers in London, said in a research note he expects a one-quarter percentage-point cut by the European \b \highlight22\uc2 Central Bank\b0 \highlight\uc2 , or at least for the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  to "signal an easing bias" on March 4.  \par \par \uc2 The dollar's slow climb back from the lows hit overnight was also helped by comments from German Chancellor Gerhard Schroeder that the European \b \highlight22\uc2 Central Bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  should consider a rate cut to counter the euro's rise. Mr. Schroeder is meeting with U.S. President George Bush later this week, and he is expected to bring up the topic of the euro's exchange rate against the dollar.  \par \par \uc2 While the visit isn't likely to result in any policy shifts, it is contributing to the uncertainty and general market jitters that has resulted in large swings in \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2  in recent sessions.  \par \par \uc2 "The more this volatility kicks in, the greater it's going to discourage dollar bears," said Mike Malpede, senior \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign-exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  analyst at Refco Group Ltd., a futures brokerage, in Chicago. "But if you look at the primary driving factors in the trade, they're still not clearly decided."  \par \par \uc2 The dollar's decline has been due in large part to low U.S. interest rates, which make it more difficult to attract enough foreign investment to fund the massive current account deficit.  \par \par \uc2 Federal Reserve Chairman Alan Greenspan might have also provided a boost to the dollar in testimony Wednesday before the House Budget Committee, saying the economy has achieved a "more vigorous expansion."  \par \par \uc2 Mr. Greenspan said he expects the decline in inflation "to continue this way for a while," though the threat of deflation is "much lower" than it was a year ago.  \par \par \uc2 Mr. Malpede said Mr. Greenspan's comments may fuel expectations that rate increases would come sooner than expected, but that nothing suggests that the dollar's longer-term decline has ended.  \par \par \uc2 Late Wednesday, the euro was trading around $1.2490, down sharply from $1.2685 late Tuesday in New York, and was also lower against the yen, at 136.21 yen versus 137.20 yen. The dollar was stronger against the Swiss franc at 1.2597 francs from 1.2415 francs, while sterling fell to $1.8691 from $1.8910. The dollar rose against the yen, to 109.03 yen from 108.15 yen.  \par \par \uc2 The Bank of Japan, acting on behalf of \b \highlight22\uc2 Japan\b0 \highlight\uc2 's \b \highlight22\uc2 Ministry of Finance\b0 \highlight\uc2 , was widely suspected of \b \highlight22\uc2 intervening\b0 \highlight\uc2 , selling yen to push the U.S. \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  back above 108 yen. However, the dollar's ability to hold above 109 yen didn't appear to need any official help, as investors were buying dollars across the board.  \par \par \uc2 Japan has spent a record amount of money in 2003 and so far this year to keep the yen from appreciating too quickly and threatening the export-led economy. However, the government has apparently switched tactics over the past week, continuing to buy dollars on the way up after spending the previous few weeks defending 105 yen.  \par \par \uc2 Some estimates put \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  levels this week at around $7 billion to $8 billion.  \par \par \uc2 The Finance Ministry received an unexpected endorsement for its policy Wednesday from the International Monetary Fund, with managing director Horst Koehler calling \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  a "pragmatic" way to counter deflationary forces.  \par \par \uc2 -By Tom Barkley, Dow Jones Newswires; 201-938-4385  \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par     tom.barkley@dowjones.com   \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 (Jamie McGeever contributed to this story.) [ 02-25-04 1657ET ]  \f28 \par \par \uc2 Dokument DJON000020040225e02p0000x\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw184\pich22\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2760\pichgoal330
