\par\par \b \uc2 IMF Koehler:Japan  \highlight22\uc2 FX \highlight\uc2  Steps OK Given Few Monetary Options  \b0\par\par\uc2 1,441 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 25 Februar 2004\par 04:50\par \uc2 Dow Jones International News\par \uc2 DJI\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 2004 Dow Jones & Company, Inc.  \par \par \uc2 TOKYO (Dow Jones)--The head of the International Monetary Fund gave qualified support Wednesday for \b \highlight22\uc2 Japan\b0 \highlight\uc2 's massive yen-selling \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  campaign, calling it a "pragmatic" way to fight deflation given a lack of other monetary options.  \par \par \uc2 "I do think it was an appropriate policy stance," IMF Managing Director Horst Koehler told a press conference after wrapping up two days of talks with Japanese officials.  \par \par \uc2 He added, however, that he believes Japanese officials see \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  as "a temporary policy," and are not trying to bolster exporters by fixing the yen's exchange rate at an unfairly low level. [ 25-02-04 0412GMT ]  \par \par \uc2 Japan has poured more than Y27 trillion into \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  markets since the start of 2003 in an effort to curb the yen's strength.  \par \par \uc2 Koehler said his talks with Prime Minister Junichiro Koizumi and other officials had convinced him that \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  has been aimed primarily at slowing the pace of domestic price falls and bolstering Japan's weak financial system.  \par \par \uc2 "I do not think ... that the objective of these \b \highlight22\uc2 interventions\b0 \highlight\uc2  has been to boost exports," he said.  \par \par \uc2 "It was an option taken because of the limited number of other options to avoid spiraling deflation and secure the integrity of the financial system here - and it worked."  \par \par \uc2 With Japanese interest rates already at virtually zero and fiscal policy constrained by ballooning deficits, battling price falls through \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  policy was "a practical step," he said.  \par \par \uc2 His comments are likely to embolden Japan's Finance Ministry to continue to cap the yen's rise, despite complaints from Europe and elsewhere that the policy has forced the euro and other \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2  higher, as they absorb a larger share of the dollar's broad-based decline.  \par \par \uc2 In addition to his meeting with Koizumi, Koehler also held talks with Bank of Japan Governor Toshihiko Fukui, Finance Minister Sadakazu Tanigaki and Economy Minister Heizo Takenaka. He is scheduled to leave for Seoul later Wednesday.  \par \par \uc2 Koehler said he supported the Bank of Japan's "increasingly proactive approach" to monetary policy under Fukui, who was appointed to head the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  last year.  \par \par \uc2 "We do think that, for the time being, the Bank of Japan should continue with its zero interest rate policy" despite a recent pickup in economic growth, he said, adding deflation "remains a concern" for Japan's economy.  \par \par \uc2 Despite such worries, however, Koehler was upbeat about the recent strong performance of the world's second-largest economy.  \par \par \uc2 Japan's gross domestic product grew at a 7.0% annualized clip in the quarter to December, the fastest pace in 13 years and the best showing among major economies.  \par \par \uc2 "Japan's economic prospects have clearly brightened," he said.  \par \par \uc2 He said he believed Japan's economy would grow at a significantly faster rate this year than the 2.2% pace forecast by the IMF last September.  \par \par \uc2 "In our view the figure goes more to 3.0% and possibly beyond that," he said.  \par \par \uc2 He urged Japan's government to use the "golden opportunity" of stronger growth to push ahead with structural reforms and gradually reduce the country's massive public debt.  \par \par \uc2 Much of the discussion focused on exchange rates, however.  \par \par \uc2 Addressing China's fixed exchange rate system, a focus of keen interest among investors and policy makers, Koehler urged Beijing to let markets have a larger say in setting the value of its \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2 , the yuan.  \par \par \uc2 "I do think that it is in the self-interest of China to move to more flexibility," he said.  \par \par \uc2 He added, however, that China's trading partners should "accept (China's) decision to think about such kinds of movements in gradual steps."  \par \par \uc2 Beijing fixes the yuan's exchange rate to a razor-thin range close to CNY8.28 to the U.S. dollar, a level most economists believe sharply undervalues the Chinese \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2 .  \par \par \uc2 There have been signs recently, however, that Chinese officials may be preparing to loosen their grip on the yuan, perhaps by widening its official trading band.  \par \par \uc2 More generally, Koehler said the dollar's two-year decline against major \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2  has helped alleviate international trade imbalances. But he urged economic officials to focus less on exchange rates and more on spurring growth at home.  \par \par \uc2 "I do think that developments (in exchange rates) up to now have contributed to a decline in these global imbalances," Koehler said. "But we should concentrate now on strengthening growth based on domestic sources."  \par \par \uc2 -By Jonathan Soble, Dow Jones Newswires; 813-5255-2929; jonathan.soble@dowjones.com  \par \par \uc2 -Edited by Kirsty Mackenzie and Hugh Lawson [ 25-02-04 0605GMT ]  \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par    By Jonathan Soble \par    Of DOW JONES NEWSWIRES \par    \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 TOKYO (Dow Jones)--The head of the International Monetary Fund gave qualified support Wednesday for Japan's aggressive campaign to curb the yen's rise, calling it an effective way to fight deflation given a lack of other monetary policy options.  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 The high-level endorsement could embolden Japan's \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 Ministry of Finance\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2 , which has drawn criticism from some of Japan's trade partners by weighing the yen down with more than Y27 trillion in market \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 intervention\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  since the start of 2003.  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 IMF Managing Director Horst Koehler told a press conference that \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 intervention\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  was "an appropriate policy stance," adding he believed it was "a temporary policy" that was not designed to give Japanese exporters an unfair competitive edge.  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 "It was an option taken because of the limited number of other options to avoid spiraling deflation and secure the integrity of the financial system here - and it worked," he said, noting recent improvement in Japan's long-slumping economy.  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Koehler said conversations with Prime Minister Junichiro Koizumi and other officials had convinced him that \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 intervention\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  has been used primarily as an adjunct of monetary policy, rather than as a trade weapon.  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 That view appeared to be contradicted, however, by comments from Japan's trade minister, Shoichi Nakagawa.  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Nakagawa told Dow Jones Newswires and CNBC Asia Pacific in an interview that a strong yen wasn't desirable because exports have been fueling Japan's economic rebound.  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 "If you understand that exports have been driving the economy, then a stronger yen isn't a good thing," Nakagawa said.  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Critics in Europe, in particular, complain that Japan's relentless yen selling has hurt their exporters by forcing the euro to rise excessively, as it absorbs a larger share of the dollar's broad-based decline.  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Nevertheless, Koehler called \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 intervention\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  a "pragmatic" way to fight deflation in \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 Japan\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2 , where interest rates have already been pushed to virtually zero and fiscal policy is constrained by ballooning deficits.  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Koehler praised Japan's "increasingly proactive approach" to monetary policy under Bank of Japan Governor Toshihiko Fukui, who was appointed to head the \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 central bank\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  last year.  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 He said he expected Japan's economy to grow 3.0% in 2004 "and possibly beyond that," outpacing the IMF's latest forecast, made last September, for a 2.2% expansion.  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 "Japan's economic prospects have clearly brightened," he said.  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 The improved view reflects a startling 7.0% annualized expansion in Japanese gross domestic product in the quarter to December, the fastest growth in 13 years and the best showing among major economies.  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Much of Wednesday's press conference focused on exchange rates, however.  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Asked about China's fixed \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currency\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  system - a focus of keen interest among investors and policy makers - Koehler urged Beijing to let markets have a larger say in setting the value of the yuan.  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 "I do think that it is in the self-interest of China to move to more flexibility," he said.  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 He added, however, that China's trading partners should give Beijing room to loosen its grip on the yuan "in gradual steps."  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 China fixes the yuan's exchange rate to a razor-thin range close to CNY8.28 to the U.S. dollar, a level most economists believe sharply undervalues the Chinese \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currency\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2 .  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 There have been signs recently, however, that Chinese officials may be preparing to loosen their grip on the yuan, perhaps by widening its official trading band.  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 More generally, Koehler said the dollar's two-year decline against major \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currencies\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  has helped alleviate international trade imbalances. But he urged economic officials to focus less on exchange rates and more on spurring growth at home.  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 "I do think that developments (in exchange rates) up to now have contributed to a decline in these global imbalances," Koehler said. "But we should concentrate now on strengthening growth based on domestic sources."  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 -By Jonathan Soble, Dow Jones Newswires; 813-5255-2929; jonathan.soble@dowjones.com  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 James Simms and Shigeru Sato contributed to this item  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 -Edited by Kirsty Mackenzie, Hugh Lawson and Alastair Gale [ 25-02-04 1003GMT ]  \f28 \par \par \uc2 Dokument DJI0000020040225e02p0003v\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw184\pich22\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2760\pichgoal330
