\par\par \b \uc2 BOJ to mull policy, further easing seen unpopular.   \b0\par\par\uc2 By Ritsuko Ando   \par \uc2 712 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 24 Februar 2004\par 06:20\par \uc2 Reuters News\par \uc2 LBA\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 2004 Reuters Limited   \par \par \uc2 TOKYO, Feb 24 (Reuters) - With data pointing to a healthy economic recovery and attention shifting to the risks of trying too hard to beat deflation, the Bank of Japan is expected to keep monetary policy unchanged at a meeting on Thursday.   \par \par \uc2 Japan's economy is performing better than at any time in the past 13 years, and analysts say the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2 's aggressive easing stance of the past year could do more harm than good by undermining its credibility.   \par \par \uc2 "The problem is that by easing so aggressively, the aim of the BOJ's policies have become more ambiguous. As a result, its decisions become less effective in the eyes of the market," said Takashi Kodama, senior economist at Daiwa Institute of Research.   \par \par \uc2 The BOJ kept policy unchanged at its previous meeting, but investors are still talking about its surprise decision in January to ease policy by raising the reserves target to 30-35 trillion yen ($277-323 billion) from 27-32 trillion yen.   \par \par \uc2 That shocked the market because the economy is showing signs of improvement, and led some analysts to suspect the BOJ was responding to political pressure instead of trends in the economy and demand for funds.   \par \par \uc2 Critics say repeatedly raising the reserves target - the target amount for current accounts deposits held by commercial banks at the BOJ - has done little to boost business activity.   \par \par \uc2 Data earlier this month showed bank lending fell in January for the 73rd straight month, and economists expect little change as banks and their borrowers remain wary of taking risks and focus instead on cutting debt.   \par \par \uc2 "The market doesn't believe the easing does any good. And the BOJ hasn't clearly stated its benefits, so the question is why they keep doing it," Kodama said.   \par \par \uc2 DEFLATION NOT OVER   \par \par \uc2 Some say the BOJ is right to act, as deflation is continuing.   \par \par \uc2 The consumer price index (CPI) has been near zero percent year-on-year in recent months but economists say it is being lifted by a temporary jump in the price of rice and is still stuck in a five-year downtrend.   \par \par \uc2 While some wonder if the BOJ can smoothly change gear when prices begin to rise, most say it will be at least another two years before it considers abandoning its easing stance.   \par \par \uc2 The BOJ has said it will maintain its current policy framework until the year-on-year change in CPI stabilises at or above zero percent.   \par \par \uc2 Adding to views that the BOJ has not finished easing, Deputy Governor Kazumasa Iwata said last week that strong economic growth figures would not affect monetary policy much.   \par \par \uc2 "Just because the figures...came in a bit strong, it doesn't mean the BOJ will change its stance," he said.   \par \par \uc2 Data last Wednesday showed gross domestic product (GDP) rose 1.7 percent in October-December from the previous quarter, and grew a real 7.0 percent on an annualised basis, helped by robust exports and capital spending.   \par \par \uc2 Some say the BOJ may ease further if the yen strengthens and breaches the psychologically key level of 100 yen to the dollar.   \par \par \uc2 The yen was around 108.5 yen on Tuesday after retreating from a 3 = year high near 105 yen hit two weeks ago.   \par \par \uc2 Goldman Sachs senior economist Naoki Murakami said the BOJ may take more radical easing steps in future, such as setting an inflation target and buying riskier assets such as shares.   \par \par \uc2 "(Governor Toshihiko) Fukui has shown he is very flexible in terms of policy. One gets the impression he might be open to anything, eventually," he said.   \par \par \uc2 The BOJ already buys shares from banks under a programme aimed at reducing banks' exposure to stock market volatility. It will buy up to three trillion yen of shares by September 30.   \par \par \uc2 But most say the BOJ has gone deep enough into risky territory and further damage to its balance sheet and credibility would prove disastrous, since the government's tactics on \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign exchange intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  are also under question.   \par \par \b \highlight22\uc2 Japan\b0 \highlight\uc2  sold 20 trillion yen in 2003 and another seven trillion in January to rein in the yen's value, meaning the government has acquired a massive pile of government debt and foreign reserves. ($1=108.25 Yen).    \par \par \uc2 Dokument LBA0000020040224e02o000l3\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw184\pich22\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2760\pichgoal330
