\par\par \b \uc2 Dollar Sinks To Intraday Lows After Weak Confidence Data  \b0\par\par\uc2 By Tom Barkley  \par \uc2 Of DOW JONES NEWSWIRES  \par \uc2 664 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 24 Februar 2004\par 16:02\par \uc2 Dow Jones Capital Markets Report\par \uc2 CM\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 2004 Dow Jones & Company, Inc.  \par \par \uc2 NEW YORK (Dow Jones)--The dollar tumbled to intraday lows Tuesday morning in New York following data showing an erosion in U.S. consumer confidence.  \par \par \uc2 The Conference Board's consumer confidence index slide to 87.3 in February from 96.4 the month before, the steepest one-month decline in a year. The number was worse than forecasts, which had predicted a reading of 92, with employment-related responses deteriorating markedly.  \par \par \uc2 The data provided additional fodder to the increasingly bearish sentiment toward the dollar that has reasserted itself this week following a rebound in the U.S. \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  last week. With many of the short-dollar positions shaken out of the market after Friday's rally, it clears the way for further dollar weakness.  \par \par \uc2 "People were mostly likely overextended, so there was no one to sell dollars," said Lauren Germain, \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign-exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  strategist at Bank of America in New York. "Once people closed out of their positions and were more neutral, they can reopen these positions because their books aren't full."  \par \par \uc2 The dollar triggered stops on the move, falling over a half cent against the euro and pound.  \par \par \uc2 At 10.45 a.m. EST (1545 GMT), the euro was trading at $1.2668, up sharply from $1.2552 late Monday in New York. It was also at Y136.94, versus Y136.01. The dollar was at Y108.15, below Y108.33 late Monday.  \par \par \uc2 Against the Swiss franc, the dollar fell to CHF1.2425 from CHF1.2571, while sterling jumped to $1.8856 from $1.8664.  \par \par \uc2 The dollar continues to extend its losses, with stops triggering more selling pressure. The \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  has lost about a cent against the euro and pound since the consumer confidence data was released.  \par \par \uc2 The dollar's slide will likely encourage investors to reestablish short positions, essentially betting on a further decline. But the move doesn't yet signal that the downtrend is back on course, said analysts.  \par \par \uc2 "Our expectation is that a lot of dollar cross rates are going to be rangy," said Germain of Bank of America, noting that investors have become "more wary of entering into long-term dollar-short positions."  \par \par \uc2 Joseph Barnea, head of \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  at Bank Leumi in New York, said he believes "the downtrend is still intact, but those who are short dollars seem to require a little more proof."  \par \par \uc2 Meanwhile, U.S. Treasury Secretary John Snow reiterated the Bush administration's support for a strong dollar, but the market largely ignored the oft-repeated comments.  \par \par \uc2 Snow, taking a tour of JPMorgan Chase & Co.'s (JPM) trading floor in New York, also said he was encouraged China's steps to make its \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign-exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  policy more flexible.  \par \par \uc2 Some traders also cited suspicious bids against the yen as providing some support to the dollar. While it's unclear whether Japanese monetary officials were placing bids through agent banks, they are widely believed to have shifted tactics in the past week.  \par \par \uc2 Whereas earlier this year the Bank of \b \highlight22\uc2 Japan\b0 \highlight\uc2 , acting on behalf of \b \highlight22\uc2 Japan\b0 \highlight\uc2 's Finance Ministry, was \b \highlight22\uc2 intervening\b0 \highlight\uc2  largely to defend 3 1/2-year lows just above Y105, the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  was suspected of buying dollars as the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  rebounded strongly last week to over Y109.  \par \par \b \highlight22\uc2 Japan\b0 \highlight\uc2 's government has spent record amounts in \b \highlight22\uc2 interventions\b0 \highlight\uc2  in 2003 and so far this year to keep the yen from appreciating too quickly and threatening the country's export-led recovery.  \par \par \uc2 At 11:30 a.m. EST (1630 GMT), the euro had climbed to $1.2684 from $1.2552 late Monday in New York, and to Y137.16 from Y136.01. The dollar was at Y108.13, below Y108.33 late Monday. Against the Swiss franc, the dollar fell to CHF1.2394 from CHF1.2571, while sterling rose to $1.8875 from $1.8664.  \par \par \uc2 -By Tom Barkley, Dow Jones Newswires; 201-938-4385  \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par     tom.barkley@dowjones.com \par    \f28 \par \par \uc2 1074  \par \par \uc2 Dokument CM00000020040224e02o0001h\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw200\pich25\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal3000\pichgoal375
