\par\par \b \uc2 Weak U.S. Consumer-Confidence Data Sinks Dollar  \b0\par\par\uc2 By Jamie McGeever  \par \uc2 Of DOW JONES NEWSWIRES  \par \uc2 1,094 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 24 Februar 2004\par 22:50\par \uc2 Dow Jones Business News\par \uc2 DJON\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 2004 Dow Jones & Company, Inc.  \par \par \uc2 NEW YORK -- The dollar weakened across the board Tuesday, its decline on disappointing U.S. consumer-confidence data, suggesting last week's rally was merely a technical correction paving the way for renewed selling.  \par \par \uc2 The dollar lost ground against all widely traded rivals, particularly the major European and high-yielding \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2  like the euro, sterling and Australian dollar. Suspected yen-selling \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  from \b \highlight22\uc2 Japan\b0 \highlight\uc2 's \b \highlight22\uc2 Ministry of Finance\b0 \highlight\uc2  limited the dollar's losses against the yen, dealers said.  \par \par \uc2 Although the dollar was already under pressure at the opening of U.S. trading, the catalyst for the big tumble came with the Conference Board's consumer-confidence index reading for February. It slid to 87.3 from 96.4 the month before, the steepest one-month decline in a year. The number was worse than forecast, with job-related responses deteriorating markedly.  \par \par \uc2 "The consumer can't keep going forever," said Axel Merk, President of Merk Investments, an investment fund based in Palo Alto, Calif., with around $50 million in assets under management.  \par \par \uc2 Ram Bhagavatula, chief economist for North America at Royal Bank of Scotland in New York, said the dollar's reaction to the weak number was "understandable," but noted actual retail-sales numbers so far this month appear quite strong.  \par \par \uc2 "But the whole game in financial markets is trying guess when the [Federal Reserve] is going to tighten monetary policy," he added. "And when you get a piece of evidence suggesting it might be later rather than sooner, credit markets go up and the dollar goes down."  \par \par \uc2 Dovish comments Tuesday from Fed officials suggest short-term U.S. interest rates will remain low for some time to come, which is bearish for the dollar.  \par \par \uc2 In testimony before the Senate Banking Committee, Fed Chairman Alan Greenspan extolled the virtues of adjustable-rate mortgages, telling senators U.S. consumers would have saved thousands of dollars in the past decade had they been widely available.  \par \par \uc2 In an interview with Dow Jones Newswires, Richmond Fed President Alfred Broaddus said rates can remain low for an "extended period of time" given continuing signs of disinflation even as the U.S. economy is picking up speed.  \par \par \uc2 "With all these policies focussed on the short end of the yield curve, the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  has to weaken," said Mr. Merk.  \par \par \uc2 Overnight interest rates in the U.S. stand at 1%, a 45-year low, among the lowest in the industrialized world. That makes investors look elsewhere for yield.  \par \par \uc2 At 4:30 p.m. EST, the euro was trading at $1.2685, up sharply from $1.2552 late Monday in New York. The dollar was at 108.15 yen, down from 108.33 yen. Sterling jumped to $1.8910 from $1.8664.  \par \par \uc2 The Australian dollar, which had retreated from seven-year highs during last week's surge for the U.S. dollar, also benefitted when the greenback extended its retreat Tuesday. Although some analysts had wondered whether the Australian \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  might have already peaked for 2004, it was trading at US$0.7808 late Tuesday in New York, regaining nearly a cent from US$0.7717 late Monday.  \par \par \uc2 Stephen Cox, chief technician for Dow Jones Newswires, said the dollar's slide sends a bearish technical signal.  \par \par \uc2 "It's not yet clear that Tuesday's steep dollar selloff ended this month's strong corrective rally," he wrote. "But it's certain dollar bulls have been routed for the time being and the February uptrend has at least been interrupted."  \par \par \uc2 But on the face of it, at least, the news flow Tuesday wasn't all positive for the euro.  \par \par \uc2 Germany's Ifo business-sentiment index fell to 96.4 in February from 97.5 in January, breaking an upward trend that had lasted for nine months. Hans-Werner Sinn, president of the Ifo economic institute, said the strength of the euro was a factor behind the deterioration.  \par \par \uc2 German Economics Minister Wolfgang Clement described the decline of the Ifo index "a clear warning shot that no one could ignore." He added the data represented a clear signal to the European \b \highlight22\uc2 Central Bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  to "pay utmost attention to the consequences of its monetary policy."  \par \par \uc2 The ECB, meanwhile, said the euro's strength was also a major factor in the narrowing current-account surplus posted by the euro zone in 2003. The surplus was 28.1 billion euros, compared with 67.1 billion euros in 2002. "This reduction resulted from a lower surplus for goods, mainly reflecting lower exports," the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  said.  \par \par \uc2 The seasonally adjusted surplus in December narrowed to 2.5 billion euros from 5.3 billion euros a year earlier, but bigger than November's 2.1 billion euro surplus.  \par \par \uc2 The financial account saw a combined direct and portfolio investment net outflow of 10.4 billion euros in December, the ECB said.  \par \par \uc2 Steven Saywell, senior \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  strategist at Citibank in London, said in a research note Tuesday that money-market outflows from the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  area in December appear to be a one-time event. He added the narrowing of the current-account surplus should stabilize, signaling "less downside risk for the euro over the medium term, consistent with an expanding U.S. current-account deficit and only moderate portfolio and [foreign-direct investment] inflow into the U.S."  \par \par \uc2 Meanwhile, U.S. Treasury Secretary John Snow shrugged off the weak U.S. consumer-confidence data Tuesday and reaffirmed the Bush administration's commitment to a strong dollar policy.  \par \par \uc2 "All I have is a strong dollar policy," he said to a \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  trader on a tour of the J.P. Morgan trading floor in New York. On the consumer confidence, he said it was only one statistic among many, which broadly reflect the strength of the U.S. economy.  \par \par \uc2 In taking questions from reporters, Mr. Snow struck a more conciliatory tone on the thorny issue of China's \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign-exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  regime, saying he is encouraged by the steps Chinese officials are taking towards floating the yuan. "We think flexibility of the yuan is very much in the interests of the Chinese," he added.  \par \par \uc2 Mr. Snow acknowledged that "interim steps" must be taken before China's financial system can fully cope with a floating \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2 . "But I'm very much encouraged they're on the path to doing just that."  \par \par \uc2 A U.S. Treasury delegation visits Beijing this week to discuss financial issues, including \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2 .  \par \par \uc2 -By Jamie McGeever; Dow Jones Newswires; 201-938-2096; jamie.mcgeever@dowjones.com [ 02-24-04 1650ET ]  \par \par \uc2 Dokument DJON000020040224e02o0002u\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw184\pich22\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2760\pichgoal330
