\par\par \b \uc2 INTERVIEW:BOJ Policy May Be Kept In Check By  \highlight22\uc2 Forex \highlight\uc2  Moves  \b0\par\par\uc2 By Michiyo Seki  \par \uc2 Of DOW JONES NEWSWIRES  \par \uc2 755 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 24 Februar 2004\par 10:19\par \uc2 Dow Jones International News\par \uc2 DJI\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 2004 Dow Jones & Company, Inc.  \par \par \uc2 TOKYO (Dow Jones)--A surge in borrowing by \b \highlight22\uc2 Japan\b0 \highlight\uc2 's Finance Ministry to fund recent heavy yen-selling \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  will constrain Bank of \b \highlight22\uc2 Japan\b0 \highlight\uc2  policy in the future, Totan Research chief economist Izuru Kato says.  \par \par \uc2 As long as the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  maintains its quantitative easing policy, which targets short-term rates near zero, the borrowing will be manageable, he said.  \par \par \uc2 "But I'm worried about what could happen when the time comes for the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  to lift the zero-rate policy," Kato told Dow Jones Newswires in a recent interview.  \par \par \uc2 While market participants fret about the impact on government funding and the economy from a sharp rise in long-term bond rates that could accompany the end of the BOJ's quantitative easing, Kato said there is an equally serious problem in the short-term money market.  \par \par \uc2 The government's issuance of short-term financing bills have ballooned by a third in the past 12 months due to borrowing to fund \b \highlight22\uc2 Japan\b0 \highlight\uc2 's yen-selling \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  campaign.  \par \par \uc2 Kato believes the BOJ may have difficulty in managing short-term interest rates as the time approaches for it to end quantitative easing and tighten policy given the glut of financing bills in the market.  \par \par \uc2 To avoid any sharp rises in financing bill rates, which would place an extra burden on the government's already huge debt-servicing costs, the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  may even put off ending its current policy for longer than it might like, he said.  \par \par \uc2 "The BOJ may not be able to start tightening policy in a timely manner," Kato added.  \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par      Financing Bill Issuance Up Sharply \par    \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 To avoid such a problem, the BOJ may revive its previous practice of directly purchasing financing bills from the government, Kato said.  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Toshiro Mutoh, deputy governor at the BOJ and a former Finance Ministry official, could help negotiate this process, he added.  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 The BOJ stopped buying financing bills directly from the government in 2000, and the Finance Ministry then sold the securities to market participants - a move that was expected to impose market discipline on the government's issuance.  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 But that discipline hasn't been effective.  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 To fund record \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 intervention\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  in the \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 foreign exchange\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  market last year of Y20 trillion and a further Y7 trillion in January, Kato said the Finance Ministry's outstanding amount of financing bills on issue will reach Y60 trillion by the end of this fiscal year on March 31 from Y45 trillion a year earlier.  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Even if the dollar remains above its recent three-and-a-half year low of Y105.16, the Finance Ministry will have to continue to roll over Y60 trillion of financing bills every three months until it either finds a new way to fund the past \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 intervention\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  or it reverses its \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 interventions\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  and starts buying yen, which is unlikely.  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 "The government says \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currency intervention\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  is a smoothing \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 operation\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2 . But in fact, it has sold yen in most cases" of \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 intervention\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2 , Kato said.  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Should the \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currency\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  fall sharply against the dollar, rather than \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 intervene\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2 , "\f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 Japan\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  would simply welcome the dollar's rise, say, to around Y120 or Y130," he added.  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par      BOJ May Postpone Tightening \par    \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 In the near term, the Finance Ministry is having no trouble refinancing its short-term debt, funding its latest issue at an average yield of just 0.0035%.  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Market participants are willing to accept such low yields because they expect the BOJ to keep its quantitative easing for at least another year.  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 But with Japan recording ever-stronger economic data, the ultra-easy policy won't likely be here for ever.  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Japan's gross domestic product grew at an annualized, real rate of 7.0% last quarter and its trade surplus grew fivefold in January from a year earlier to Y507.1 billion.  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Kato said ending quantitative easing would be most troublesome if it came between 2006 and 2008, when the government faces the huge task of refunding a large amount of bonds that mature in those years.  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Due to pressure from maturing JGBs during that period, the ministry may not be able to substitute financing bill issuance by selling more longer-term JGBs, he said.  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 "Although monetary policy should be conducted in line with the condition of the economy, the \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 central bank\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  may postpone tightening if it chooses to coordinate with the government's debt management," Kato said.  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 -By Michiyo Seki, Dow Jones Newswires; 813-5255-2929; michiyo.seki@dowjones.com  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 -Edited by Hugh Lawson  \f28 \par \par \uc2 Dokument DJI0000020040224e02o000e7\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw184\pich22\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2760\pichgoal330
