\par\par \b \uc2 Dow Jones Asia-Pacific Top Stories Sked For Feb 24  \b0\par\par\uc2 2,393 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 24 Februar 2004\par 10:04\par \uc2 Dow Jones International News\par \uc2 DJI\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 2004 Dow Jones & Company, Inc.  \par \par \uc2 Following are the top Asia-Pacific stories featured on Dow Jones Newswires as of 0900 GMT, along with items expected later in the day. Please direct any questions or comments to asianewseds@dowjones.com or by phone on 65-6415-4020.  \par \par \uc2 MACRO/\b \highlight22\uc2 FOREX\b0 \highlight\uc2 /FIXED INCOME  \par \par \uc2 BEIJING: Lehman Brothers Holdings Inc. launches its first made-for-China bond index Tuesday, in a move to tap the growing foreign-investor interest in the country's fixed-income market. In trying to crack the China market, the U.S. investment bank and the world's top-ranked producer of bond indexes has teamed with Xinhua Financial Network, a Hong Kong-based group that also produces financial news and credit ratings. The initiative combines Lehman's 30 years of expertise in bond indexes with XFN's local knowledge and sales force. ("Lehman Teams With XFN To Offer China Bond Index" ran at 0252 GMT)  \par \par \uc2 TOKYO: The head of the International Monetary Fund said Tuesday that Japan can sustain its recent red-hot economic growth, and that Tokyo is making "clear progress" on key financial-sector reforms. ("IMF Koehler Says Strong Japanese Econ Growth Sustainable" first take ran at 0217 GMT)  \par \par \uc2 =\b \highlight22\uc2 FX\b0 \highlight\uc2  ASIA: The Australian dollar's hefty capitulation from seven-year highs over the weekend has raised the prospect that the largest rally in the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2 's post-float history has at last come to an end. ("=\b \highlight22\uc2 FX\b0 \highlight\uc2  ASIA: Australian Dollar May Have Seen 2004 Peak" ran at 0240 GMT)  \par \par \uc2 TAIPEI: Seldom has Taiwan's \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  faced so much pressure to let the local \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  appreciate. The U.S. dollar's tumble against \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2  worldwide, an improving Taiwanese economy, expectations that China may let its yuan rise and a continuous flow of money into the stock market here all point to a stronger New Taiwan dollar. But with a presidential election less than four weeks away, iron-handed \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  Governor Perng Fai-nan may feel an even stronger sense of urgency to ensure the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2 's "dynamic stability" - a phrase market observers read as code for preventing sharp swings in the local unit. ("=TAIWAN VOTE: Ctrl Bank Does Its Part By Taming Local Dlr" ran at 0348 GMT)  \par \par \uc2 SEOUL: South Korea, China and Japan have agreed after two days of working-level talks to cooperate more to keep regional \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  rates stable, said a senior official at the \b \highlight22\uc2 Ministry of Finance\b0 \highlight\uc2  and Economy. ("S Korea Agrees To Greater \b \highlight22\uc2 FX\b0 \highlight\uc2  Alliance With China, Japan" ran at 0237 GMT)  \par \par \uc2 BEIJING: China is unlikely to raise interest rates in the short term, Li Yang, a member of the People's Bank of China Monetary Policy Committee, said Tuesday, scotching speculation the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  is contemplating higher rates to head off inflation and overinvestment. ("China \b \highlight22\uc2 Central Bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  Official Doubts Interest Rate Rise" ran at 0657 GMT)  \par \par \uc2 BEIJING: Ongoing talks between U.S. treasury officials and their Chinese counterparts are on the beginning of a dialogue rather than signaling a sudden shift to a more flexible exchange rate, a \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  official said Tuesday. (story will run around 0915 GMT)  \par \par \uc2 =BOJ WATCH: The Bank of Japan's nine board members will likely vote to leave monetary policy settings steady when they meet Thursday as the yen's recent fall against the dollar reduces the need for looser policy. ("=BOJ Watch: Policy Shift Unlikely But Yen, Stocks Key" ran at 0214 GMT)  \par \par \uc2 TOKYO: While the dollar's decline against the yen may have halted for now, a surge in short-term debt issued by \b \highlight22\uc2 Japan\b0 \highlight\uc2 's Finance Ministry to fund previous heavy yen-selling \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  will place constraints on policy and make it difficult for the Bank of Japan to ends it zero-rate regime once deflation ends, warns Totan Research chief economist Izuru Kato. (INTERVIEW will run around 0930 GMT)  \par \par \uc2 HONG KONG: BOC Hong Kong launches its banking services in the Chinese yuan; other local banks also releasing details of their services in preparation for a formal start Wednesday. (story will run around 1000 GMT)  \par \par \uc2 NEW DELHI: India is right to be concerned over recent protectionist steps taken by the U.S. to curb outsourcing, but it should use the World Trade Organization to secure better global market access in this sector, said the World Bank's chief economist for international trade. ("=INTERVIEW: India Should Use WTO To Boost Outsourcing Mkt" ran at 0719 GMT)  \par \par \uc2 KARACHI: The Pakistani government may sell PKR8 billion of three-year Islamic bonds in the first year of its launch of the financial instrument in 2004, said a \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  official Tuesday. ("=INTERVIEW: Pakistan Plans 1st Islamic Bond Before June" ran at 0753 GMT)  \par \par \uc2 TAIPEI: Taiwan's export orders posted solid growth in January, powered by strong demand for Taiwan-made goods as economies in Asia and Europe expanded, but industrial output fell, the government said Tuesday. ("DATA SNAP: Taiwan Jan Orders Solid; Output Disappoints" ran at 0822 GMT; DATA VIEW will run around 1000 GMT)  \par \par \uc2 WELLINGTON: New Zealand Prime Minister Helen Clark announced Tuesday a cabinet reshuffle to fill posts vacated by disgraced government minister Lianne Dalziel. Clark also created a new cabinet post, Coordinating Minister Race Relations, to which she appointed eighth-ranked minister Trevor Mallard. The new role is likely a response to opposition criticism of the government's handling of race relations policy. ("NZ Cabinet Reshuffled; Wilson Loses Labor, Gets Commerce" ran at 2353 GMT)  \par \par \uc2 CANBERRA: The latest Newspoll voter survey putting the Australian government and major opposition neck and neck on a two-party preferred basis is evidence the next election will be a tight race, Prime Minister John Howard said Tuesday. ("=UPDATE: Australian PM: Newspoll Points To Tight Election" ran at 0043 GMT)  \par \par \uc2 =\b \highlight22\uc2 FOREX\b0 \highlight\uc2  MARKET ROUNDUP: The dollar edged higher against other major \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2  in Asia Tuesday, but trading was sluggish as investors held out for fresh incentives. ("Asia \b \highlight22\uc2 Forex\b0 \highlight\uc2 : Dollar Edges Higher But New Incentives Sought" ran at 0542 GMT)  \par \par \uc2 EQUITIES  \par \par \uc2 TOKYO: U.S. investment fund Steel Partners Japan Strategic Fund Tuesday ended its attempt to takeover Japanese textile firm Sotoh Co., ending an aggressive two-month bidding war that marked Japan's first real hostile takeover battle. Steel Partners' bid for Sotoh and the textile company's ultimately investor-friendly defense signaled that the rules of the game are starting to change in Japan's financial markets. ("=UPDATE: US Fund Ends Takeover Bid For Japan's Sotoh" ran at 0555 GMT)  \par \par \uc2 TOKYO: Two of Japan's top five drug companies, Yamanouchi Pharmaceutical and Fujisawa Pharmaceutical, said they will merge on April 1, 2005, marking the first consolidation in Japan's crowded and highly competitive pharmaceutical industry. (UPDATE will run arond 1030 GMT)  \par \par \uc2 HONG KONG: Steelmaker China Oriental Group will raise HK$1.925 billion from its initial public offering after its shares were priced at HK$2.75 apiece, at the top end of an earlier indicative range on strong market interest, people close to the deal said Tuesday. ("=UPDATE: China Oriental IPO Draws Strong Retail Interest" ran at 0402 GMT)  \par \par \uc2 =ASIAN STOCK FOCUS: China Minsheng Banking Corp.'s admission last week that it faked a shareholders meeting four years ago may not only cast a shadow on its reputation but could also delay and lower the price of its planned H-share offer in Hong Kong, analysts say. ("=ASIAN STOCK FOCUS: China Minsheng Fakery Clouds Outlook" ran at 0507 GMT)  \par \par \uc2 TOKYO: All of Japan's major auto makers except Mitsubishi Motors posted domestic sales growth in January, reflecting the strength of new car lineups, and underscoring troubles at the struggling DaimlerChrysler unit. (UPDATE will run around 0930 GMT)  \par \par \uc2 HONG KONG: The government Tuesday said it has asked Hong Kong's two rail operators, MTR Corp. and Kowloon-Canton Railway Corp., to sit down for formal merger talks. ("Hong Kong Govt Invites Talks On Merger Of KCRC, MTRC" ran at 0556 GMT; UPDATE will run around 1030 GMT)  \par \par \uc2 =ASIA FUND VIEW: Demand for Asia-Pacific equity funds will likely continue to outpace demand for other regional or single-country portfolios in Hong Kong this year, said Hong Kong Investment Funds Association executive director Sally Wong, citing the region's strong economic outlook that will help boost corporate earnings. ("Asia Fund View:HK Investors Still Favor Asia Equity Funds" ran at 0333 GMT)  \par \par \uc2 TOKYO: The Tokyo stock rally since early last year may end in May or June as the global economy hits a peak, but steady Japanese industrial output will temper share price falls after that, a Japanese investment asset manager says. ("=INTERVIEW: Sompo Japan: Topix To Peak At 1100-1150 Soon" ran at 0859 GMT)  \par \par \uc2 MELBOURNE: AXA Asia Pacific Holdings reported Tuesday a near trebling of 2003 net profit, with earnings at the Australia-based life insurer and funds manager buoyed by asset sales and a rebound in global equity markets. ("=UPDATE: AXA Asia Pacific Earnings Triple On Mkt Rebound" ran at 0532 GMT)  \par \par \uc2 SYDNEY: International airport operator Macquarie Airports Tuesday posted a sharp improvement in net profit for 2003 and unveiled a takeover bid for privately held airport fund Macquarie Airports Group. ("=UPDATE: Australia's Macquarie Airports Posts Strong 03" ran at 0435 GMT)  \par \par \uc2 SINGAPORE: Neptune Orient Lines, the world's sixth- largest shipping company, is expected to report a turnaround in earnings with likely net profit of US$393 million for 2003, from a US$330 million loss the previous year. (results expected around 0900 GMT; UPDATE will run by around 1100 GMT)  \par \par \uc2 KUALA LUMPUR: Malaysian conglomerate Sime Darby posted a 6.3% on-year rise in fiscal first-half net profit to 446.8 million ringgit, citing higher palm oil prices and strong demand for its property projects for the better-than-expected results. ("Malaysia Sime Darby 1H Net MYR446.8M Vs MYR421M -2-" ran at 0529 GMT)  \par \par \uc2 KUALA LUMPUR: It's been six years since a plan to insure bank deposits was mooted in Malaysia. But a top adviser to the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  on deposit insurance says it is more critical to customize the plan than to implement it in a rush. ("=INTERVIEW:Malaysia Needs Time To Customize Deposit Insur" ran at 0551 GMT)  \par \par \uc2 HONG KONG: Shui On Construction & Materials said it will swap a Shanghai-based property project acquired from its chairman Vincent Lo in 2001 for a minority stake in a newly established real estate firm controlled by Lo. ("HK Shui On Swaps Ppty Proj For Stake In Real Estate Co" ran at 0300 GMT; UPDATE will run around 1030 GMT)  \par \par \uc2 HONG KONG: The container liner arm of China's major shipping conglomerate, China Shipping (Group) Co., is seeking to raise around US$2 billion in an IPO, people close to the deal said Tuesday. (story will run around 0915 GMT)  \par \par \uc2 MELBOURNE: Citing solid contributions from acquisitions and a focus on contracts that generate better margins, Australian transport company Toll Holdings posted a better-than-expected 51% rise in first-half net profit and said it is on track for record earnings in the full year . ("=UPDATE: Australia Toll Pft Jumps; On Track For Record FY" ran at 0503 GMT)  \par \par \uc2 SYDNEY: Embattled Australian gambling machine maker Aristocrat Leisure Tuesday reported a A$106 million net loss for 2003, the worst year in its history, reversing from a A$80.2 million profit in 2002. ("=UPDATE: Australia's Aristocrat Leisure Wipes Slate Clean" ran at 0559 GMT)  \par \par \uc2 BEIJING: Large-format film/theater firm IMAX Corp. expects a combination of rampant movie piracy, Internet film distribution and an ongoing domestic theater building boom to make China the company's second-largest global market by the end of the decade, an executive said Tuesday. (INTERVIEW will run around 1030 GMT)  \par \par \uc2 WELLINGTON: NGC Holdings, a New Zealand gas distribution and metering company, reported a 44% surge in first-half net profit, and said it is aims to pay a dividend of between 80% and 100% of net earnings in the second half compared with 74% in the first half. ("=UPDATE: Restructuring Boosts NZ's NGC 1H Profit" ran at 0056 GMT)  \par \par \uc2 WELLINGTON: Wrightson Ltd., a rural supplies and services company, blamed the strong New Zealand dollar and difficult climate for a 34% decline in first-half profit, and warned its second half would also be challenging. ("=UPDATE: Wrightson Profit Dives As NZD Surge Hits Farmers" ran at 2245 GMT)  \par \par \uc2 =ASIAN IPO FOCUS: Bird flu has taken down the country's poultry industry, but it shouldn't ground state-owned Airports of Thailand PCL, whose initial public offering still represents an attractive long-term investment. ("=Asian IPO Focus: Airports Of Thailand Immune To Bird Flu" ran at 0831 GMT)  \par \par \uc2 ENERGY/COMMODITIES  \par \par \uc2 PERTH: Santos Ltd. said Tuesday it will look to exploration rather than acquisitions as the Australian energy company tries to rebound from a production slump this year caused by the January fire at its Moomba gas facility. The company said earlier today its 2003 net profit rose 1.5% on year to A$327 million. ("=REPEAT:INTERVIEW: Santos Explores For Production Rebound" will run at 0724 GMT)  \par \par \uc2 SYDNEY: Papua New Guinea-based miner Lihir Gold says it has boosted its gold reserves and resource but simultaneously cut its planned production through 2007. Earlier today, Lihir said 2003 net profit fell 35% to US$34.8 million from US$53.2 million in 2002. ("=UPDATE: PNG's Lihir Boosts Reserves But Cuts 5-Yr Output" ran at 0652 GMT)  \par \par \uc2 NEW DELHI: Indian Oil Corp., India's largest refiner and oil retailing firm, is looking to export up to 3 million metric tons of petroleum products a year to Pakistan, a company official said Tuesday. ("India IOC Wants To Export 3M Tons/Yr Oil Pdts To Pakistan" ran at 0803 GMT)  \par \par \uc2 SINGAPORE: South Korean oil products trading company Ko&Pec plans to build and operate a major terminal and storage tanks in China's Shandong province, in a $509 million joint venture with a local Chinese company. ("=INTERVIEW: S Korea Ko&Pec Plans $509M Oil Port In E China" ran at 0324 GMT)  \par \par \uc2 PERTH: As it embarks on a A$37.5 million initial public share offering, Australian gas producer CH4 Ltd is confident its coal seam production can compete with other suppliers in the growing Australian market. ("=INTERVIEW: Australia's CH4 To Challenge Gas Suppliers" ran at 2351 GMT)  \par \par \uc2 