\par\par \b \uc2 ANALYSIS-Too early to declare a turnaround by the dollar.  \b0\par\par\uc2 By Mariko Hayashibara  \par \uc2 744 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 23 Februar 2004\par 07:47\par \uc2 Reuters News\par \uc2 LBA\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 2004 Reuters Limited  \par \par \uc2 TOKYO, Feb 23 (Reuters) - The dollar's spike up in the past few days has caught many off guard, but investors say it is not due to any shift in fundamentals and it is too early to declare an end to the greenback's two-year downtrend.  \par \par \uc2 Just last week the dollar appeared to be reeling because of worries about a record U.S. budget deficit, prospect for U.S. interest rates to stay stuck at their lowest level since 1958 and an unconvincing statement by G7 finance ministers.  \par \par \uc2 Then it suddenly began to recover, rising four percent on the euro from a life low of $1.2930 hit just last Wednesday. It also rose sharply against higher-yielding \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2  such as sterling and the Australian and New Zealand dollars.  \par \par \uc2 Against the yen, it soared four percent to 109.40 yen on Monday from a 105.14 low hit after the G7.  \par \par \uc2 Analysts see room for the dollar to rise further, but they are not yet convinced it has really turned a corner.  \par \par \uc2 "The dollar's recovery is not related to any fundamental change. It has a lot to do with Japanese yen-selling \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  and verbal \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  by European and Australian officials warning against rapid appreciations in their \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2 ," said Mitsuru Saito, general manager of UFJ Tsubasa Securities.  \par \par \uc2 "What's happening now is a broad-based correction after very rapid falls in the dollar, so when this adjustment mood cools down, the dollar is likely to face another round of downward pressure," he said.  \par \par \uc2 Against the yen, some expected the dollar to rise as high as 111.50 yen as many short-term players were likely to adjust positions further.  \par \par \uc2 Richard Franulovich, a senior \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  strategist at Westpac Banking, said about 90 percent of outstanding speculative short dollar/yen positions were underwater at current levels.  \par \par \uc2 The dollar's further rise versus the yen should make Japanese exporters happy as their value of goods and investments rises, but it will frustrate U.S. manufacturers.  \par \par \uc2 Analysts said any break in the dollar's downtrend would still require a clearer change in the U.S. interest rate outlook or capital flows trend.  \par \par \uc2 Some were encouraged to buy the dollar on the back of steady capital inflows to U.S. securities in November and December, an improvement from a meagre $4.3 billion of inflows in September.  \par \par \uc2 "But when you look at who is buying them, it's mostly Japan and other Asian central banks using the proceeds from \b \highlight22\uc2 currency intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2 ," said Tomoko Fujii, market and economic analysis director at Nikko Citigroup.  \par \par \uc2 "What's important is whether private-sector investors feel confident about investing in U.S. assets, but their appetite is in contrast fading," she said.  \par \par \b \highlight22\uc2 INTERVENTION\b0 \highlight\uc2  EFFECT  \par \par \uc2 Against the yen, analysts said the dollar's recovery was helped by a huge amount of yen-selling \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  by \b \highlight22\uc2 Japan\b0 \highlight\uc2 , which prevented the dollar's freefall from passing 105 yen.  \par \par \uc2 "Basically what happened was that speculators gave up on selling the dollar beyond 105 yen as Japanese authorities seemed to be determined to protect that level," said Seiya Nakajima, chief economist at Itochu Corp.  \par \par \b \highlight22\uc2 Japan\b0 \highlight\uc2  sold seven trillion yen ($65.42 billion) in \b \highlight22\uc2 interventions\b0 \highlight\uc2  in January and is widely thought to have sold trillions more yen this month to curb the yen's rise, which officials worry could undermine a recovery in the Japanese economy by hitting export industries.  \par \par \uc2 January's \b \highlight22\uc2 interventions\b0 \highlight\uc2  compare with a net total of 2.1 trillion yen of fund inflows to Japanese securities that month and a trade surplus of about 600 billion yen, producing a "gap" of some 4.5 trillion yen. That gap created huge dollar demand, analysts say.  \par \par \uc2 But they wonder how long Japan can keep overspending in this way, since rising evidence of economic recovery makes it hard for \b \highlight22\uc2 Japan\b0 \highlight\uc2  to justify its massive \b \highlight22\uc2 interventions\b0 \highlight\uc2 .  \par \par \uc2 Last week, \b \highlight22\uc2 Japan\b0 \highlight\uc2  said the economy grew a real 7.0 percent on an annualised basis in October-December, well above a 4.0 percent expansion in the United States in the same period.  \par \par \uc2 "It will look odd if \b \highlight22\uc2 Japan\b0 \highlight\uc2  still \b \highlight22\uc2 intervenes\b0 \highlight\uc2  at the 109 yen level," said UFJ Tsubasa's Saito.  \par \par \uc2 "The Japanese public has grown aware of how costly it is to \b \highlight22\uc2 intervene\b0 \highlight\uc2  in the \b \highlight22\uc2 forex\b0 \highlight\uc2  market, making the \b \highlight22\uc2 Ministry of Finance\b0 \highlight\uc2  nervous of criticism. I wonder if they will keep \b \highlight22\uc2 intervening\b0 \highlight\uc2  so aggressively." ($1=107.00 Yen).   \par \par \uc2 Dokument LBA0000020040223e02n001e7\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw184\pich22\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2760\pichgoal330
