\par\par \b  \highlight22\uc2 FOREX \highlight\uc2  VIEW: Positioning Suggests Dollar Rebound Flagging  \b0\par\par\uc2 926 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 23 Februar 2004\par 20:10\par \uc2 Dow Jones Capital Markets Report\par \uc2 CM\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 2004 Dow Jones & Company, Inc.  \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par    By Jamie McGeever and Tom Barkley \par    A Dow Jones Newswires Column \par    \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 NEW YORK (Dow Jones)--It will become clearer in the next few days whether the dollar's surge last week marks a turning point in its broader downtrend or a correction that paves the way for further losses.  \f28 \par \par \uc2 The general consensus, though, based on positioning indicators in a market where the environment is still bearish for the dollar, is that, while further modest advances can't be ruled out, the shakeout last week leaves the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  poised to resume its move lower against most of its major rivals.  \par \par \uc2 Unnerved by fears European monetary authorities might \b \highlight22\uc2 intervene\b0 \highlight\uc2  and sell euros as the single \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  approached $1.30 last week, short-term investors started liquidating long euro positions Wednesday. At the same time, frustrated by their inability to push the dollar below Y105.00 and break the stout resistance offered by persistent yen-selling \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  from \b \highlight22\uc2 Japan\b0 \highlight\uc2 's \b \highlight22\uc2 Ministry of Finance\b0 \highlight\uc2 , they also started unwinding short dollar positions against the yen.  \par \par \uc2 That move, started Wednesday, accelerated dramatically Friday after Japan raised its national security alert to its highest level. The scramble to cover short dollar positions - effectively bets the dollar will weaken - sent the U.S. \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  soaring higher across the board in extremely choppy trade.  \par \par \uc2 But the lack of follow-through buying Monday suggests many of those short-term players are once again betting against the dollar.  \par \par \b \highlight22\uc2 Currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  analysts at ABN Amro noted Monday evidence of a "broader return" to short dollar positions against the euro after Friday's "major clear-out." Friday, the bank's speculative positioning indicator (SPI) showed euro long positions - bets that the euro will strengthen - at around just a quarter of the one-year maximum.  \par \par \uc2 Short-term speculators are highly leveraged and so are less able to withstand sharp moves in the market. They limit losses by quickly exiting those positions, creating a snowball effect. This is what happened Friday - Japan's move to raise its terror alert was the catalyst they needed to start buying back dollars.  \par \par \uc2 "Short-term players don't have the staying power," said Bob Kozak, \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  futures analyst at Alaron \b \highlight22\uc2 FX\b0 \highlight\uc2 , a futures and options brokeragein Chicago. They will be quick to start selling dollars if they feel that trend is picking up again, Kozak said.  \par \par \uc2 Michael McGuinness, senior director of \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  at American Express in New York, also sees evidence of short dollar positions being reestablished Monday, particularly against those \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2  that got battered Friday, including the euro, sterling, and the Australian and New Zealand dollars.  \par \par \uc2 He expects this to continue "gradually" as the week progresses. "None of the fundamentals has really changed, so we're just easing off the accelerator," said McGuinness.  \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par    Risk-Reversals Neutral \par    \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Those longer-term economic drags on the dollar include benign inflationary pressures and low U.S. interest rates, the so-called "twin deficits" of massive U.S. budget and current account shortfalls and a general feeling U.S. policy-makers want a weak dollar to help boost exports. In a presidential election year, few observers believe the current administration wants to see the dollar strengthen and do even more damage to employment in an already ravaged manufacturing sector.  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Citing those longer-term factors, John Fryback, principal at Beacon Management Corp., a hedge fund in Princeton, N.J., said that his intermediate- to long-term \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currency\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  model fund is still positioned for a dollar downtrend.  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 "That was a dramatic change last week, but the long-term trend remains intact, so we're still short the dollar," said Fryback, whose investment firm has $80 million under management.  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Until these fundamentals start to change, dollar recoveries will likely be short-lived, agree \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currency\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  strategists at Citibank. Monday, they established a long sterling/short dollar position at $1.8630, targeting a rally up to $1.9300.  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Around 2::0 pm EST, 1900 GMT, Monday, the pound was trading at $1.8606, up from $1.8590 late Friday. The euro was back up at $1.2533 from $1.2530 late Friday, while the dollar was lower at Y108.47 from Y109.10.  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 The dollar hit a record low against the euro and multiyear lows against sterling and the Australian and New Zealand dollars last week before reversing course Wednesday. This rally helped bring one-month risk reversals, an indication of how the \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currency\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  options market is positioned, back into neutral territory after being skewed so heavily against the dollar in recent months.  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 One-month options on euro/dollar and dollar/yen, for example, suggest dealers' bias toward dollar call and put options are roughly balanced on a one-month horizon. A "call" gives the option holder the right but not the obligation to buy the underlying asset at a certain price at some point in the future, while a "put" gives the holder the option of selling. Investors buy call options in expectation of further price increases and put options if they think the price of the asset will decline.  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Citing previous instances of one-month risk reversals falling back into neutral territory, Deutsche Bank \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currency\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  analysts reckon the dollar's rally will run out of steam.  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 "The other instances in 2004 when one-month euro/dollar risk reversals have moved to 0 have signaled a bottom in the euro/dollar (downward) correction," they wrote in a research note Monday.  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par    -Jamie McGeever; Dow Jones Newswires; 201 938 2096; jamie.mcgeever@dowjones.com \par    -Tom Barkley; Dow Jones Newswires; 201-9358-4385; tom.barkley@dowjones.com \par    \f28 \par \par \uc2 Dokument CM00000020040223e02n0003j\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw200\pich25\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal3000\pichgoal375
