\par\par \b \uc2 Dollar Pulls Back Modestly as Market Regroups  \b0\par\par\uc2 By Tom Barkley and Jamie McGeever  \par \uc2 Of DOW JONES NEWSWIRES  \par \uc2 1,026 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 23 Februar 2004\par 23:08\par \uc2 Dow Jones Business News\par \uc2 DJON\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 2004 Dow Jones & Company, Inc.  \par \par \uc2 NEW YORK -- The dollar traded modestly lower against most of its major counterparts Monday, but activity was subdued as investors took a break from the volatility of recent sessions.  \par \par \uc2 After a bout of profit-taking sent the dollar soaring to register its best performance in 18 months against the yen Friday, market participants were waiting for any news that would provide direction for the market.  \par \par \uc2 "It's a period of consolidation," said Ed Stapleton, head of \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  at Fortis Bank New York. "We saw some big moves last week, and there's no news out (Monday), so people are just sitting back a bit."  \par \par \uc2 News that the U.S. dispatched a six-member delegation to China Monday to meet with \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  and government officials came too late in the day to have an effect during New York trading. The talks will center on technical issues related to laying the groundwork for China's shift to a floating \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2 , the Treasury Department said.  \par \par \uc2 It is the first in series of meetings the group is expected to hold. The U.S. delegation is headed by David Loevinger, deputy assistant Treasury secretary for Africa, Middle East and Asia.  \par \par \uc2 Late Monday afternoon, the euro was higher at $1.2552 versus $1.2530 late Friday in New York, but was lower against the yen at 136.01 yen from 136.60 yen. Against the Swiss franc, the dollar fell to 1.2571 francs from 1.2605 francs, while sterling climbed to $1.8664 from $1.8590. The dollar was also trading around 108.33 yen, down from 109.10 yen late Friday in New York.  \par \par \uc2 It should become clearer in the next few days whether the dollar's surge last week marks a turning point in its broader downtrend or a correction that paves the way for further losses. However, the lack of follow-through buying Monday suggests many short-term players are once again gearing up to bet against the dollar.  \par \par \b \highlight22\uc2 Currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  analysts at ABN Amro noted Monday evidence of a "broader return" to long euro positions against the dollar - essentially betting that the euro will strengthen. At the end of Friday's trading, the bank's speculative positioning indicator showed long euro positions around just a quarter of the one-year maximum.  \par \par \uc2 While further modest advances in the dollar can't be ruled out, the shakeout last week also leaves the U.S. \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  vulnerable to resume its move lower against most of its major rivals, said analysts. ABN Amro closed its recommendation to short the euro on Monday, while Citigroup recommended reestablishing a long sterling position against the dollar.  \par \par \uc2 Michael McGuinness, senior director of \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  at American Express in New York, also sees evidence of short dollar positions being reestablished, particularly against those \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2  that got battered Friday, including the euro, sterling, and the Australian and New Zealand dollars.  \par \par \uc2 He expects this trend to continue "gradually" as the week progresses. "None of the fundamentals has really changed, so we're just easing off the accelerator," added McGuinness.  \par \par \uc2 John Fryback, principal at Beacon Management Corp., a hedge fund in Princeton, N.J., said that his intermediate- to long-term \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  model fund is still positioned for a dollar downtrend.  \par \par \uc2 "That was a dramatic change last week, but the long-term trend remains intact, so we're still short the dollar," said Fryback, whose investment firm has $80 million under management.  \par \par \uc2 The dollar is expected to remain under pressure over the longer-term because low interest rates make it difficult for the U.S. to attract enough foreign investment to fund the massive current account deficit.  \par \par \uc2 "The only thing that will support the dollar going forward is the market's perception that interest rates are going to go higher," said Stapleton of Fortis.  \par \par \uc2 Following an unexpected 0.5% rise in the January consumer price index on Friday, the market will be watching closely to see if Federal Reserve Chairman Alan Greenspan strays from his previous statements that monetary policy makers can afford to "patient" about raising rates due to low inflation. He testifies on the economy before committees in the Senate and House of Representatives this week.  \par \par \uc2 However, Federal Reserve Bank of Minneapolis President Gary Stern said in an interview with Dow Jones Newswires that inflation is "showing no signs of any real appreciable acceleration," while the dollar "seems to have done little" to stoke upward pressure on prices.  \par \par \uc2 Stern said it is too soon to say when it will be time to raise interest rates, but added that a modest tightening in interest rates wouldn't be a problem for growth.  \par \par \uc2 The other major source of support for the dollar has come from Asian central banks, and Japanese monetary authorities were suspected once again by traders of covertly buying dollars.  \par \par \uc2 The Bank of Japan, acting on behalf of \b \highlight22\uc2 Japan\b0 \highlight\uc2 's \b \highlight22\uc2 Ministry of Finance\b0 \highlight\uc2 , has \b \highlight22\uc2 intervened\b0 \highlight\uc2  at record levels in 2003 and so far this year to keep the yen from appreciating too rapidly and threatening the country's export-led recovery.  \par \par \uc2 The BOJ recently switched tactics from trying to slow the dollar's decline to actively pushing it higher, with the U.S. \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  hitting its strongest point in nearly three months at 109.35 yen early Monday in Tokyo trade.  \par \par \uc2 As an indication that \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  may be working, \b \highlight22\uc2 Japan\b0 \highlight\uc2 's merchandise trade surplus jumped nearly fivefold in January from a year earlier to 507.1 billion yen, fueled in large part by an 11% rise in exports.  \par \par \uc2 Masakazu Hayashi, Japan's administrative vice finance minister, said Friday in Tokyo that "some of the fairly large speculative positions that had grown have started to reverse on their own."  \par \par \uc2 But he added that the Finance Ministry would continue to take "smoothing" actions when \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  moves are disorderly and out of line with economic fundamentals.  \par \par \uc2 -By Tom Barkley, Dow Jones Newswires; 201-938-4385  \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par     tom.barkley@dowjones.com   \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 -By Jamie McGeever, Dow Jones Newswires; 201-938-2096; jamie.mcgeever@dowjones.com  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 (Deborah Lagomarsino in Washington contributed to this article.)  \f28 \par \par \uc2 Dokument DJON000020040223e02n00035\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw184\pich22\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2760\pichgoal330
