\par\par \b \uc2 Tanigaki: Dlr Rise A Correction Of Speculative Moves  \b0\par\par\uc2 302 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 24 Februar 2004\par 00:52\par \uc2 Dow Jones International News\par \uc2 DJI\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 2004 Dow Jones & Company, Inc.  \par \par \uc2 TOKYO (Dow Jones)--Japan's finance minister Tuesday welcomed the recent rebound in the dollar, saying it was a correction of speculative movements.  \par \par \uc2 "\b \highlight22\uc2 Foreign exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  market movements in the wake of the Dubai Group of Seven meeting had been speculative," Sadakazu Tanigaki told a news conference.  \par \par \uc2 "It appears that such movements are being reassessed," he said.  \par \par \uc2 Tanigaki was referring to the dollar's recent rally against its major counterparts, including the yen. After the greenback fell to a low of Y105.16 earlier this month, it recovered to the Y109 level this week as short dollar positions were unwound.  \par \par \uc2 Tanigaki's comments are likely a tacit warning that the ministry will continue to take action in the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  market if speculators hamper what it believes to be a healthy correction of the yen's unwarranted strength in the wake of September's G7 meeting in Dubai.  \par \par \uc2 The dollar weakened broadly after the G7 finance ministers and \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  governors in Dubai called for "more flexibility" in exchange rates - a statement that was interpreted as a sign that the U.S. wanted \b \highlight22\uc2 Japan\b0 \highlight\uc2  to stop \b \highlight22\uc2 intervening\b0 \highlight\uc2  to support the dollar.  \par \par \uc2 The G7 expanded on the Dubai statement at a meeting in the U.S. earlier this month, adding specifically that it wants more flexibility from "major countries and economic areas that lack such flexibility."  \par \par \uc2 Japanese officials, claiming the new phrase indicates Tokyo's \b \highlight22\uc2 interventions\b0 \highlight\uc2  are not a problem for the G7, has taken both verbal and physical action in \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  markets to make clear it won't tolerate a further strengthening of the yen.  \par \par \uc2 At 0054 GMT, the dollar was trading at Y108.48.  \par \par \uc2 -By Miyako Takebe, Dow Jones Newswires; 813-5255-2929; miyako.takebe@dowjones.com  \par \par \uc2 -Edited by Alastair Gale and John Gibson  \par \par \uc2 Dokument DJI0000020040223e02n000xy\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw172\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2580\pichgoal600
