\par\par \b \uc2 Japan's fiscal year end may present dollar pitfall.  \b0\par\par\uc2 By Kyle Peterson  \par \uc2 805 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 20 Februar 2004\par 18:31\par \uc2 Reuters News\par \uc2 LBA\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 2004 Reuters Limited  \par \par \uc2 CHICAGO, Feb 20 (Reuters) - The dollar may be off the ropes against the yen for now but many \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  traders think it could once again tread perilously close to 105 yen, a level some view as a trap door set to swing wide open on April 1.  \par \par \uc2 That's the first day of Japan's new fiscal year, and it's then that many traders expect Japanese monetary authorities to tone down an unprecedented campaign to curb the strength of the yen against the dollar.  \par \par \uc2 Many U.S. traders believe the main motive of the Bank of Japan in selling massive amounts of yen for dollars has been the hope of preventing a dollar decline below 105 yen before the end of this fiscal year.  \par \par \uc2 "They have needed record levels of \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  to keep the dollar from going below 105," said Michael Woolfolk, senior \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  strategist at Bank of New York. "All bets are off once we pass March 31."  \par \par \uc2 The dollar on Friday jumped to an 11-week high above 109 yen in what traders described as a rapid unraveling of short-dollar positions. A short position essentially is a bet that a \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  will fall in value. Analysts, however, maintain the view that the dollar downtrend is far from over.  \par \par \uc2 Some say the 105 level is key to Japan as the fiscal year winds down because Japanese companies who make money overseas are soon to report their year-end earnings. Many of those companies have based their earnings forecasts on an exchange rate of 105 yen to the dollar.  \par \par \uc2 A sharply weaker dollar could skew their earnings to the downside. Weak earnings, in turn, could threaten the Japanese stock market, which last year began to emerge from a multi-year slump.  \par \par \uc2 Since starting its spectacular downtrend in February 2002, the dollar has fallen about 20 percent against the yen. The yen's steep gains against the dollar, however, are troubling to Japanese leaders who say the strength of their \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  threatens economic growth in Japan by making exports too dear.  \par \par \uc2 In January alone, the Bank of \b \highlight22\uc2 Japan\b0 \highlight\uc2  bought $67 billion in yen-weakening \b \highlight22\uc2 interventions\b0 \highlight\uc2 , a record for any \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2 . In 2003, the BOJ sold 20 trillion yen ($189 billion).  \par \par \b \highlight22\uc2 Forex intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2 , by nature, tends to be covert activity in which a \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  buys or sells large amounts of \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  to take traders by surprise and spark rapid price moves. The BOJ generally does not specify the exchange rate at which it will \b \highlight22\uc2 intervene\b0 \highlight\uc2  or its target exchange rate. Often the bank declines to admit to \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  until well after the fact.  \par \par \uc2 On Feb. 11, the dollar touched a three-year low of 105.14 yen. The U.S. \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  hovered just above those levels until Wednesday when it vaulted suddenly above 107 yen.  \par \par \uc2 Analysts linked the move to profit-taking in the market on key \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2  against the dollar. But many traders also said they suspected the BOJ had been in the market selling yen.  \par \par \uc2 "Their reserves are big enough that they could put (the dollar) at 120 (yen) tomorrow if they wanted to," said Enrico Caruso, vice president and chief dealer at Tempest Asset Management in Irvine, California. "They just want to put that big collapse off until after the (fiscal) year end."  \par \par \uc2 Furthermore, Japanese monetary authorities know that if the dollar manages to slip below 105, there is a mine field of sell orders and options strikes that threaten to accelerate the dollar's decline much lower.  \par \par \uc2 "It's just a popular level. That's just a natural play," Caruso said.  \par \par \uc2 BATTLING REPATRIATION TIDE  \par \par \uc2 Japan's efforts to keep the dollar bolstered against the yen for another six weeks runs counter to the strong dollar downtrend as well as seasonal yen repatriation flows generated by Japanese companies who must buy yen ahead of the fiscal year end in order to square their books.  \par \par \uc2 Once the fiscal year ends, those repatriation flows may reverse as overseas investment resumes. But analysts warn that many funds will seek investment opportunities in Japan's budding growth sectors, a trend that would support the yen and send the dollar lower still.  \par \par \uc2 On Wednesday, markets learned that Japan's economy expanded by a bigger-than-expected 1.7 percent in October-December from the previous quarter, helped by robust exports and capital spending. That figure, which marked a fourth straight quarter of growth, was much stronger than expected.  \par \par \uc2 "We believe the main rationale for a stronger yen is that the domestic economy in Japan is shaping up to be a lot stronger than people had imagined a year ago," said Paresh Upadhyaya, \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  analyst at Putnam Investments. "There's probably a good likelihood that the yen will strengthen in the coming months."   \par \par \uc2 Dokument LBA0000020040220e02k002co\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw172\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2580\pichgoal600
