\par\par \b  \highlight22\uc2 FOREX \highlight\uc2  VIEW: Japan Alert Fans Flames Of Dollar's Comeback  \b0\par\par\uc2 952 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 20 Februar 2004\par 20:24\par \uc2 Dow Jones Capital Markets Report\par \uc2 CM\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 2004 Dow Jones & Company, Inc.  \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par    By Jamie McGeever and Grainne McCarthy \par    A DOW JONES NEWSWIRES COLUMN \par    \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 NEW YORK (Dow Jones)--By any standards, the dollar has had a phenomenal few days, particularly considering the pressure it's been under for so long.  \f28 \par \par \uc2 Although the euro briefly touched a fresh lifetime high around $1.2925 in early Asian trade Wednesday, the dollar has come soaring back and on Friday is trading at three-month highs against the yen, while the euro has lost about four cents from that record peak.  \par \par \uc2 The dollar's gains are spreading broader too; government bond prices and equities are under pressure, so are prices of commodities, gold and crude oil. High-yielding \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2  such as the Australian dollar, which have been major beneficiaries of the dollar's decline over the past couple of years, are sliding. Sterling, long buoyed by higher U.K. official interest rates relative to the U.S., has tumbled nearly 3% from an 11-year high of $1.9137 on Wednesday.  \par \par \uc2 The dollar's gains Friday have been extended by the misfortunes of the yen, which was sent reeling by Japan raising its terror alert to its highest level and mobilizing heavily armed police around airports, nuclear plants and government offices to guard against a possible attack, sending a chill through global financial markets.  \par \par \uc2 Rather than concluding that dollar sentiment has suddenly turned brighter however, \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  strategists reckon the dramatic gains in the greenback owe much to simple market positioning. The surge has been led by a mix of short-term speculators and more medium-term funds, flushing out many investors who had effectively placed bets on the dollar falling further.  \par \par \uc2 As Marc Chandler, chief \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  strategist at HSBC in New York noted, this may be "less neat than a fundamental explanation, or even a clear chart pattern," but that doesn't make it any less true.  \par \par \uc2 For months, this segment of the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  market has been pushing the dollar down, forcing it to a string of record lows against the euro in the process. At the same time, they've been repeatedly testing the resolve of \b \highlight22\uc2 Japan\b0 \highlight\uc2 's \b \highlight22\uc2 Ministry of Finance\b0 \highlight\uc2  to \b \highlight22\uc2 intervene\b0 \highlight\uc2  to support the dollar and stop a strong yen from derailing its export-led recovery.  \par \par \uc2 This week, several things happened that combined to make the tipping point that sent the dollar soaring. For one, on Wednesday Japan reported stunningly strong fourth quarter gross domestic product growth of 7%, which was followed by a barrage of comments from Japanese officials stressing no change to official \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  policy. Then there was strong suspicion during U.S. trading the same day that Japan had put its money where its mouth was.  \par \par \uc2 "They jawboned heavily saying they have a common party line and they \b \highlight22\uc2 intervened\b0 \highlight\uc2 ," said Marcel Kasumovich, head of G-10 \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  strategy at Merrill Lynch in New York. "They go from being passive to being a little more proactive."  \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par      Time On Their Side \par    \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Unlike on many previous occasions, this week the \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 intervention\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  efforts had timing on their side. The short-term speculators in the market had grown fatigued with their repeated efforts to send the dollar crashing through the $1.30 barrier against the euro. Having succeeded in only briefly breaching $1.29 on Wednesday, they were ready to throw in the towel in any case and buy the dollar back. The turn in the yen gave them even more reason to do so.  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Because speculators, rather than longer-term investors, are in the driving seat, many \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currency\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  analysts doubt whether this marks a true turning point for the dollar, which is still down about 17% against the euro since the start of 2003. Interest rates in the U.S. remain at historic lows and are well below many global counterparts, rendering it simply less attractive to hold the \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currency\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2 .  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 "This is still speculative," said T.J. Marta, senior \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currency\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  strategist at Citibank in New York. "We view this as a short, violent shakeout."  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Still, because of the increasingly interrelated nature of markets, the dollar's rebound has already taken on a life of its own which means it could have more room to run.  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 "I think it is mostly short-term guys but this move will definitely force some longer-term accounts to reevaluate their positions," said Waiman Leung, vice president of derivatives trading at Credit Suisse Asset Management in New York.  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 It's forcing investors in other markets to do the same. Comex April gold futures dropped to 9-day lows of $402.50 per ounce Friday as the dollar rally rendered dollar-denominated gold more expensive in other major \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currencies\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2 .  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Should the dollar continue to gain ground, the yellow metal is expected to remain under pressure and target support around $400 and $398 initially should $402 support give way.  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 The yen too may have embarked on a bit more of a lasting, downward track. Marta said dealers are tired of trying to penetrate the solid defense ahead of Y105.00 put up by Japanese monetary authorities via their aggressive yen-selling \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 intervention\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2 . On Friday, the dollar traded above Y109.00 for the first time in three months as the yen sold off globally.  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 (Jamie McGeever, who covers \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 foreign exchange\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  markets in New York, has also reported on \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currencies\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  for Dow Jones Newswires in Rio de Janeiro and London.)  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 (Grainne McCarthy is Assistant Managing Editor for fixed income, macroeconomics and \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 foreign exchange\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  news. Grainne was previously Indonesia bureau chief and also reported for Dow Jones Newswires in Belgium).  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par    -Jamie McGeever; Dow Jones Newswires; 201 938 2096; jamie.mcgeever@dowjones.com   \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 - Grainne McCarthy; Dow Jones Newswires; 201 938 2381; grainne.mccarthy@dowjones.com  \f28 \par \par \uc2 Dokument CM00000020040220e02k0004n\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw184\pich22\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2760\pichgoal330
