\par\par \b \uc2 Fin Min: Bk of Korea Won't Attend Cabinet Mtgs In Future  \b0\par\par\uc2 610 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 20 Februar 2004\par 03:18\par \uc2 Dow Jones International News\par \uc2 DJI\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 2004 Dow Jones & Company, Inc.  \par \par \uc2 SEOUL (Dow Jones)--South Korea's Minister of Finance and Economy Lee Hun-Jai said Friday that rising oil and raw material prices won't affect the country's \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  policy.  \par \par \uc2 "The \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  rate should be left to supply and demand in the market and, instead, we will try to ease raw material prices in line with our policy to stabilize consumer prices," Lee said in his first weekly media briefing since taking office.  \par \par \uc2 The minister also said director generals from the finance ministries of South Korea, Japan and China will sit down for a routine meeting in China over the weekend.  \par \par \uc2 "The meeting will focus on greater regional cooperation on \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2 ," he said, without elaborating. +  \par \par \uc2 Lee, who previously said the government will "openly and confidently interfere in the financial markets when interference is necessary," rejected revealing the size of dollar-buying \b \highlight22\uc2 interventions\b0 \highlight\uc2 , like \b \highlight22\uc2 Japan\b0 \highlight\uc2  does.  \par \par \uc2 "Officially, we don't interfere in the \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  market," he said.  \par \par \uc2 Lee became the country's first two-time finance minister after his predecessor quit office last week to run in the general elections in April. Lee served as finance minister in 2000 during former President Kim Dae-Jung's administration.  \par \par \uc2 Chairing his first cabinet meeting on economic affairs Friday, Lee asked Bank of Korea governor and deputy governor not to attend the cabinet meetings in future to prevent the government from influencing the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2 's monetary policy.  \par \par \uc2 "No government offices or officials will talk about interest rates any more. That's entirely up to the Bank of Korea to decide," said Lee.  \par \par \uc2 But working-level officials from the finance ministry and the Bank of Korea will keep open an "active dialogue channel" to coordinate policies, he said.  \par \par \uc2 In the immediate future, the ministry will focus on reducing credit delinquency and boosting employment to help revive domestic consumption, Lee said.  \par \par \uc2 The number of South Koreans classified as credit defaulters - or those who fail to meet their debt obligation for at least three months - hit an all-time high of 3.7 million in December.  \par \par \uc2 "I will solve this problem," said Lee. He said details to curb credit delinquency could be out next month.  \par \par \uc2 But the government won't provide new policies to support LG Card Co. (032710.SE), after helping to put together a bailout package for the finance company to prevent it from going bankrupt.  \par \par \uc2 "LG Card would have to save itself through the existing bailout package," Lee said.  \par \par \uc2 The government will continue its financial sector restructuring as scheduled, including the sales of Korea Investment Trust Corp. and Daehan Investment Trust Corp. The government plans to sell the two nationalized investment trust companies in the first half of this year.  \par \par \uc2 "If we succeed in revitalizing the service sector from last year's severe depression, our economy could return to its past growth level," Lee said.  \par \par \uc2 The South Korean economy is estimated to have grown just 2.9% last year, less than half of the 6.3% expansion in 2002. The government officially targets growth at 5%-5.9% this year, but former finance minister Kim Jin-Pyo said 6% growth was possible.  \par \par \uc2 Lee told the National Assembly on Thursday that the economy might not meet its growth target this year if a recovery in consumption doesn't pick up speed.  \par \par \uc2 Elaborating Friday, he said: "I meant it's both difficult to attain 5% and also that 5% growth isn't sufficient to spur necessary employment recovery."  \par \par \uc2 -By Hye-Seung Seo, Dow Jones Newswires; 822-732-2165; hyeseung.seo@dowjones.com  \par \par \uc2 -Edited by Dawn Lye  \par \par \uc2 Dokument DJI0000020040220e02k00021\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw172\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2580\pichgoal600
