\par\par \b \uc2 GLOBAL MARKETS-Euro up after stumble, European stocks surge.  \b0\par\par\uc2 By Simon Johnson  \par \uc2 575 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 19 Februar 2004\par 11:51\par \uc2 Reuters News\par \uc2 LBA\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 2004 Reuters Limited  \par \par \uc2 LONDON, Feb 19 (Reuters) - The euro recouped some losses after a sharp slide in the previous session as markets tested whether the single \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  can sustain recent gains, while European stocks surged to new peaks helped by strong corporate results.  \par \par \uc2 Asian shares were mixed after profit taking on Wall Street on Wednesday. The dollar's recovery in the previous session from a life-time low against the euro squeezed European government debt and gold prices. Crude oil futures were flat ahead of U.S. inventory data.  \par \par \uc2 The euro edged up on Thursday after a two-cent fall in the previous session, leaving markets to ponder whether the single \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2 's rally has run out of steam, at least temporarily.  \par \par \uc2 "Everyone was taken by surprise by the sharp correction of the dollar and snowballing closing of short dollar positions," said Niels Christensen, \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  strategist at Societe Generale in Paris. "The market is still shell-shocked."  \par \par \uc2 The dollar staged a strong recovery on the euro, yen and Swiss franc on Wednesday, partly helped by warnings from European politicians about the euro's strength.  \par \par \uc2 Suspected \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  from the Bank of \b \highlight22\uc2 Japan\b0 \highlight\uc2  in early Asian trade on Thursday also served to support the dollar's bounce.  \par \par \uc2 However, by 1000 GMT the euro had clawed back a third of a percent from its closing levels to $1.2712 but was still two cents below a record high of $1.2927 set early on Wednesday.  \par \par \uc2 The dollar was up against the yen at 106.89 yen.  \par \par \uc2 The European \b \highlight22\uc2 Central Bank\b0 \highlight\uc2 's governing council meets today, but is not expected to discuss monetary policy. The latest U.S. jobless figures are due at 1330 GMT.  \par \par \uc2 STOCKS, BONDS  \par \par \uc2 European stocks forged ahead to the highest level in more than 18 months, boosted by upbeat corporate results such as Royal Bank of Scotland, Zurich Financial and mining group BHP Billiton.  \par \par \uc2 The world's largest steel maker Arcelor reported pleasing results after the market closed on Wedneday, reducing its debt by more than double its target.  \par \par \uc2 "On balance the results... have been solid, reinforcing the view that corporate profitability is improving as companies cut costs," said Nigel Cobby, a managing director of European Equities at JP Morgan.  \par \par \uc2 By 1030 GMT, the FTSE Eurotop 300 index was up 0.98 percent at 1,013.8 points. The Dow Jones Euro Stoxx 50 index was up 0.76 percent at 2919 points.  \par \par \uc2 Japanese shares closed higher on Thursday with the weaker yen boosting exporters. The Nikkei was up 0.7 percent to 10,753.80 points. A broad index of Asian shares excluding Japan was down 0.5 percent, mirroring a slide on Wall Street the previous session.  \par \par \uc2 European government debt prices slipped on Thursday, hurt by the euro's softer tone and ahead of hefty debt supply.  \par \par \uc2 At 1030 GMT, the two-year Schatz yield was up 2.2 basis points at 2.32 percent. On Wednesday, the yield had marked its lowest level since June 24, at 2.24 percent.  \par \par \uc2 The 10-year Bund yield was up 2.8 basis points at 4.11 percent.  \par \par \uc2 Brent crude futures were flat at $31.00 after strong gains on Wednesday and ahead of the 1530 GMT release of U.S. inventory data. Safe-haven gold slipped slightly to stand at $410.10/$410.80, down from its New York close at $410.50/$411./25.   \par \par \uc2 Dokument LB