\par\par \b \uc2 Dollar recoils on spreading doubt about reversal.  \b0\par\par\uc2 By Mariko Hayashibara  \par \uc2 665 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 19 Februar 2004\par 07:54\par \uc2 Reuters News\par \uc2 LBA\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 2004 Reuters Limited  \par \par \uc2 TOKYO, Feb 19 (Reuters) - The dollar was a touch softer on Thursday as scepticism lingered about whether a broad-based rebound a day earlier, which included a two-percent reversal rally from record lows versus the euro, was sustainable.  \par \par \uc2 While suspicion was growing that the dollar's precipitous two-year downtrend may have run its course, an absence of any clear change in the market's environment kept traders wary about trying to pick a bottom.  \par \par \uc2 The dollar's impressive recovery against the euro the previous day was partly helped by warnings from European officials about the euro's strength, putting traders on alert for possible \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  from the European \b \highlight22\uc2 Central Bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  (ECB).  \par \par \uc2 "The dollar got a further boost from euro officials' remarks suggesting a need for \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2 , but it's too early to say the dollar is on a genuine recovery path as there are no prospects of higher interest rates yet," said Tomoko Fujii, market and economic analysis director at Nikko Citigroup.  \par \par \uc2 German Economy Minister Wolfgang Clement said on Wednesday that the strong euro was one risk to recovery and Europe should be prepared to act if necessary.  \par \par \uc2 By 0618 GMT, the dollar was trading at around $1.2710 down a quarter cent from late New York levels but up nearly two cents from record lows around $1.2930 per euro hit on Wednesday.  \par \par \uc2 The pound drifted about two cents below the previous day's 11-year highs around $1.9140 while the Australian dollar was about a cent below seven-year highs around $0.80  \par \par \uc2 "The dollar rose across the board, but to see a real self-sustainable recovery, the U.S. current account deficit problem needs to peak out and this is likely to take until later in the year," said Takashi Yamanaka, senior economist at UFJ Bank.  \par \par \uc2 TURNAROUND?  \par \par \uc2 Although most traders thought the dollar's downtrend remained intact, others warned that a turnaround could not be ruled out.  \par \par \uc2 "There is no consensus in the market yet, but there is a feeling that something may be changing," said Toru Umemoto, \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  strategist at Morgan Stanley.  \par \par \uc2 He noted that Wednesday's rally was all the more impressive as it - ironically - came only hours after surprisingly strong Japanese growth data, which showed the economy was booming at a seven percent annual pace in October-December, and after the dollar plunged to new lows against the euro, sterling and the Australian dollar.  \par \par \uc2 "It could just be that people who had bet on an accelerated drop in the dollar on the Japanese GDP figures were disappointed by a lack of such an outcome. They didn't get a big down move after the Group of Seven meeting earlier this month either, so they might be a bit frustrated," Umemoto said.  \par \par \b \highlight22\uc2 JAPAN INTERVENTION\b0 \highlight\par \par \uc2 Against the yen, the greenback erased some losses and inched up on suspected \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  by \b \highlight22\uc2 Japan\b0 \highlight\uc2 .  \par \par \uc2 The greenback was near the day's high around 106.80 yen up from a low of 106.30 yen. It rose as high as 107.15 yen on Wednesday.  \par \par \uc2 Japan's Nikkei stock average climbed almost one percent at one point, buoyed by an easing of the yen against the dollar and the strong market debut for Shinsei Bank It ended trade up 0.72 percent at 10,753.80.  \par \par \uc2 In the \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  market, speculation was growing about whether U.S. investment fund Ripplewood Holdings would repatriate funds generated from Shinsei Bank's $2.35 bln initial public offering (IPO), as such a move could give a boost to the dollar.  \par \par \uc2 Dealers recalled Oracle Corp Japan's IPO in April 2000, which resulted in a huge amount of repatriation flows in just several days and sparked a dollar-buying spree.  \par \par \uc2 For now, the market was awaiting the U.S. weekly jobless claims report due later in the day. The ECB's governing council was also due to meet but no interest rate announcement is scheduled.   \par \par \uc2 Dokument LBA0000020040219e02j00135\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw172\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2580\pichgoal600
