\par\par \b \uc2 Dollar recoils as scepticism lingers about reversal.    \b0\par\par\uc2 By Mariko Hayashibara    \par \uc2 710 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 19 Februar 2004\par 06:57\par \uc2 Reuters News\par \uc2 LBA\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 2004 Reuters Limited    \par \par \uc2 TOKYO, Feb 19 (Reuters) - The dollar was a touch softer on Thursday as scepticism lingered about whether a broad-based rebound a day earlier, which included a two-percent reversal rally from record lows versus the euro, was sustainable.    \par \par \uc2 Against the yen, the greenback erased some losses and inched up towards the day's high on suspected \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  by \b \highlight22\uc2 Japan\b0 \highlight\uc2 .    \par \par \uc2 "After the dollar's rally yesterday, (the Japanese authorities) seem be letting exporters sell dollars and at the same time preventing the dollar/yen rate from going lower," said Jun Takeuchi, general manager at Gaitame.com.    \par \par \uc2 By 0245 GMT, the dollar had edged up near the day's high around 106.80 yen from a low of 106.30 yen. The greenback rose as high as 107.15 yen on Wednesday.    \par \par \uc2 Against the euro, the dollar stood at $1.2716/19 , down a quarter cent from late New York levels but up nearly two cents from record lows around $1.2930 hit on Wednesday.    \par \par \uc2 The pound drifted about two cents below the previous day's 11-year highs around $1.9140 , while the Australian dollar was about a cent below seven-year highs around $0.80 .    \par \par \uc2 While suspicion was growing that the dollar's precipitous two-year downtrend may have run its course, an absence of any clear change in the market's environment kept traders wary about trying to pick a bottom.    \par \par \uc2 "The dollar rose across the board, but to see a real self-sustainable recovery, the U.S. current account deficit problem needs to peak out and this is likely to take until later in the year," said Takashi Yamanaka, senior economist at UFJ Bank.    \par \par \uc2 Dealers said that Wednesday's rally was not driven by any fundamental news but a result of buy-backs by some major fund managers that snowballed into a wider short-covering spree.    \par \par \uc2 TURNAROUND?    \par \par \uc2 Although most traders thought the dollar's downtrend remained intact, others warned that a turnaround could not be ruled out.    \par \par \uc2 "There is no consensus in the market yet, but there is a feeling that something may be changing," said Toru Umemoto, \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  strategist at Morgan Stanley.    \par \par \uc2 He noted that Wednesday's rally was all the more impressive as it - ironically - came only hours after surprisingly strong Japanese growth data, which showed the economy was booming at a seven percent annual pace in October-December, and after the dollar plunged to new lows against the euro, sterling and the Australian dollar.    \par \par \uc2 "It could just be that people who had bet on an accelerated drop in the dollar on the Japanese GDP figures were disappointed by a lack of such an outcome. They didn't get a big down move after the Group of Seven meeting earlier this month either, so they might be a bit frustrated," Umemoto said.    \par \par \uc2 "But we might be seeing what may be change in the trend."     \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par Japan's Nikkei stock average  climbed almost one percent at one point,\par buoyed by an easing of the yen against the dollar and the strong market\par debut for Shinsei Bank         . It ended morning trade up 0.78 percent\par at 10,760.40.\par     \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 In the \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 foreign exchange\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  market, speculation was growing about whether U.S. investment fund Ripplewood Holdings would repatriate funds generated from Shinsei Bank's $2.35 bln initial public offering (IPO), as such a move could give a boost to the dollar.    \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Dealers recalled Oracle Corp Japan's (4716.T) IPO in April 2000, which resulted in a huge amount of repatriation flows in just several days and sparked a dollar-buying spree. For now, the market was awaiting the U.S. weekly jobless claims report due later in the day. The European \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 Central Bank\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2 's governing council was also due to meet but no interest rate announcement is scheduled. ((Reporting by Mariko Hayashibara and Shinichi Kishima, editing by Brent Kininmont; Reuters Messaging: mariko.hayashibara.reuters.com@reuters.net; +81-3-3432-8478))    \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 For instant \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 forex\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  news click on     \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par  \par  ..        Debt...      Indicators...\par   \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 Currency\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  reports..\par  \par All spots  Tokyo spots        Europe spots        Volatilities\par Tokyo \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 Forex\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  market info from BOJ\par  \par   World \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 central bank\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  news\par   Economic Forecasts...          Official rates...\par   \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 Forex\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  Diary.......             Top events........\par   Diaries...........        Diaries Index........\par   Press Digests....         Polls on G7 economies..\par     \f28 \par \par \uc2 Dokument LBA0000020040219e02j000k2\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw184\pich22\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2760\pichgoal330
