\par\par \b \uc2 EUROPEAN MORNING BRIEFING: US Jobless Data In Focus  \b0\par\par\uc2 2,322 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 19 Februar 2004\par 06:56\par \uc2 Dow Jones International News\par \uc2 DJI\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 2004 Dow Jones & Company, Inc.  \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par US SUMMARY: Stocks Fall On Profit-Taking, Treasurys Flat \par  \par DJIA         10671.99    loss 42.89    dn 0.4% \par NASDAQ        2076.47    loss  3.88    dn 0.2% \par S&P 500       1151.82    loss  5.17    dn 0.5% \par NASDAQ Future 1516.50    gain  5.50    up 0.4% \par S7P Future    1153.70    gain  2.50    up 0.2% \par 10-Yr U.S. Treasury: 99 19/32, unchanged to yield 4.05% \par    \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 NEW YORK (Dow Jones)--Wall Street stock markets fell Wednesday on a bout of profit-taking, while Treasurys ended flat as a rebounding dollar offset weak housing data.  \f28 \par \par \uc2 STOCKS: U.S. stocks couldn't hold onto Tuesday's gains Wednesday as investors took profits from recent strength against the backdrop of a weak home construction report, a rebound in the dollar and a ratings downgrade of blue-chip SBC Communications.  \par \par \uc2 SBC, which fell 1.7%, set the tone after Credit Suisse First Boston downgraded the stock to neutral from outperform based on the view that Cingular's purchase of AT&T Wireless will overhang SBC's stock for the next 9 to 12 months and the price paid of $15 a share was higher than expected.  \par \par \uc2 BellSouth, which jointly owns Cingular with SBC, was also downgraded to neutral from outperform, sending the stock down 2.4%.  \par \par \uc2 On the economic front, the Commerce Department reported home construction fell for the first time in five months in January, dropping 7.9% last month to a seasonally adjusted 1.903 million annual rate. Building permits, an indicator of future building activity, fell 2.8%. A Dow Jones Newswires-CNBC poll of 18 economists predicted housing starts would slip 4.3%.  \par \par \uc2 That put pressure on homebuilding stocks.  \par \par \uc2 BONDS: U.S. treasurys prices ended little changed on the day Wednesday as evidence of weakness in housing was offset by a rebounding dollar, suggesting that foreign central banks may not need to buy as many U.S. bonds as part of \b \highlight22\uc2 currency intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2 .  \par \par \uc2 The housing data helped push the 10-year yield below 4%, but gains then eroded.  \par \par \uc2 "It's going to take some disappointing data on jobless claims or payrolls" for the 10-year yield to stay below 4%, said a bond strategist.  \par \par \uc2 Bond investors will focus Thursday on jobless claims for the latest week. Claims are expected to fall by 9,000 to a level of 354,000, suggesting an improving labor market.  \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par ASIAN SUMMARY: Stocks Boosted By Dollar, IPO; Bonds Lower \par USD-Yen:      106.67     loss 0.13      dn 0.1% \par AUD-USD:      0.7920     gain 0.0026    up 0.3% \par Hang Seng:    13896.44   loss 31.94     dn 0.2% \par Nikkei 225:   10766.61   gain 89.80     up 0.8% \par    \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 TOKYO (Dow Jones)--Tokyo stocks advanced Thursday as export-related issues got a boost from a sharp rise in the dollar against the Japanese yen.  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Technology issues such as Advantest and Tokyo Electron, as well as automakers Honda Motor, Nissan and Toyota, rose Thursday morning.  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 The market was also upbeat about the strong debut of Shinsei Bank Ltd. on the Tokyo Stock Exchange - one of the most closely monitored initial public offering stocks in Japan slated for this year.  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Shinsei, successor to the provisionally nationalized Long-Term Credit Bank of Japan (LTCB), is the first Japanese bank to be bought and turned around by foreign investors.  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Investors in Tokyo ignored the drop in U.S. stocks Wednesday.  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Japanese government bonds fell Wednesday, pressured by profit-taking after bargain-hunting prompted a brief early surge.  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Traders expect investors to buy more government bonds in the run-up to the end of March, but any gains in the dollar or stocks will cap the upside. At the same time, bargain-hunting will continue to provide support, say some.  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 However, Barclays Capital strategist John Richards thinks it's time to build shorts in JGBs, based on the strength of the economy.  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 "The next significant move in rates will be higher and it will come sooner than we expected. Dip buyers on weakness that came in yesterday won't be around for much longer," he said.  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par EUROPEAN OUTLOOK: Stocks Seen Mixed, Bonds Steady \par  \par Euro-USD      1.2721     gain 0.0035    up 0.3% \par Stlg-USD      1.8916     gain 0.0062    up 0.3%   \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 LONDON (Dow Jones)--European stocks are set for a mixed start with the dollar and key earnings to dictate early direction. Bonds are set for a steady start after the euro rose against the dollar overnight.  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 STOCKS: European stocks are set for a mixed but steady start Wednesday, with markets looking to corporate earnings and economic data for directional impetus.  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 IG Index is calling the FTSE 100 down 5 points at 4437, the DAX up 5 points at 4100 and the CAC 40 unchanged at 3709.  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 European stocks were little changed Wednesday on concern that the sluggish euro-zone economy may not be able to support more market gains.  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Earnings will again stay near the top of the agenda with DaimlerChrysler, Total, Royal Bank of Scotland, Zurich Financial Services, ABB, ING and Reed Elsevier among those posting results.  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 During the afternoon, attention is likely to turn to U.S. Jobless data as well as earnings reports from the likes of Wal-Mart.  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 BONDS: European government bonds should open steady, after the dollar dropped back against the euro following Tuesday's rebound.  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Economic data from Europe and the U.S. will be in focus Wednesday, but traders expect \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currency\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  moves to dominate early trading in the bond markets.  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Traders expect investors to start moving out along the euro-zone yield curve if the market continues with its recent uptrend because the short end has recently outperformed and offers less value.  \f28 \par \par \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 FOREX\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2 : The dollar starts the session lower against other major \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currencies\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  Wednesday as Tuesday's rally ran out of steam amid skepticism that the greenback can sustain a turnaround short-term.  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Traders said Tuesday's near 2% reversal in the value of the greenback against the other major \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currencies\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  was a natural correction after the markets had become oversold, but didn't suggest a longer run-up any time soon.  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Suspected \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 intervention\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  by \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 Japan\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2 's \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 central bank\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  failed to prevent the dollar slipping back against the yen, euro and pound.  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Some market participants are predicting that sterling will hit $2 in coming weeks.  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par CALENDAR: Thursday, Feb. 19: US Jobless, UK Retail Sales \par GMT/EST Country Indicator/Event \par  \par 0700/0200  GER  4Q Final GDP \par 0730/0230  ITA  Dec Sales and orders \par 0745/0245  FRA  Dec Current Account \par 0930/0430  UK   Jan PSNB/PSNCR \par 0930/0430  UK   Jan Retail Sales                             +1330/0830  US   Feb 14 Ini