\par\par \b \uc2 WRAPUP 1-Chirac, Clement, German industry warn of euro risk.  \b0\par\par\uc2 By Dave Graham  \par \uc2 514 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 18 Februar 2004\par 17:45\par \uc2 Reuters News\par \uc2 LBA\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 2004 Reuters Limited  \par \par \uc2 BERLIN, Feb 18 (Reuters) - Germany and France again warned on Wednesday that the rise in the euro's exchange rate posed a risk to Europe's economic recovery after the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  hit a new high against the dollar.  \par \par \uc2 Speaking at a three-way summit with British Prime Minister Tony Blair and German Chancellor Gerhard Schroeder in Berlin, French President Jacques Chirac expressed concern about \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  market volatility and said stability was vital for growth.  \par \par \uc2 "Europe is the main zone in the world which is absorbing the strong volatility in exchange rates. A large degree of stability is absolutely necessary for sustained growth," Chirac said in televised remarks.  \par \par \uc2 German Economy Minister Wolfgang Clement said the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  was one risk to recovery and Europe should be prepared to act if necessary. But he declined to say at what point the European \b \highlight22\uc2 Central Bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  should \b \highlight22\uc2 intervene\b0 \highlight\uc2  to halt the euro's rise.  \par \par \uc2 "...we see that the recovery is not free from risks. This in particular applies to the euro's exchange rate against the dollar. The exchange rate of the euro against the dollar must remain sensible and bearable," Clement said in a statement.  \par \par \uc2 "We can't have the euro zone bearing the burden of the dollar's adjustment on its own while for example the Japanese and Chinese central banks are partially compensating for the pressure on their \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2 ," he said.  \par \par \uc2 In an apparent reference to Asia, Clement added Europe should be prepared to act unilaterally if others did not respond to the G7's renewed call for more exchange rate flexibility.  \par \par \uc2 Germany's BdB private banks group and VDMA engineering association also warned of the potential risk the euro posed.  \par \par \uc2 The VDMA said the euro, which surged to a lifetime high of $1.2927 early on Wednesday, was impeding stronger engineering output growth in 2004.  \par \par \uc2 "An exchange rate of $1.40 or $1.50 would be a complete catastrophe. But it won't come to that," VDMA President Diether Klingelnberg told reporters in Frankfurt, saying the European \b \highlight22\uc2 Central Bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  and \b \highlight22\uc2 Japan\b0 \highlight\uc2 's \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  would \b \highlight22\uc2 intervene\b0 \highlight\uc2  first.  \par \par \uc2 The BdB said the single \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  had not risen enough yet to outweigh the positive effects of strengthening demand, but that avoiding a "clear overshoot" was in all countries' interests.  \par \par \uc2 "All nations involved should do their homework," the BdB said in its monthly report.  \par \par \uc2 Along with the United States, Germany is one of the world's top export nations and the government is counting on export growth to boost the economy.  \par \par \uc2 Investor concerns about U.S. budget and current account deficits and the prospect of low interest rates outweighed better than expected U.S. capital inflow data on Tuesday, driving the dollar lower.  \par \par \uc2 The euro trimmed its gains later on Wednesday, trading at $1.2810 by 1630 GMT.  \par \par \uc2 The euro's trade weighted index is below record peaks set in January but it has appreciated 3.5 percent against the dollar since the start of the month.  \par \par \uc2 (additional reporting by Iain Rogers and Nick Antonovics).   \par \par \uc2 Dokument LBA0000020040218e02i003fd\par }\page {\par\