\par\par \b \uc2 Dollar Rebounds Vs Majors As  \highlight22\uc2 Intervention \highlight\uc2  Fears Weigh  \b0\par\par\uc2 By Jamie McGeever  \par \uc2 Of DOW JONES NEWSWIRES  \par \uc2 640 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 18 Februar 2004\par 18:43\par \uc2 Dow Jones Capital Markets Report\par \uc2 CM\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 2004 Dow Jones & Company, Inc.  \par \par \uc2 NEW YORK (Dow Jones)--The dollar is rebounding across the board midday Wednesday, led by strong buying against the yen in a market growing increasingly wary of holding euros above $1.2900 for fear of \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  from European monetary authorities. \u57345\'E0\'01 If there's been any dollar-buying \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  Wednesday, however, it's more likely to have come from Japanese authorities, who are suspected of having sold yen for dollars through at least two agent banks, dealers said.  \par \par \uc2 This lifted the dollar to two-week highs against the yen and helped push the euro briefly below $1.2800. The single \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  had printed a new lifetime high around $1.2930 in Asian hours Wednesday, while sterling and the Australian dollar also surged to fresh multiyear highs.  \par \par \uc2 But the dollar is recovering in New York trade as a wide range of market participants - including model-driven funds, Commodity Trading Advisors, hedge funds and U.S. corporates - lock in profits. The dollar's immediate upside, however, may be limited. Good bids for euros and sterling around current intraday lows are emerging and large Japanese exporter offers around Y106.50 could prove tough to absorb, dealers said.  \par \par \uc2 "The market seems to get very nervous every time we get up around $1.29," on the euro, said Grant Wilson, senior trader at Mellon Bank in Pittsburgh. "Dollar/yen is leading the way here and it looks like the dollar is having a good time of it right now but we're seeing some bids for euro and sterling down here."  \par \par \uc2 The euro briefly dipped as low as $1.2790 late Wednesday morning, well off its record high, while sterling is trading below $1.90, well below its new 11-year peak of $1.9140.  \par \par \uc2 At 12:40 p.m. EST Wednesday (1740 GMT), the euro was at $1.2803, weaker than $1.2835 late Tuesday in New York. But lifted by the dollar's gains against the yen, the single \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  was up at Y136.32 from Y135.66.  \par \par \uc2 Against the Swiss franc, the dollar was up at CHF1.2307 from CHF1.2271, while sterling was weaker at $1.8987 from $1.9074 late Tuesday. The dollar was up at Y106.47 -its intraday high - from Y105.67.  \par \par \uc2 The dollar opened New York already rebounding from its overnight lows but this trend accelerated on heavy buying against the yen. One large U.S. investment bank, CTAs, funds and at least two Japanese banks - favored conduits for \b \highlight22\uc2 Ministry of Finance intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  - were all active.  \par \par \uc2 "I'm pretty certain it was \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  as they (Japanese monetary authorities) are trying to force the issue and back the dollar away from Y105.50 as best they can after the great GDP number," said Enrico Caruso, senior dealer at Tempest Asset Management in Irvine, Calif.  \par \par \uc2 Wednesday, Japan's government announced that the economy grew a real 1.7% in last quarter of 2003 from the previous period, or 7.0% in annualized terms, beating a market consensus of on-quarter growth of 1.2% and marking the fourth straight quarter of growth. The expansion was the fastest in 13 years.  \par \par \uc2 On the back of these figures, capital flows into Japan should pick up from their already stellar pace, suggesting any yen-selling \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  from Japanese monetary authorities Wednesday could have been of a preemptive nature.  \par \par \uc2 Japanese monetary authorities regularly \b \highlight22\uc2 intervene\b0 \highlight\uc2  in \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  markets selling yen for dollars in a bid to maintain the competitiveness of the country's exporters and protect \b \highlight22\uc2 Japan\b0 \highlight\uc2 's economic recovery. European officials have verbally \b \highlight22\uc2 intervened\b0 \highlight\uc2  in recent weeks to talk down the euro for exactly the same reasons but have yet to \b \highlight22\uc2 intervene\b0 \highlight\uc2  selling their \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2 .  \par \par \uc2 -Jamie McGeever; Dow Jones Newswires; 201 938 2096; jamie.mcgeever@dowjones.com  \par \par \uc2 1074  \par \par \uc2 Dokument CM00000020040218e02i0002c\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw172\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2580\pichgoal600
