\par\par \b \uc2 Tokyo leads Asian shares after growth data.   \b0\par\par\uc2 By Neil Fullick   \par \uc2 744 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 18 Februar 2004\par 07:11\par \uc2 Reuters News\par \uc2 LBA\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 2004 Reuters Limited   \par \par \uc2 SINGAPORE, Feb 18 (Reuters) - Tokyo offered Asian shares a firm lead on Wednesday after data showed the world's second-biggest economy grew faster than expected in the December quarter, but the yen struggled to rise due to suspected Bank of \b \highlight22\uc2 Japan intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2 .   \par \par \uc2 The beleaguered dollar held near multi-year lows against several \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2 , with the high-yielding pound pushing to a fresh 11-year high against the U.S. unit.   \par \par \uc2 Base metals, such as copper and aluminium, and silver pushed to their highest in several years on hopes for world economic growth.   \par \par \uc2 Tokyo's benchmark Nikkei average rose as much as 0.9 percent to 10,798.15 in early dealings, helped by the data and a firm close on Wall Street. It eased back by midday to be flat.   \par \par \uc2 Japan's gross domestic product (GDP) grew 1.7 percent in the three months to December from July-September, underpinned by strong exports and investment in items like plant and machinery.   \par \par \uc2 The result outpaced economists' expectations for 1.1 percent growth in the quarter. GDP rose seven percent on an annualised basis, indicating stronger growth than the United States, which expanded an annualised four percent in the fourth quarter.    \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par Hong Kong's Hang Seng index  gained one percent and South Korean shares\par         hopped to a fresh near 22-month high, before skipping lower on\par the day. Steel maker POSCO             was flat at 168,000 won having\par traded 1.8 percent higher earlier.\par    \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Australian shares were at their highest since June 2002, driven by metal firms BHP Billiton (BHP.AX) and the country's four major banks.    \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par Taiwan's TAIEX  hit its highest level since September 2000, before\par heading lower on the day. Singapore's Straits Times Index        was\par firm.\par    \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 MSCI's broadest index of markets in Asia outside Japan hit a near four-year high of 243.10.   \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 YEN CAPPED   \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 "The basic message is that the speed of economic growth improved dramatically towards the end of calendar 2003, driven by exports and corporate investments," said Richard Jerram, economist at ING Barings Securities. "I think the outlook remains good going into 2004."   \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 However, the yen struggled to find any support from the data and was knocked lower by suspected \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 intervention\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  by the Bank of \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 Japan\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2 , which wants to limit the \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currency\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2 's strength to support the country's exporters.   \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Traders said the dollar suddenly jumped to 105.75 yen from 105.60 yen, which they believe marked \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 central bank\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  buying.    \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par Sterling  pumped up to an 11-year high of $1.9084, lifted by higher\par British interest rates. The euro       , which rose to a life high of\par $1.29 in January, was firm at $1.2857.\par    \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 The Australian dollar rose to a seven-year high of 79.88 U.S. cents on Tuesday on hopes for higher interest rates, but had backed off on Wednesday to 79.76 cents.   \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Tokyo heavyweight NTT DoCoMo (9437.T) underpinned Tokyo, gaining as much as 4.6 percent to 229,000 yen on expectations it would reap $6.5 billon in cash from the $41 billion takeover of AT&T Wireless (AWE.N) by Cingular Wireless (SBC.N).   \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 NTT, Japan's largest mobile telephone carrier and the second biggest issue by market value listed on the Tokyo market, said on Tuesday it would sell its 16 percent stake in AT&T.    \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par The AT&T takeover boosted Wall Street, to leave the Dow Jones\par industrial average  up 0.8 percent at 10,714.88 and the Nasdaq\par composite         up 1.3 percent at 2,080.35.\par    \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Data from the Federal Reserve showing business activity at factories, mines and utilities increased a sharp 0.8 percent in January from December, reinforced confidence in the economic outlook. Copper , widely used in construction and wiring, rose to an eight-year high of $2,843.50 a tonne and aluminium pushed to a four-year high of $1,752 a tonne.    \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par Tin  was at an eight-year high, zinc        a three-year high and lead\par at its highest since the early 1990s.\par    \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Precious metal silver, used in the photographic industry, rose to a six-year high of $6.86 an ounce. Spot gold was trading at $415.80 an ounce, steady from New York.   \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 U.S. light crude futures fell from four-week highs but stayed firm above $35 a barrel underpinned by worries about tight supplies in the months ahead.    \f28 \par \par \uc2 Dokument LBA0000020040218e02i0015f\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw172\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2580\pichgoal600
