\par\par \b  \highlight22\uc2 FOREX \highlight\uc2  VIEW: Dollar, Flows Relationship Breaks Down A Bit  \b0\par\par\uc2 979 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 17 Februar 2004\par 21:02\par \uc2 Dow Jones Capital Markets Report\par \uc2 CM\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 2004 Dow Jones & Company, Inc.  \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par    By Jamie McGeever \par    A DOW JONES NEWSWIRES COLUMN \par    \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 NEW YORK (Dow Jones)--Huge investment flows into U.S. asset markets are failing to lift the heavy downward pressure weighing on the dollar because of two main reasons: investor efforts to insulate themselves against \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currency\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  losses and short-term speculators pushing the U.S. \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currency\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  down.  \f28 \par \par \uc2 Data released Tuesday showed foreign investor appetite for U.S. assets in December was voracious. That was also a month in which the dollar was sold off aggressively and slumped to fresh lows on an almost daily basis.  \par \par \uc2 Indeed, the relationship between the dollar and U.S. portfolio flows in December was a microcosm of the year as a whole: net inflows hit record levels and were more than enough to cover the U.S. current account deficit but the dollar depreciated sharply to plumb multiyear lows and against the euro, a lifetime low.  \par \par \uc2 On the face of it, the two don't add up, simply because net inflows into an economy typically boost a \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2 . But the dollar weakened over 5% against the euro in December and short-term \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  speculators, often hedge funds, and foreign investors seeking protection against further dollar weakness - a policy known as \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  hedging - are the principal reasons why.  \par \par \uc2 "It's hedging," said T.J. Marta, \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  analyst at Citibank in New York. "People like U.S. assets but not the dollar." This type of hedging against dollar weakness could involve selling dollars in the forward market while at the same time buying underlying assets, Marta said.  \par \par \uc2 Steven Englander, chief \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  strategist for North America at Barclays Capital in New York, pointed out that at the start of December, interest rate futures contracts were forecasting a federal funds target rate of 1.35% in June 2004, but had fallen to 1.12% by the end of the month.  \par \par \uc2 Englander said this could well have provided investors with an attractive arbitrage opportunity to buy longer-dated debt, such as Treasury bonds, while at the same time selling overnight money market contracts, which don't appear in the Treasury's flows data.  \par \par \uc2 "Hedging was certainly a contributory factor behind the decline in the dollar," agreed Michael Woolfolk, senior \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  strategist at Bank of New York in New York. "And the dollar fell victim to speculative selling pressure which began mounting after the Sept. 20 G7 communique. With low U.S. interest rates the dollar became a `funding \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2 ' for carry trades investing in such high-yielders as the Australian dollar and sterling and to a lesser degree the euro and Canadian dollar."  \par \par \uc2 Low interest reates in the U.S. have been a key factor weighing on the dollar and prompted a massive shift into higher-yielding alternatives.  \par \par \uc2 The Group of Seven's September statement, calling for "more flexibility" in exchange rates, was interpreted as a tacit approval for a weaker dollar in order to help redress major global economic and financial "imbalances" such as the U.S. current account deficit. It triggered an acceleration in the dollar's global selloff.  \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par    Market Hears What It Wants To Hear \par    \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 The flows data released Tuesday show the current account gap, running at an estimated $550 billion a year or $45 billion a month, is actually being adequately funded, despite all the brouhaha to the contrary.  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 A net $75.5 billion flowed into U.S. stocks and bonds in December, compared with an even healthier $87.5 billion in November, the Treasury said. That brought the total net inflow for 2003 to a record $708 billion from $575 billion a year earlier. This means not only is the deficit being plugged, the net foreign direct investment outflows of up to around $100 billion a year are also being offset.  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 What's more, it's not only the well-documented demand from Asian central banks that's driving investment flows into the U.S., private investors are also buying U.S. assets in large quantities.  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Citibank's Marta notes that purchases of U.S. corporate assets typically reflect private investor interest while purchases of government and agency debt reflect official, or \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 central bank\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2 , interest. Both are trending higher.  \f28 \par \par \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 Japan\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2 , which sold over Y2 trillion in \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currency\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  market \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 interventions\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  in December - buying dollars in an effort to limit yen strength - increased its holdings of U.S. Treasury notes and bonds to a record $545.2 billion in December from $525.5 billion the previous month. China more than doubled its net purchases of agency and government securities to $8.7 billion.  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Using data pertaining to Bermuda and the Bahamas as a proxy for foreign private demand for U.S. debt, Woolfolk at Bank of New York notes that the respective inflows into the U.S. announced by the Treasury of $7.2 billion and $2.1 billion show good underlying demand from the private sector too.  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Rising stock markets, good demand for the Treasury Department's quarterly refunding last week, anecdotal evidence and record yen-selling \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 intervention\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  from \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 Japan\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2 's \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 Ministry of Finance\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  in January suggest flows into U.S. assets remain strong. Yet the dollar's continued weakness - the euro hit a record high of $1.2898 in January - also points to continued hedging activity and speculative selling.  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 That means the Treasury's portfolio flow data, while certainly a market-mover in recent months, aren't quite as good a guide to the dollar's behavior after all. Or the market nly reacts if the flows contract a lot.  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 "When they matter is when they tell the market what it wants to hear," noted Englander at Barclays Capital.  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 (Jamie McGeever, who covers \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 foreign exchange\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  markets in New York, has also reported on \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currencies\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  for Dow Jones Newswires in Rio de Janeiro and London.)  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par    -Jamie McGeever; Dow Jones Newswires; 201 938 2096; jamie.mcgeever@dowjones.com \par    \f28 \par \par \uc2 Dokument CM00000020040217e02h0003p\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw172\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2580\pichgoal600
