\par\par \b \uc2 GLOBAL MARKETS-Euro eyes highs vs dollar, European stocks surge.  \b0\par\par\uc2 By Simon Johnson  \par \uc2 558 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 17 Februar 2004\par 12:02\par \uc2 Reuters News\par \uc2 LBA\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 2004 Reuters Limited  \par \par \uc2 LONDON, Feb 17 (Reuters) - The euro eyed its all time high against the dollar on Tuesday, boosting the region's government bond prices, while European stocks chased Asian bourses higher to stand at levels not seen since August 2002.  \par \par \uc2 Crude oil prices rose in thin trade and the weaker dollar helped gold gain.  \par \par \uc2 A day after European \b \highlight22\uc2 Central Bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  President Jean-Claude Trichet passed on the chance to talk down the surging single \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2 , the euro stood half a cent from its all time high.  \par \par \uc2 Trichet's refusal to say whether the ECB had or would \b \highlight22\uc2 intervene\b0 \highlight\uc2  to temper its rise, left markets with no clear obstacle to pushing the euro gradually higher.  \par \par \uc2 "Trichet wasn't really signalling anything new yesterday," said Paul Mackel, \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  strategist at ABN AMRO.  \par \par \uc2 "With him also mentioning that some imbalances need to adjust, this is implicitly dollar-negative."  \par \par \uc2 Slumping investor confidence in Germany failed to help the dollar against the euro with markets also focusing on U.S. industrial production figures for January due at 1415 GMT.  \par \par \uc2 At 1040 GMT, the dollar traded around half a percent lower on the day at $1.2847 per euro, within sight of all-time lows at $1.2898.  \par \par \uc2 Nervousness over possible Bank of \b \highlight22\uc2 Japan intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  helped the dollar to stand a touch higher at 105.50 yen after hitting 11-day highs in Asia.  \par \par \uc2 Some dealers said they thought Tokyo may have come into the market to soften the yen ahead of fourth-quarter gross domestic product figures due on Wednesday, which are expected to show growth of around five percent.  \par \par \uc2 STOCKS, BONDS  \par \par \uc2 European stocks surged to 18-month highs on Tuesday taking their cue from Asia where indices posted strong gains.  \par \par \uc2 Mobile giant Vodafone spurred European gains after it said it had withdrawn from the auction for AT&T Wireless, sending its shares up seven percent.  \par \par \uc2 By 1040 GMT, the FTSE Eurotop 300 index of pan-European blue chips was 0.8 percent higher at 1,003.8 points.  \par \par \uc2 The narrower DJ Euro Stoxx 50 index rose 0.6 percent to 2,894.0 points.  \par \par \uc2 Earlier a broad index of Asian shares edged close to four year highs, while Japan's Nikkei index ended the day up 1.4 percent on hopes for strong fourth quarter GDP data, due on Wednesday. Wall Street was closed on Monday.  \par \par \uc2 Euro zone government bond yields fell on Tuesday as the euro headed above $1.28. But flagging investor sentiment in Germany, due to worries over the effect of the euro on corporate profits, failed to add any further impetus.  \par \par \uc2 "There is no doubt that it is disappointing but it is still consistent with healthy economic recovery," said Simon Rubinsohn, chief economist at Gerrard in London.  \par \par \uc2 At 1040 GMT, the two-year Schatz yield was down 3.5 basis points at 2.287 percent, but still off a 4-1/2-month low of 2.261 percent struck on Friday. The 10-year Bund yield was down 3.5 basis points at 4.08 percent.  \par \par \uc2 Crude oil futures reversed Monday's losses in thin trade to stand up 17 cents at $30.49. Safe-haven gold was at $413.50/$414.25 against its close in Europe on Monday at $410./80/$411.30.   \par \par \uc2 Dokument LBA0000020040217e02h001rz\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw172\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2580\pichgoal600
