\par\par \b  \highlight22\uc2 FOREX \highlight\uc2 -Dollar down versus euro, eyes $1.29 after Trichet.   \b0\par\par\uc2 By Burton Frierson   \par \uc2 631 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 17 Februar 2004\par 10:08\par \uc2 Reuters News\par \uc2 LBA\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 2004 Reuters Limited   \par \par \uc2 LONDON, Feb 17 (Reuters) - The euro rose within a cent of all-time highs against the dollar on Tuesday, a day after European \b \highlight22\uc2 Central Bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  (ECB) President Jean-Claude Trichet passed up an opportunity to express concern over its strength.   \par \par \uc2 Markets took his unwillingness to depart from the line the Group of Seven nations recently agreed - that rapid \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  moves hurt growth - as a signal that he remained relaxed about euro strength as long as it did not gain in leaps and bounds.   \par \par \uc2 This, along with his refusal to discuss in European parliamentary testimony whether the ECB had or would \b \highlight22\uc2 intervene\b0 \highlight\uc2  to temper its rise, left markets with no clear obstacles to pushing the euro gradually higher.   \par \par \uc2 "Trichet wasn't really signalling anything new yesterday. With him also mentioning that some imbalances need to adjust, this is implicitly dollar-negative," said Paul Mackel, \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  strategist at ABN AMRO.   \par \par \uc2 "Euro/dollar will be sensitive coming up through $1.29 but once it goes through the market will say the trend is still higher."   \par \par \uc2 At 0900 GMT, the dollar traded half a percent lower on the day at $1.2846 per euro , within sight of all-time lows at $1.2898.   \par \par \uc2 Sterling extended its rally against the greenback to climb above $1.90 for the first time in 11 years.   \par \par \uc2 However, the dollar traded a touch higher at 150.54 yen after hitting 11-day highs in Asia on nervousness over possible Bank of \b \highlight22\uc2 Japan intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2 .   \par \par \uc2 Some dealers said they thought Tokyo may have come into the market to soften the yen ahead of fourth-quarter gross domestic product figures due on Wednesday, which are expected to show growth of around five percent.   \par \par \uc2 However, some dealers blamed dollar/yen's gains on options-related buying or the yen's fall against other \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2 . The yen traded down half a percent against the broadly stronger euro at 135.41 yen .   \par \par \uc2 Although the dollar fell to 3-1/2 year lows around 105.15 yen last week, traders grew hesitant about retesting those troughs on the view that the Japanese authorities were determined to defend the 105 yen level.   \par \par \uc2 "The lower end of the ranges have been so difficult to break, and people are having to wonder what they would have to do if they built up short dollar positions here," said Hiroyuki Watanabe, a \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  manager at Shinsei Bank.   \par \par \uc2 Japanese Finance Minister Sadakazu Tanigaki reiterated on Tuesday that \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  was aimed at curbing speculative movements in the market.   \par \par \uc2 DATA DUE, NO NEW ECB VIEW   \par \par \uc2 With Trichet's remarks leaving little changed in the ECB's stance, markets focused on upcoming data releases, including Germay's ZEW indicator which is expected to fall to 72.7 in February from 72.9 a month ago when it is released at 1000 GMT.   \par \par \uc2 "If it is a stronger-than-expected figure today you could say it shows the German economy can stand a strong euro and that's also positive for the euro," said Mackel at ABN AMRO.   \par \par \uc2 During a see-saw session on Friday, the euro climbed within a whisker of January's record highs but then rapidly fell more than one cent, fanning rumours the European \b \highlight22\uc2 Central Bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  (ECB) may have been behind the drop.   \par \par \uc2 Analysts brushed aside the rumours but said the price action showed markets were nervous about Europe's position on the euro, making Trichet's Monday testimony more timely.   \par \par \uc2 Traders also awaited U.S. industrial production data for January due at 1415 GMT and monthly portfolio flow data from the U.S. Treasury.   \par \par \uc2 Output growth was forecast at 0.8 percent month-on-month in a Reuters poll after a rise of 0.1 percent in December.    \par \par \uc2 Dokument LBA0000020040217e02h001kf\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw172\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2580\pichgoal600
