\par\par \b \uc2 GLOBAL MARKETS-Stocks edge to new highs, dlr near lows.  \b0\par\par\uc2 By Neil Fullick  \par \uc2 676 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 17 Februar 2004\par 04:37\par \uc2 Reuters News\par \uc2 LBA\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 2004 Reuters Limited  \par \par \uc2  SINGAPORE, Feb 17 (Reuters) - Asian stocks crept up to near four-year highs on Tuesday, helped by demand for technology firms, while the beleaguered U.S. dollar shuffled in tight trading ranges above multi-year lows.   \par \par \uc2  Base metals, like copper and aluminium, were at their highest values in several years, reflecting global economic growth, while gold was flat.   \par \par \uc2  But the region's financial markets were trading in small volumes in the absence of leads from U.S. markets, which were closed on Monday to mark Presidents Day.   \par \par \uc2  Hong Kong's Hang Seng index reached a fresh two-and-half-year high, with China plays in focus, before edging lower by mid-morning. Taiwan's TAIEX extended a 40-month high, led by plastics firms, and Seoul's Korea Composite Stock Price Index traded at its highest level since May 2002 as investors focused on exporters.   \par \par \uc2  MSCI's broadest index of markets in Asia outside Japan rose as high as 241.05, marking its highest since April 2000.   \par \par \uc2  The Nikkei average in Tokyo edged up 0.8 percent in morning dealings to 10,629.87, led by foreign buying of technology stocks such as Sony Corp.   \par \par \uc2  The consumer electronics giant, which plans to increase the volume of digital camera sales by as much as 50 percent in the next fiscal year, rose two percent to 4,400 yen.   \par \par \uc2  "Foreign investors were seen hunting for high-techs and auto issues that look cheap," said Koichi Seki, equity manager at Chuo Securities. "But they are less interested in those dependent on domestic demand, although such issues had risen recently prior to the GDP data."   \par \par \uc2  Japan is due to release fourth-quarter gross domestic product data on Wednesday, which is expected to show the world's second-biggest economy expanded at a greater pace than the world's biggest economy, the United States.   \par \par \uc2  JAPAN GDP   \par \par \b \highlight22\uc2 Currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  dealers say such a result could weaken the resolve of the Bank of \b \highlight22\uc2 Japan\b0 \highlight\uc2  to \b \highlight22\uc2 intervene\b0 \highlight\uc2  to bolster the dollar's value to help exporters.   \par \par \uc2  "If it turns out that the Japanese economy is doing better, they would lose their justification for \b \highlight22\uc2 intervening\b0 \highlight\uc2 ," said Mitsuru Sahara, vice president of the \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  dealing group at UFJ Bank.   \par \par \uc2  At 0226 GMT, the dollar was trading at 105.53 yen, above a 40-month low reached in January of 105.15 yen. Dealers fear Bank of \b \highlight22\uc2 Japan\b0 \highlight\b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  at 105.   \par \par \uc2  The euro was trading at $1.28, below a life high of 1.29 reached last month.   \par \par \uc2  The dollar found little support on Monday from comments by European \b \highlight22\uc2 Central Bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  President Jean-Claude Trichet, who said excessive \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  volatility was undesirable. He avoided any signal on lowering interest rates.   \par \par \uc2  The Australian dollar pushed towards a seven-year peak of 79.48 U.S. cents, reached on Friday, emboldened by \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  comments on interest rates.   \par \par \uc2  Base metals traded around multi-year highs in Asia, extending a bull run built on surging demand from China's factories.   \par \par \uc2  Copper, widely used in construction and wiring, rose as high as $2,750.50 a tonne, its highest since 1995. Aluminium nudged up to 1,730 a tonne, a four-year high.   \par \par \uc2  Zinc, used to protect steel from corrosion, was trading around three-year peaks of $1,114 a tonne. Lead was at $865 a tonne, around seven-year peaks.   \par \par \uc2  Australia's diversified miner BHP Billiton rose 0.8 percent to A$11.90 on the back of the base metals prices.   \par \par \uc2  Spot gold was trading around $413 an ounce, the top end of its trading range for the day and up slightly from London.   \par \par \uc2  U.S. light crude future were trading at $34.51 a barrel, flat from the last trading day on Friday, but underpinned by a pledge from oil cartel OPEC to cut production.   \par \par \uc2  On Friday, the Dow Jones industrial average fell 0.62 percent to 10,627.85 and the tech-heavy Nasdaq Composite Index dropped one percent to 2,053.56.   \par \par \uc2 Dokument LBA0000020040217e02h000hf\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw184\pich22\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2760\pichgoal330
