\par\par \b \uc2 U.S. data cools Asian stocks - dollar steady.   \b0\par\par\uc2 By Neil Fullick   \par \uc2 631 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 16 Februar 2004\par 07:24\par \uc2 Reuters News\par \uc2 LBA\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 2004 Reuters Limited   \par \par \uc2 SINGAPORE, Feb 16 (Reuters) - Asian stocks stepped back from four-year highs on Monday in cautious trading after confidence in the outlook for the U.S. economy took a knock, while the dollar hovered just above multi-year lows against the euro and the yen.    \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par The dollar dangled just above record lows against the euro  and\par three-and-a-half year lows against the yen       .\par    \f28 \par \par \uc2 Wariness among \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  dealers of Bank of \b \highlight22\uc2 Japan intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  to help keep Japanese exporters competitive offered the dollar a floor, but traders said they saw renewed dollar weakness to come.   \par \par \uc2 "The general trend in the market is still for dollar selling," said Toru Umemoto, \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  strategist at Morgan Stanley.   \par \par \uc2 The dollar fell within a whisker of record lows versus the euro on Friday on profit-taking amid rumours that the European \b \highlight22\uc2 Central Bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  (ECB) had \b \highlight22\uc2 intervened\b0 \highlight\uc2 .   \par \par \uc2 "At this point, it was probably just a rumour but the market is getting nervous," said a trader at a Japanese securities firm.   \par \par \uc2 Stocks stepped tentatively following reports showing the trade deficit of the United States, the world's biggest economy, ballooned to a record in 2003 and consumer confidence withered in February.   \par \par \uc2 Tokyo's key Nikkei average rose just 7.55 points to 10,575.24 in its morning session, paring early gains as \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  jitters hit Advantest Corp. (6857.T) and other high-tech exporters.   \par \par \uc2 Most other Asian markets were slightly lower, although Hong Kong showed modest gains.   \par \par \uc2 MSCI's broadest index of markets in Asia outside Japan was down just 0.1 percent at 239.76 points at 0219 GMT, just off Friday's high of 240.37, the highest since March 2000.    \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par Key indices in both Taiwan and South Korea hit new highs before pulling\par back. The Korea Composite Stock Price Index  rose to 883.69, its\par highest since April 2002, helped by Hana Bank            .\par    \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Taiwan's TAIEX reached a 40-month high of 6,590.25, led by active trading in Chang Hwa Bank (2801.TW), up more than four percent in late morning dealings.   \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 DOLLAR SEEN WEAKENING   \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 At 0225 GMT, the euro was trading at $1.2724, just below its record high of $1.29 traded last month. The dollar was trading at 105.41 yen, holding just above the three-and-a-half year low of 105.15. Dealers fear \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 intervention\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  at 105 yen.    \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par Strong investment flows helped the Taiwan dollar  hit an 18-month high\par against the dollar of T$33.12 per dollar. The Australian dollar       ,\par which rose to a seven-year peak of 79.48 U.S. cents on Friday, was\par trying to regain ground lost in late trade on Friday. At 0230 GMT, it\par was trading at 79.02 cents.\par    \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Spot gold was trading around $410 an ounce, steady from New York. Upbeat comments by U.S. Federal Reserve Chairman Alan Greenspan injected confidence into Wall Street last week, but that was knocked on Friday by the trade and consumer reports.   \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 The U.S. trade deficit widened nearly 11 percent in December to leave the 2003 trade gap at a record $489.4 billion, 17 percent wider than 2002.   \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 The University of Michigan index of consumer confidence fell sharply to a surprisingly low 93.1 in February, reversing a strong rise in January to 103.8.   \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 "A 10-point drop in the confidence index is significant and suggests there is a new pessimism among consumers," said Chris Low, chief economist at FTN Financial in New York.   \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 The benchmark Dow Jones industrial average dropped 0.62 percent on Friday to 10,627.85, while the tech-heavy Nasdaq Composite Index fell one percent to 2,053.56.   \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 U.S. financial markets are shut on Monday to mark Presidents Day.    \f28 \par \par \uc2 Dokument LBA0000020040216e02g0024y\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw172\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2580\pichgoal600
