\par\par \b \uc2 GLOBAL MARKETS-U.S. data caps Asian stock rises - dollar steady.  \b0\par\par\uc2 By Neil Fullick  \par \uc2 636 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 16 Februar 2004\par 09:26\par \uc2 Reuters News\par \uc2 LBA\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 2004 Reuters Limited  \par \par \uc2  SINGAPORE, Feb 16 (Reuters) - Asian stocks hovered just below four-year highs on Monday in cautious trading after confidence in the outlook for the U.S. economy took a knock, while the dollar dangled just above multi-year lows against the euro and the yen.   \par \par \uc2  European markets were expected to trade lower on Monday, although traders said dealing would be quiet because U.S. markets would be closed to mark the U.S. Presidents Day.   \par \par \uc2  The dollar was supported by wariness among \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  dealers of Bank of \b \highlight22\uc2 Japan intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  to help keep Japanese exporters competitive, but traders said they saw renewed dollar weakness to come.   \par \par \uc2  "The general trend in the market is still for dollar selling," said Toru Umemoto, \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  strategist at Morgan Stanley. "The only thing that's supporting the dollar is \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2 ."   \par \par \uc2  The dollar fell within a whisker of record lows versus the euro on Friday on profit-taking amid rumours that the European \b \highlight22\uc2 Central Bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  (ECB) had \b \highlight22\uc2 intervened\b0 \highlight\uc2 .   \par \par \uc2  "At this point, it was probably just a rumour but the market is getting nervous," said a trader at a Japanese securities firm.   \par \par \uc2  Asian stocks stepped tentatively following reports showing the trade deficit of the United States, the world's biggest economy, had ballooned to a record in 2003 and consumer confidence withered in February.   \par \par \uc2  Tokyo's key Nikkei average was steady, shaving just nine points to close at 10,548.72 as high-tech issues were hit by the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  jitters and as domestic earners found support.   \par \par \uc2  Reflecting the opposing forces, Advantest Corp, Japan's leading maker of semiconductor testing equipment, fell 2.5 percent to 7,880 yen, while the country's biggest property developer, Mitsui Fudosan Co, gained 3.5 percent to 1,130 yen.   \par \par \uc2  MSCI's broadest index of markets in Asia outside Japan was up just 0.03 point at 240.03 at 0358 GMT and just below Friday's peak of 240.37, the highest since March 2000.   \par \par \uc2  Taiwan's TAIEX closed at a 40-month high of 6,565.37, helped by Cathay Life, the island's largest listed financial firm, which rose 3.25 percent to T$63.50.   \par \par \uc2  Seoul's Korea Composite Stock Price Index nudged to a 22-month high but ended 0.1 percent lower at 881.28 to break a seven-session winning streak.   \par \par \uc2  Hong Kong's Hang Seng index closed up at 13,831.53, its highest since February 2001, on the prospects for strong earnings reports.   \par \par \uc2  DOLLAR SEEN WEAKENING   \par \par \uc2  At 0806 GMT, the euro was trading at $1.2737, just below its record high of $1.29 traded last month. The dollar was trading at 105.47 yen, holding just above the three-and-a-half year low of 105.15. Dealers fear \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  at 105 yen.   \par \par \uc2  The Australian dollar, which rose to a seven-year peak of 79.48 U.S. cents on Friday, hugged 79 cents on Monday, trying to regain ground lost in late trade on Friday.   \par \par \uc2  Spot gold was trading just below $410 an ounce, steady from New York.   \par \par \uc2  Upbeat comments by U.S. Federal Reserve Chairman Alan Greenspan injected confidence into Wall Street last week, but that was knocked on Friday by the trade and consumer reports.   \par \par \uc2  The U.S. trade deficit widened nearly 11 percent in December to leave the 2003 trade gap at a record $489.4 billion, 17 percent wider than 2002.   \par \par \uc2  The University of Michigan index of consumer confidence fell sharply to a surprisingly low 93.1 in February, reversing a strong rise in January to 103.8.   \par \par \uc2  The benchmark Dow Jones industrial average dropped 0.62 percent on Friday to 10,627.85, while the tech-heavy Nasdaq Composite Index fell one percent to 2,053.56.   \par \par \uc2 Dokument LBA0000020040216e02g001bf\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw172\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2580\pichgoal600
