\par\par \b \uc2 Dow Jones Asia-Pacific Top Stories Sked For Feb 13  \b0\par\par\uc2 2,346 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 13 Februar 2004\par 10:07\par \uc2 Dow Jones International News\par \uc2 DJI\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 2004 Dow Jones & Company, Inc.  \par \par \uc2 Following are the top Asia-Pacific stories featured on Dow Jones Newswires as of 0900 GMT, along with items expected later in the day. Please direct any questions or comments to asianewseds@dowjones.com or by phone on 65-6415-4020.  \par \par \uc2 MACRO/\b \highlight22\uc2 FOREX\b0 \highlight\uc2 /FIXED INCOME  \par \par \uc2 TOKYO: Top Japanese officials said Friday they were encouraged by signs China may loosen its restrictive \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign-exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  regime, a move that would allow the Chinese \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  to rise in value against the dollar. The remarks come at a time of growing speculation that China might let its \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2 , the yuan or renminbi, move in a slightly wider band, bowing to calls from trading partners for it to do so. ("=WRAP: Japan Voices Cautious Support For China \b \highlight22\uc2 FX\b0 \highlight\uc2  Change" ran at 0848 GMT)  \par \par \uc2 BEIJING: Rising food prices drove up China's consumer price index for the 13th straight month in January, setting the stage for an upward trend that should prevail through 2004, economists said. ("DATA SNAP: China's January CPI Up For 13th Straight Month" ran at 0132 GMT; DATA VIEW will run around 1030 GMT)  \par \par \uc2 BEIJING: China recorded a trade deficit of $30 million in January, data issued Friday by the General Administration of Customs show. The data varied from those issued by the Ministry of Commerce Wednesday, which showed a deficit of $20 million and exports totaling $35.72 billion. ("China Jan Trade/Customs -2: Exports At $35.71 Bln" ran at 0235 GMT)  \par \par \uc2 SHANGHAI: China top banking regulator put his weight behind China's banking reform, making the work of restructuring state banks and rural cooperatives a top priority for this year. ("China Bk Regulator:State Bk, Rural Coop Reform A Priority" first take ran at 0306 GMT)  \par \par \uc2 SYDNEY: Australian housing finance approvals fell moderately in December, adding to the view that the overheated housing sector is finally cooling. ("=WRAP: Australian Dec Housing Finance Slows Slightly" ran at 0110 GMT)  \par \par \uc2 =\b \highlight22\uc2 FX\b0 \highlight\uc2  ASIA: There may be some relief in sight for the overworked Japanese Finance Ministry bureaucrats who orchestrate the nation's \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2 -market \b \highlight22\uc2 interventions\b0 \highlight\uc2  - but not until late this year. By then, the recovery in the world's second-biggest economy may fizzle, prompting a natural slide in the yen, which is now near a three-year high against the dollar. ("=\b \highlight22\uc2 FX\b0 \highlight\uc2  Asia: \b \highlight22\uc2 Japan\b0 \highlight\uc2 's \b \highlight22\uc2 Intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  May Let Up Toward Year-End" ran at 0421 GMT)  \par \par \uc2 =ASIA DEBT: Hutchison Whampoa's gargantuan $5 billion bond sale in November made its mark in more ways than one. Besides snatching the crown as the largest-ever bond from ex-Japan Asia, it may also have reignited a trend of selling debt in multiple tranches. Bankers say the multi-tranche structure is being pushed heavily to potential issuers across the region, notably by the seven banks involved in the Hutchison Whampoa deal. Goldman Sachs, Citigroup, HSBC and Merrill Lynch were the joint book runners on all three tranches. They were joined by Deutsche Bank on the seven-year tranche, JPMorgan on the 10-year chunk and Morgan Stanley on the 30-year portion. ("=ASIA DEBT: More Multi-Tranche Bonds As Big Issues Loom" ran at 0733 GMT)  \par \par \uc2 SHANGHAI: China's \b \highlight22\uc2 Ministry of Finance\b0 \highlight\uc2  designated new short-term benchmarks for the country's bond market, a key reform that has implications for building a true market-based interest rate system. (story will run around 1030 GMT or later)  \par \par \uc2 SEOUL: South Korean companies are putting off overseas bond offerings earlier planned for the next two months due to rising borrowing costs in emerging market debt and a government campaign to keep the dollar-won exchange rate supportive of local exports. ("=EXCLUSIVE/S Korean/1Q USD Bond -2:Rising Borrowing Costs" ran at 0735 GMT)  \par \par \uc2 KUALA LUMPUR: Just beneath the smooth surface of Malaysian markets this week lies lots of thinking and rethinking by investors as they absorb the jolt of two high-profile arrests, including one cabinet minister. ("=Malaysia Abdullah Anti-Corruption Move To Win Mkt Praise" ran at 0756 GMT)  \par \par \uc2 =JAPAN CREDIT: Japanese companies and corporate bond investors are getting to know each other. Firms that previously focused on maintaining relations with shareholders are, like their U.S. and European counterparts, wooing corporate bond holders that are increasingly sophisticated and more choosy in the debt they hold. ("=JAPAN CREDIT: Companies Get Serious With Bond Holders" ran at 0524 GMT)  \par \par \uc2 HONG KONG: Now that China's Communist authorities have clearly signaled they won't accept an early move to democracy in Hong Kong, local activists are starting to look at more modest proposals in hopes of finding some that might pass muster with Beijing. ("=HK's Democrats Look For A Reform Plan Beijing Can Accept" ran at 0655 GMT)  \par \par \uc2 WELLINGTON: New Zealand's weak retail sales for December has given more ammunition to opponents of interest rate hikes, but that probably won't be enough to push the country's \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  governor Alan Bollard into performing an embarrassing about-face by leaving rates unchanged in March. ("=DATA VIEW: Ostensibly Soft Data May Not Sway NZ Bollard" ran at 0007 GMT)  \par \par \uc2 MANILA: The Asian Development Bank is looking to increasing its exposure to the private sector this year by providing around $800 million in financial backing to projects in financial services, infrastructure development and trade across the region. (INTERVIEW with ADB official will run around 1015 GMT)  \par \par \uc2 =\b \highlight22\uc2 FOREX\b0 \highlight\uc2  MARKET ROUNDUP: The dollar held steady in a tight range against the yen in Asia Friday, as Japanese monetary authorities kept guard against any attempts to chase the Japanese \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  higher. ("Asia \b \highlight22\uc2 Forex\b0 \highlight\uc2 : Japan MOF Pressure Keeps Dollar Steady Vs Yen" ran at 0543 GMT)  \par \par \uc2 EQUITIES  \par \par \uc2 SEOUL: Hyundai Motor, South Korea's largest auto maker, said Friday its 2003 net profit surged 21% to a record high 1.75 trillion won, buoyed by robust exports and sales of pricier models. ("Hyundai Motor Earnings -3: Brisk Exports, Pricier Models" ran at 0236 GMT; WRAP will run after 0900 GMT)  \par \par \uc2 TOKYO: The Japanese unit of McDonald's said Friday its group net loss deepened to Y7.12 billion for the year ended December from a loss of Y2.34 billion the previous year due to weak sales and a special loss resulting from the cancellation of the services of a consulting firm managed by a former McDonald's chief executive officer. ("McDonald's Japan -2: Hurt By Weak Sales, Special Loss" ran at 0727 GMT; WRAP will run around 1000 GMT)  \par \par \uc2 SEOUL: Hana Bank's fourth-quarter net profit surged to KRW176.5 billion from KRW33.8 billion a year ago, boosted by higher interest and fee income as well as improved net interest margin. ("S Korea Hana Bank/Earnings -3: Interest, Fee Income Up" ran at 0446 GMT; WRAP will run around 0930 GMT)  \par \par \uc2 HONG KONG: The Securities & Futures Commission said it is investigating possible market manipulation at the stock market debuts of two major initial public offerings at the end of last year. The regulator is conducting a probe into the pre-market trading of shares in PICC Property & Casualty and Great Wall Automobile Holding. ("HK SFC Probes Bid Pricing At Debuts Of PICC, Great Wall" first take ran at 0333 GMT)  \par \par \uc2 HONG KONG: Investors can't seem to get enough of share placements, with 16 major share placements so far this year as Hong Kong-listed firms or big shareholders take advantage of current high share prices to cash in. Fund managers and bankers expect to see more block sales this year as more investors join the bull camp, but those deals aren't likely to come cheap as strong liquidity continues to drive demand. (story will run around 0930 GMT)  \par \par \uc2 TOKYO: Tokio Marine & Fire Insurance, the largest Japanese non-life insurance company, said Friday it has received approval from the Chinese government to start selling property and casualty insurance policies to Chinese corporations and individuals. ("Tokio Marine To Start Selling Insurance To Chinese Cos" ran at 0204 GMT)  \par \par \uc2 SINGAPORE: Thailand's third budget airline Nok Air intends to break-even within a year, paving the way for an initial public offering in the next few years, said chief executive Patee Sarasin. ("=INTERVIEW:Thai Budget Carrier Aims To Breakeven In 1 Yr" ran at 0222 GMT)  \par \par \uc2 HONG KONG: Century Sunshine Ecological Technology, a fertilizer maker specializing in organic fertilizer for the tea industry for Fujian province, said Friday the retail tranche of its IPO was 125 times oversubscribed, while the placement tranche was 4 times oversubscribed. The company raised HK$52.8 million ahead of its listing on Hong Kong's Growth Enterprise Market Tuesday. ("HK Century Sunshine IPO Retail Tranche 126 Times Covered" ran at 0219 GMT)  \par \par \uc2 WELLINGTON: A dispute between Australasian insurer Tower Ltd. and its largest shareholder, London-based investment company Guinness Peat Group, over the sale of a parcel of Tower shares might end up in the courts, Tower's chairman Olaf O'Duill said Friday. ("=INTERVIEW: NZ Tower, GPG May Clash In Court Over Shares" ran at 0050 GMT)  \par \par \uc2 WELLINGTON: Loss-making TelstraClear., New Zealand's second largest fixed-line telephone company, Friday said the slow pace of regulatory change is disrupting its investment plans and capping earnings growth. Earlier, the company reported its first-half operating loss narrowed to NZ$18 million from NZ$38 million a year ago, but revenue growth remained modest. ("=INTERVIEW: Telstra's NZ Growth Hindered By Slow Reforms" ran at 0303 GMT; "NZ's TelstraClear Narrows 1H Loss But Rev Growth Modest" ran at 2252 GMT)  \par \par \uc2 TOKYO: Kanebo and Kao have denied a local media report that they will revise Kao's planned acquisition of Kanebo's cosmetics division. The Asahi Shimbun reported in its Friday morning edition that debt-strapped textile and cosmetics maker Kanebo will review the deal in which Kao would acquire Kanebo's cosmetics \b \highlight22\uc2 operations\b0 \highlight\uc2 , due to strong opposition from Kanebo's labor union. ("Kanebo, Kao Deny Review Of Cosmetic Ops Acquisition" ran at 0329 GMT)  \par \par \uc2 TOKYO: Major seasonings maker Ajinomoto said Friday it chalked up 78% of its group net profit target for the current fiscal year in the nine months ended December, posting a group net profit of Y27.36 billion. ("Ajinomoto -2: Keeps This FY Earnings Outlooks" ran at 0639 GMT)  \par \par \uc2 TOKYO: Railway operator East Japan Railway Co., or JR East, reported weak earnings for the April-December period, hit by sluggish revenue from its railway transport and station kiosk \b \highlight22\uc2 operations\b0 \highlight\uc2 . ("JR East -2: Cuts FY Rev Outlook, Keeps Pft View Unchanged" ran at 0727 GMT)  \par \par \uc2 TOKYO: Japanese consumer financing firm Orient Corp. said Friday a strong financing business is keeping it on track to achieve a projected earnings recovery for the fiscal year to March after four straight years in the red. Orient left unchanged its group net profit outlook for the year at Y26.3 billion. ("Orient -2: Expects 1st FY Profit After 4 Years Of Loss" ran at 0434 GMT)  \par \par \uc2 TOKYO: Domestic shipments of cellular phone handsets in 2003 surged 27% to 50.78 million units, surpassing the 50 million mark for the first time. ("Japan 2003 Mobile Phone Shipments Jump 27% To 52.2M Units" ran at 0312 GMT)  \par \par \uc2 BEIJING: China's top insurance regulator said Friday that Ping An Insurance (Group) Co. and New China Life Insurance Co. would list this year. Pin An Insurance is widely expected to be seeking a Hong Kong listing, while New China Life is expected to be preparing a listing of A shares in the mainland. ("China Regulator/Insur -2: Listings Overseas, In China" ran at 0307 GMT)  \par \par \uc2 HONG KONG: Morgan Stanley Capital International Friday said it will add auto maker AviChina Industry & Technology and gold mine operator Fujian Zijin Mining Industry to its Standard Index Series as of the close of Feb. 27. ("MSCI Adds China's AviChina, Fujian Zijin To Index Series" ran at 0211 GMT)  \par \par \uc2 SEOUL: South Korea's STX Shipbuilding says it has secured an order worth $172 million to build container vessels, for Hansa Treuhand Schiffsbeteiligungs of Germany, with an optional contract to build two more ships at a later date. ("S Korea STX/Order -2: Two 3,500 TEU Container Vessels" ran at 0239 GMT)  \par \par \uc2 SEOUL: Hanaro Telecom, South Korea's second-largest broadband services provider, said its net loss for 2003 widened to KRW165.3 billion from KRW123.1 billion a year earlier, due to the disposal of outdated equipment and a rise in non-operating expenses. ("Korea Hanaro Telecom/Results -2: Revenue Up 10% On Yr" ran at 0148 GMT)  \par \par \uc2 SEOUL: Dacom Corp., South Korea's second-largest fixed-line operator, said its fourth-quarter net loss widened to KRW201 billion from KRW3.7 billion a year ago due to bigger allowances for bad debt and disposal of bad assets. ("S Korea Dacom/Results -3: Cites Disposal Of Bad Assets" ran at 0536 GMT)  \par \par \uc2 BANGKOK: Siam City Cement, Thailand's second-biggest cement producer, reported a 27% rise in 2003 net profit to THB3.29 billion from THB2.59 billion in 2002, due to higher cement sales and asset-divestment gains. ("Siam City Cement/Earnings-3: Sales Up, Asset Sale" ran at 0350 GMT)  \par \par \uc2 SINGAPORE: The proposed introduction of privately managed pension plans in Singapore is viable and NTUC Income Insurance Cooperative hopes it will be among the two or three providers selected in the initial phase, its Chief Executive Officer Tan Kin Lian said. ("INTERVIEW: Singapore Pvt Pension Plan Is Viable-Income CEO" ran at 0111 GMT)  \par \par \uc2 ENERGY/COMMODITIES  \par \par \uc2 WELLINGTON: New Zealand's largest power generator, Meridian Energy, said Friday it is seeking to raise US$200 million in debt from the U.S. private placement market, and aiming for seven-year, 10-year and 12-year tranches. ("=NZ Meridian Energy/$200M -2: Roadshows Begin Next Week" ran at 0236 GMT)  \par \par \uc2 CANBERRA: Barley marketing concern ABB Grain Ltd. said Friday it's in preliminary discussions about a possible merger with grain logistics and malting concern AusBulk Ltd. ("Australia's ABB -2: Natural Fit With AusBulk" ran at 0202 GMT)  \par \par \uc2 62550  \par \par \uc2 Dokument DJI0000020040213e02d000dm\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw184\pich22\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2760\pichgoal330
