\par\par \b \uc2 ANALYSIS- \highlight22\uc2 Japan \highlight\uc2  seen needing to address  \highlight22\uc2 FX intervention \highlight\uc2  risk.   \b0\par\par\uc2 By Tamawa Kadoya   \par \uc2 748 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 13 Februar 2004\par 08:26\par \uc2 Reuters News\par \uc2 LBA\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 2004 Reuters Limited   \par \par \uc2 TOKYO, Feb 13 (Reuters) - Dampening the yen's rise may be one of the few policy tools the Japanese government has left to reflate the economy, with fiscal stimulus constrained by soaring public debt and short-term interest rates stuck at zero.   \par \par \uc2 But its massive yen-selling \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  could be reaching a limit, and risks of the policy need to be addressed as the nation piles up government debt and foreign reserves, analysts say.   \par \par \uc2 "The effects of \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  are limited. But what is more frightening is the prospect of a surge in debt at a time when the nation's finances are already in tatters," said Sayuri Kawamura, a senior economist at Japan Research Institute.   \par \par \uc2 The government borrows yen by selling short-term government paper to pay for its dollar-buying in the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  market.   \par \par \uc2 Japan sold a record 20.4250 trillion yen ($193.8 billion) in the \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  market last year, followed by over seven trillion yen in January alone, a record for a single month. Authorities are believed to have \b \highlight22\uc2 intervened\b0 \highlight\uc2  intermittently since then to slow the yen's rise.   \par \par \uc2 Some argue that the yen's rise would have been much steeper if the authorities had not stepped in. Japanese officials worry that a strong yen could harm an export-led recovery.   \par \par \uc2 The yen rose some 10 percent against the dollar in 2003, compared with a more than 15 percent rise by the euro against the dollar during the year.   \par \par \uc2 It has continued its rise, hitting a three-year high of 105.16 yen to the dollar on Wednesday. On Friday afternoon in Tokyo it was around 105.40 yen.   \par \par \uc2 Others say Tokyo's persistent forays into the market are preventing the dollar/yen rate from finding a natural trough, and declining volatility for the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  pair is hindering the market's inherent function.   \par \par \uc2 "\b \highlight22\uc2 Intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  is killing the market," said a senior \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  dealer at an European bank.   \par \par \uc2 Despite that, Japanese authorities may be satisfied to have prevented a sharp rise in the yen, which would have sparked criticism and pressure from politicians and business circles.   \par \par \b \highlight22\uc2 Currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  policy is strictly controlled by the \b \highlight22\uc2 Ministry of Finance\b0 \highlight\uc2  (MOF), while the Bank of \b \highlight22\uc2 Japan\b0 \highlight\uc2  conducts \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  as the government's agent in the market.   \par \par \uc2 COSTLY \b \highlight22\uc2 INTERVENTION\b0 \highlight\par \par \uc2 The costs of such unfettered yen-selling, although not yet evident, may turn out to be high.   \par \par \uc2 Official data shows financing bills (FB) for \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  funds totalled 65.46 trillion yen at the end of September. Analysts estimate the amount at around 77 trillion yen as of January.   \par \par \uc2 The massive scale of the forays into the market has depleted the government's coffers, and parliament last week approved an extra budget increasing in the amount of debt the government can issue to raise funds for \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2 . The government is asking for even more funds in the budget for the year starting in April.   \par \par \uc2 If the MOF issues an additional 21 trillion yen from the extra budget, and the full 40 trillion yen earmarked in next year's budget, weekly issuances of FBs would rise to 10 trillion yen, according to calculations by J.P. Morgan.   \par \par \uc2 That may be possible now, with short-term interest rates near zero, but any rise in rates would cause a severe headache for the government.   \par \par \uc2 Even then, \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  at the present pace would be unsustainable as the government would run out of funds within the year, said Junya Tanase, a \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  analyst at J.P. Morgan in Tokyo.   \par \par \uc2 The huge \b \highlight22\uc2 interventions\b0 \highlight\uc2  have caused \b \highlight22\uc2 Japan\b0 \highlight\uc2 's foreign reserves ballooned to a record $741.246 billion in January. Most of the funds are channelled into U.S. Treasuries.   \par \par \uc2 MOF data shows its special account that holds foreign \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  assets acquired through \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  will rack up unrealised losses of some 7.79 trillion yen by the end of March.   \par \par \uc2 MOF officials have shrugged it off, saying such losses will not be realised unless they buy yen for dollars, which they say is unlikely to happen soon, and that profits from foreign \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  assets and other sources are expected to hit 1.43 trillion yen this year.   \par \par \uc2 Some suggest the authorities should think of more effective ways to use the funds available.   \par \par \uc2 "The current \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  is not working. Authorities need to get the market on their side and there needs to be an element of surprise," former top financial diplomat Eisuke Sakakibara told Reuters in a recent interview.    \par \par \uc2 Dokument LBA0000020040213e02d0017b\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw172\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2580\pichgoal600
