\par\par \b \uc2 Watanabe: Won't Rule Out Joint  \highlight22\uc2 Intervention \highlight\uc2  With ECB  \b0\par\par\uc2 138 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 12 Februar 2004\par 11:29\par \uc2 Dow Jones International News\par \uc2 DJI\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 2004 Dow Jones & Company, Inc.  \par \par \uc2 TOKYO (Dow Jones)--\b \highlight22\uc2 Japan\b0 \highlight\uc2  may consider carrying out coordinated \b \highlight22\uc2 currency intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  with the European \b \highlight22\uc2 Central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  if needed to stem excess exchange rate volatility, a senior Japanese Finance Ministry bureaucrat said Thursday.  \par \par \uc2 "We're not ruling out the possibility," said Hiroshi Watanabe, head of the ministry's International Bureau, when asked if \b \highlight22\uc2 Japan\b0 \highlight\uc2  would consider joint \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  with Europe's \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  to stem the dollar's slide.  \par \par \uc2 "Japan has said it will act, if needed, against excess volatility and disorderly movements. We will act in concert if needed," to curb disruptive moves, Watanabe said.  \par \par \uc2 But he added that it would be up to the ECB to decide to make any request for coordinated action.  \par \par \uc2 -By Miyako Takebe, Dow Jones Newswires; 813-5255-2929; miyako.takebe@dowjones.com  \par \par \uc2 Dokument DJI0000020040212e02c000jm\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw184\pich22\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2760\pichgoal330
