\par\par \b  \highlight22\uc2 FOREX \highlight\uc2  VIEW: Market Gears Up For Euro Test Of $1.30  \b0\par\par\uc2 739 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 12 Februar 2004\par 20:59\par \uc2 Dow Jones Capital Markets Report\par \uc2 CM\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 2004 Dow Jones & Company, Inc.  \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par    By Tom Barkley \par    A DOW JONES NEWSWIRES COLUMN \par    \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 NEW YORK (Dow Jones)--The so-called "pain threshold" of $1.30 beckons after the recent surge in the euro, but \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currency\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  investors will have to overcome some obstacles before testing the resolve of euro-zone officials to cap a rise in the single \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currency\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2 .  \f28 \par \par \uc2 The euro, after jumping 1 1/2 cents Wednesday after Federal Chairman Alan Greenspan downplayed the decline in the dollar, appeared to be taking a break on Thursday, never coming much closer than a 1/2 cent from last month's all-time high just below $1.29.  \par \par \uc2 The elusive $1.30 level has been in the cross hairs of speculators since early January, when the euro peaked and verbal \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  out of the euro zone reached a crescendo with European \b \highlight22\uc2 Central Bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  President Jean-Claude Trichet's warning that \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  moves had become "brutal."  \par \par \uc2 European officials also won mention of the undesirability of "excess volatility and disorderly movements" in the communique issued after last weekend's meeting of the Group of Seven leading industrial nations.  \par \par \uc2 But investors have largely viewed that phrase as a nod to Europe and not carrying the threat of U.S.-involved \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2 , and Greenspan's description of the dollar's decline as "gradual" Wednesday has encouraged speculators to try the ECB's hand.  \par \par \uc2 "If the market believes that $1.30 is the pain threshold, they're going to gun for it," said Enrico Caruso, chief dealer at Tempest Asset Management in Irvine, Calif.  \par \par \uc2 Following Greenspan's testimony, "the market is comfortable selling the dollar again," said Caruso. "We've had that explosive move, and it's quiet as the market consolidates, but it doesn't bode well for the dollar."  \par \par \uc2 However, with the move starting to run out of momentum, some further impetus may be needed to take it to the next level. Speculative positions have become long euro, increasing the risk of a sharp pullback if the dollar starts to rebound.  \par \par \uc2 Traders are also noting strong defense of an apparent option around $1.2850 putting pressure on the euro, and more options are said to lie every 1/2 cent all the way up to $1.32.  \par \par \uc2 Meanwhile, several investors have cited offers to sell euros by European central banks in recent days, though they often come into the market to process orders for commercial clients. Those orders are expected to grow as the euro climbs higher.  \par \par \uc2 "I would imagine that we'll start to see more \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  activity as we start to get up around $1.30," said Scott Ainsbury, portfolio manager at \b \highlight22\uc2 FX\b0 \highlight\uc2  Concepts in New York.  \par \par \uc2 Still, he said that the euro looks pretty well supported at current levels and that speculators would "probably have another go" at the highs.  \par \par \uc2 While a test of $1.30 is viewed as a given by many market participants at this point, whether or not that would prompt action by the ECB or other central banks is another question.  \par \par \uc2 Ainsbury thinks euro-zone officials are probably more concerned about the speed and volatility of the move, much as the Bank of \b \highlight22\uc2 Japan\b0 \highlight\uc2  has used record levels of \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  over the past year to help smooth the dollar's depreciation against the yen.  \par \par \uc2 Riz Din, \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign-exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  analyst at Barclays Capital in London, said any move toward $1.29 will probably prompt another round of rhetoric from euro-zone officials, which could make things "sticky north of here."  \par \par \uc2 There's also increased risk of some sort of policy response by the ECB in the form of a rate cut or \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2 .  \par \par \uc2 But Din doesn't expect the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  to act until the euro approaches $1.35, and he said that any policy response would probably require other negative factors to be in place besides just a stronger euro.  \par \par \uc2 "If as time progresses, there's a slowdown in economic growth elsewhere that hinders export growth in the euro zone, then the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  could become a more pressing factor at a lower level," he said, noting that euro-zone officials have also acknowledged that global growth has offset the impact of the euro on exports.  \par \par \uc2 (Tom Barkley is a special writer covering \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  in New York. He previously covered convertible and high-yield debt markets.)  \par \par \uc2 -By Tom Barkley, Dow Jones Newswires; 201-938-4385  \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par     tom.barkley@dowjones.com \par    \f28 \par \par \uc2 Dokument CM00000020040212e02c0005m\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw172\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2580\pichgoal600
