\par\par \b \uc2 Foreign central banks keep buying U.S. debt - Fed.   \b0\par\par\uc2 181 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 12 Februar 2004\par 22:30\par \uc2 Reuters News\par \uc2 LBA\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 2004 Reuters Limited   \par \par \uc2 NEW YORK, Feb 12 (Reuters) - Foreign \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  holdings of U.S. government and agency debt hit a new record, the Federal Reserve reported on Thursday, reflecting in part the massive \b \highlight22\uc2 currency intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  by the Bank of \b \highlight22\uc2 Japan\b0 \highlight\uc2 .   \par \par \uc2 The Fed said its total holdings of Treasury and agency debt for central banks abroad rose $4.364 billion to $1.126 trillion in the week ended Feb. 11.   \par \par \uc2 The breakdown of the Fed's custody holdings showed central banks sold a net $232 million of Treasuries, taking their total holdings to $893.961 billion. But they bought $4.596 billion in agency debt, taking those holdings to $232.385 biaverage $1.125 trillion a day.   \par \par \uc2 Overseas central banks, mostly from Asia, have been major purchasers of dollars in the past year or more to retard export-damaging gains in their own \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2 . Much of those dollars end up in Treasury and agency debt, so helping both to fund the U.S. government's ballooning budget deficit and to keep market interest rates down.    \par \par \uc2 Dokument LBA0000020040212e02c004v4\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw172\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2580\pichgoal600
