\par\par \b  \highlight22\uc2 FOREX \highlight\uc2 -Dollar off life low vs euro before Greenspan.  \b0\par\par\uc2 640 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 12 Februar 2004\par 15:34\par \uc2 Reuters News\par \uc2 LBA\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 2004 Reuters Limited  \par \par \uc2 (Recasts, adds comment, updates prices, changes byline, dateline; previous LONDON  \par \par \uc2 By Kyle Peterson  \par \par \uc2 CHICAGO, Feb 12 (Reuters) - The dollar was a touch stronger after flirting with new record lows against the euro on Thursday, a day after Federal Reserve Chairman Alan Greenspan dismissed worries about greenback weakness.  \par \par \uc2 The Fed chief said a weak dollar would help close the U.S. current account gap, that he was unconcerned about its impact on inflation and that the Fed could be patient about raising interest rates.  \par \par \uc2 Traders took this as a green light to sell the dollar, which fell steeply on Wednesday but remained in snug ranges on Thursday.  \par \par \uc2 "We're still very rangebound after Greenspan," said Lara Rhame, senior economist at Brown Brothers Harriman in New York. "The market seems very intent on buying the euro on dips."  \par \par \uc2 "I was surprised he didn't move toward a more neutral expression," Rhame said. "I think the markets expected that. I'm sure the market pays attention when he says he's not concerned about the dollar's decline."  \par \par \uc2 Greenspan, who gave his semiannual address on Wednesday to a House of Representatives committee, speaks to the Senate Banking Committee at 10 a.m. EST (1500 GMT) on Thursday. His prepared remarks are expected to be a repeat of Wednesday's, but a question-and-answer session could yield surprises.  \par \par \uc2 Analysts said it was unusual for Greenspan to talk about the dollar. This added to the weight of his comments on Wednesday.  \par \par \uc2 "Even though the Treasury is responsible for dollar policy, there is a clear view that the U.S. is following this benign neglect policy. Now this seems to have the support of Greenspan," said Michael Klawitter, senior \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  strategist at West LB.  \par \par \uc2 The dollar slipped slightly on news that initial jobless claims rose more than expected in the week to Feb. 7, to 363,000 from an upwardly revised 357,000 the week before.  \par \par \uc2 Additional pressure on the dollar came from data showing an unexpected decline in retail sales for January. Sales slipped 0.3 percent, the first decline since September. Excluding the volatile autos component, sales rose 0.9 percent.  \par \par \uc2 "You would expect a bigger reaction because the numbers today were slightly disappointing," said Lauren Germain, \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  strategist at Banc of America Securities in New York. "But a soft labor market and weak economic data were priced in yesterday after Greenspan's remarks."  \par \par \uc2 In early U.S. trade the euro was down 0.08 percent at $1.2815 after touching a new one-month high of 1.2846. The single \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  hit a lifetime high of $1.2898 on Jan. 12.  \par \par \uc2 The dollar was flat at 105.35 yen. Sterling was up 0.02 percent at $1.8914 after scoring an 11-year high of $1.8951.  \par \par \uc2 COORDINATED \b \highlight22\uc2 INTERVENTION\b0 \highlight\uc2 ?  \par \par \uc2 Hiroshi Watanabe, head of the Japanese \b \highlight22\uc2 Ministry of Finance\b0 \highlight\uc2 's international division, said Tokyo would take appropriate steps on \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  as needed, a clear signal to markets not to push the yen too high against the dollar.  \par \par \uc2 Watanabe said a weekend Group of Seven industrial nations warning against excess \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  volatility supported \b \highlight22\uc2 Japan\b0 \highlight\uc2 's \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  stance and that Tokyo did not rule out the possibility of coordinated \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign exchange intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2 .  \par \par \uc2 The European \b \highlight22\uc2 Central Bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  declined comment on Watanabe's coordinated \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  remarks.  \par \par \uc2 The dollar gained after Watanabe's comments after inching down to a three-year low around 105.15 on Wednesday before recovering on what New York traders suspected might have been Bank of \b \highlight22\uc2 Japan\b0 \highlight\uc2  yen-selling \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2 .  \par \par \uc2 There was no confirmation of official activity but the market is wary of Japanese \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  after it spent a record amount in January to prop the dollar up against the yen. (Additional reporting by Burton Frierson in London and John Parry in New York).   \par \par \uc2 Dokument LBA0000020040212e02c003cd\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw172\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2580\pichgoal600
