\par\par \b  \highlight22\uc2 FOREX \highlight\uc2 -Dollar feels Fed pressure, more Greenspan ahead.  \b0\par\par\uc2 By Burton Frierson  \par \uc2 641 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 12 Februar 2004\par 13:48\par \uc2 Reuters News\par \uc2 LBA\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 2004 Reuters Limited  \par \par \uc2 LONDON, Feb 12 (Reuters) - The dollar struggled to hold off record lows on the euro and multi-year lows on other \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2  on Thursday after the previous day's sell-off when Fed Chairman Alan Greenspan dismissed worries about its weakness.  \par \par \uc2 The market dumped the dollar on Wednesday when Greenspan indicated he was not concerned about the falling \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2 's impact on inflation and said the Fed could be patient about raising interest rates and that dollar falls should eventually help to contain the U.S. current account deficit.  \par \par \uc2 Taken altogether analysts said the comments indicated U.S. interest rates would stay unchanged at 1.0 percent for the time being in contrast to countries such as Britain where interest rates have started to rise, making their \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2  more attractive.  \par \par \uc2 Dealers said markets were short of dollars after recent selling and also a bit wary of European reaction to seeing the euro close in on January's record highs. U.S. weekly jobless claims due at 1330 GMT also kept investors cautious.  \par \par \uc2 "Greenspan lit the blue touch paper on dollar selling but people are worried that Europe is going to say something to the contrary and catch everyone napping," said a senior trader at a European bank in London.  \par \par \uc2 "To me it was pretty clear what Greenspan said: America hasn't got a problem with a weak dollar and Europe's got to get used to it. Europe is going to have to bite the bullet and cut rates."  \par \par \uc2 By 1230 GMT the dollar pulled just higher on the day to $1.2813 per euro , barely above the day's one-month low of $1.2846 and less than a cent away from January's record trough near $1.29.  \par \par \uc2 It had already notched up another 11-year low against the British pound, its fourth in a row, but just managed to avoid 6-1/2 year lows against the Australian dollar.  \par \par \uc2 FED APPROVAL?  \par \par \uc2 Analysts noted it was unusual for Greenspan, who will speak before the Senate Banking Committee at 1500 GMT, to talk about the dollar. This added to the weight of his comments on Wednesday.  \par \par \uc2 "Even though the Treasury is responsible for dollar policy, there is a clear view that the U.S. is following this benign neglect policy. Now this seems to have the support of Greenspan," said Michael Klawitter, senior \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  strategist at West LB.  \par \par \uc2 Economists have forecast 345,000 new filings for jobless benefits for the week ended February 7. They expect January U.S. retail sales, also due at 1330 GMT, to show no change from December.  \par \par \uc2 Worse than expected numbers, especially for jobless claims, could spark a further dollar sell-off since markets see job recovery as key to future U.S. rate hikes.  \par \par \uc2 COORDINATED \b \highlight22\uc2 INTERVENTION\b0 \highlight\uc2 ?  \par \par \uc2 Hiroshi Watanabe, head of the Japanese \b \highlight22\uc2 Ministry of Finance\b0 \highlight\uc2 's international division, said Tokyo would take appropriate steps on \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  as needed, a clear warning to markets not to push the yen too high against the dollar.  \par \par \uc2 Watanabe said a Group of Seven nations' statement at the weekend warning against excess \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  volatility supported \b \highlight22\uc2 Japan\b0 \highlight\uc2 's \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  stance and Tokyo did not rule out the possibility of coordinated \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign exchange intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2 .  \par \par \uc2 The European \b \highlight22\uc2 Central Bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  declined comment on Watanabe's coordinated \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  remarks.  \par \par \uc2 The dollar gained after Watanabe's comments and held near the day's high of 105.55 during European midsession trade.  \par \par \uc2 It inched down to a three-year low around 105.15 on Wednesday before recovering on what New York traders suspected might have been Bank of \b \highlight22\uc2 Japan\b0 \highlight\uc2  yen-selling \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2 .  \par \par \uc2 There was no confirmation of official activity but the market is hugely wary of Japanese \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  after it spent a record amount in January to prop the dollar up against the yen.   \par \par \uc2 Dokument LBA0000020040212e02c0031q\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw172\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2580\pichgoal600
