\par\par \b \uc2 UPDATE 3- \highlight22\uc2 Japan \highlight\uc2 's Watanabe says joint  \highlight22\uc2 intervention \highlight\uc2  possible.  \b0\par\par\uc2 By Jonathan Standing  \par \uc2 617 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 12 Februar 2004\par 14:02\par \uc2 Reuters News\par \uc2 LBA\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 2004 Reuters Limited  \par \par \uc2  TOKYO, Feb 12 (Reuters) - The possibility of coordinated \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  by the Group of Seven nations to prevent volatility in \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  markets cannot be ruled out, a top Japanese \b \highlight22\uc2 Ministry of Finance\b0 \highlight\uc2  official said on Thursday.   \par \par \uc2  "If coordinated \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  is necessary, we can't rule out the possibility of it taking place," Hiroshi Watanabe, head of MOF's international division, said at a regular briefing.   \par \par \uc2  He had been asked if \b \highlight22\uc2 Japan\b0 \highlight\uc2  might help in any \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  to keep the euro from rising.   \par \par \uc2  The euro  was close to a record high against the dollar on Thursday, raising speculation over whether the European \b \highlight22\uc2 Central Bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  would step in to bring down the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  because of its possible damaging effects on exporters' profits.   \par \par \b \highlight22\uc2 Japan\b0 \highlight\uc2  has been much more active in its \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  in the past year, selling some 27 trillion yen ($256 billion) since January 2003 to try to curb the yen's rise.   \par \par \uc2  Like their European counterparts, Japanese authorities are worried about the damage to exporters from surges in the yen at a time when strong export growth is fuelling Japan's recovery.   \par \par \uc2  The European \b \highlight22\uc2 Central Bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  declined to comment on Watanabe's remark, but analysts say it is unlikely to \b \highlight22\uc2 intervene\b0 \highlight\uc2  quickly, given a lack of U.S. support and because the euro so far has traded below its January all-time high just under $1.29.   \par \par \uc2  "The euro is strong, it has gained quite a bit, but I do not have the feeling the ECB would see a need to act, if this is where (the euro) will stay," said Julian von Landesberger, an economist at HVB Group in Munich.   \par \par \uc2  Only a swift rise from the current level above $1.28 could trigger ECB action, said von Landesberger, but the ECB would then cut rates rather than sell euros. The bank has \b \highlight22\uc2 intervened\b0 \highlight\uc2  in markets once, putting a bottom in the market around $0.84 in 2000, after a persistent euro decline since its introduction.   \par \par \uc2  JAPAN FEELS G7 SUPPORT   \par \par \uc2  Watanabe reiterated that the G7 statement after its meeting in Florida last weekend, which included a line on the undesirability of volatility, supported \b \highlight22\uc2 Japan\b0 \highlight\uc2 's stance on \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  rates.   \par \par \uc2  "Our \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  stance is the same after the G7 as it was before," he said. "We won't do anything meaningless, but overshooting and disorderly moves are not good for economic growth. To avoid these we will do what is needed when it is needed."   \par \par \uc2  Watanabe added that other reforms were needed for Japan to ultimately achieve its aim of a stable \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  reflecting economic fundamentals.   \par \par \uc2  "Looking ahead, it will be difficult to do something about \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2  just through \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2 ," he said, adding that as the G7 statement had said, structural reforms were important.   \par \par \uc2  Watanabe also weighed into the debate on China's yuan \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  reforms sparked by a Japanese newspaper report that China would revise its exchange rate mechanism this year.   \par \par \uc2  China's \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  chief Zhou Xiaochuan said in a statement on Thursday that the country would perfect its exchange rate system, which has come under attack from the United States and other countries because it pegs the yuan's exchange rate to the dollar.   \par \par \uc2  Japan's Nihon Keizai newspaper, in reporting the remarks, had said China plans changes this year, an interpretation Watanabe did not share.   \par \par \uc2  "I don't think you can read into the statement that China will do something this year," Watanabe said. "China is aware that the current exchange rate system is not the best and is studying the matter."   \par \par \uc2  A slow readjustment would be the best and cause least disruption to trade, he sad.   \par \par \uc2  (Additional reporting by Douwe Miedema, Frankfurt).   \par \par \uc2 Dokument LBA0000020040212e02c002sx\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw172\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2580\pichgoal600
