\par\par \b \uc2 OFF THE RUN: Greenspan Nonchalant On Foreign Central Bks  \b0\par\par\uc2 900 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 11 Februar 2004\par 21:40\par \uc2 Dow Jones International News\par \uc2 DJI\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 2004 Dow Jones & Company, Inc.  \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par    By Agnes T. Crane \par    A DOW JONES NEWSWIRES COLUMN \par    \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 NEW YORK (Dow Jones)--Foreign central banks have been big buyers of new Treasury issuance this week, helping to keep the U.S. interest-rate fueled consumer spending miracle alive.  \f28 \par \par \uc2 Yet, Federal Reserve Chairman Alan Greenspan downplayed that role Wednesday, raising eyebrows about the risks to the bond market in a week that official investors have snapped up over 40% of the $40 billion new notes sold in the government's quarterly refunding so far this week.  \par \par \uc2 Asian central banks, with the Bank of \b \highlight22\uc2 Japan\b0 \highlight\uc2  leading the charge, have \b \highlight22\uc2 intervened\b0 \highlight\uc2  aggressively in \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  markets in recent months in an attempt to stem the weakness of the dollar against their own \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2  and maintain their export competitiveness.  \par \par \uc2 These banks have largely recycled the dollar holdings from these \b \highlight22\uc2 operations\b0 \highlight\uc2  into the U.S. government securities market, lending a strong support to Treasurys at a time when other factors such as an exploding federal budget deficit and a pickup in U.S. economic growth weigh on prices.  \par \par \uc2 Yet when asked if a cessation of this type of activity would hurt the U.S. Treasury market, Greenspan chose to focus on the broad potential impact on U.S. assets. "I think that the concerns that have been expressed about real serious problems in our financial markets as a consequence of ending of \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  of that sort, I think those concerns are misplaced."  \par \par \uc2 Later in the session, he went on to say "people who are concerned about significant liquidations or changes in the investment policy on the part of the foreign central banks on interest rates on U.S. Treasury securities are, I think, more concerned than they should be."  \par \par \uc2 Greenspan noted that the foreign \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  purchases are concentrated in shorter-term securities which he said "can't fluctuate much so long as the maturity at par is a very short distance away." He said this would limit the broader effect on the bond market from a retreat by the foreign central banks.  \par \par \uc2 "I don't deny there will be adjustments, there always are when any large block of securities moves back and forth," the Fed chief said, but stressed it wouldn't cause disruptions in financial markets.  \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par      Not Everybody Agrees \par    \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 The rapid pickup in official purchases of government securities, however, has many Treasury market participants convinced that the \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currency interventions\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  are in fact keeping yields artificially low.  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Acting on behalf of the Japanese government, the Bank of Japan spent a record $67 billion in January to keep the yen from strengthening against the dollar, leading many to believe that it is behind the approximate $50 billion invested in government securities, including Treasurys, agencies and mortgages last month.  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 The Bank of Japan, which \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currency\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  market participants believe has been purchasing dollars daily since the Group of Seven industrialized nations essentially gave such \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 operations\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  the green light when they cautioned against "excess volatility" in \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 foreign exchange\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  rates, is also thought to be behind the strong demand for new issuance.  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 In the first two legs of Treasury's refunding auctions this week, foreign central banks bought a huge chunk of the new notes sold. According to Treasury, indirect bidders - largely made up of official foreign institutions - bought $11.1 billion worth of three-year notes, or 46% of the total sold. On Wednesday, the banks came back for more, lifting 42% of the $16 billion in five-year notes sold.  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Gerald Lucas, chief Treasury strategist at Banc of America Securities and someone who has been keeping close tabs on the foreign \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 central bank\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  purchases, said a stabilization of the dollar and subsequent cessation of \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 intervention\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  would not be good for Treasury prices.  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 "There's a price for everything, but it would definitely be detrimental to the Treasury market" if the \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 intervention\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  funds stopped flowing, he said.  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 He did agree with Greenspan that it would not necessarily disrupt other financial markets as a steady or stronger dollar, theoretically, would follow improved economic conditions in the U.S. and stronger prospects for growth. In this scenario, private investors would make up for the drop-off in \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 central bank\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  purchases, through their demand for U.S. assets such as corporate bonds and equities. They would not, however, be interested in Treasurys, which tend to lose value amid improving economic conditions.  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Michael Cheah, portfolio manager at AIG SunAmerica Asset Management who manages $2 billion in fixed income assets, characterized Greenspan's remarks as coming from a "very high macro perspective" in which he's assessing the financial markets as a whole rather than the fortunes of the Treasury market. Indeed, in the past Greenspan has pointed to the sophistication and diversity of U.S. capital markets as a reason why the U.S. can continue to finance its current account deficit.  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 What does appear certain is the Bank of Japan, and other central banks will continue to cap any rise in Treasury yields as they put their \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 intervention\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  dollars to work back in U.S. debt markets. Japanese manufactures have warned that a dollar value below Y105 will severely hamper the competitiveness of their products abroad. The dollar fetched Y105.38 in mid-afternoon trade Wednesday.  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 (Agnes T. Crane has covered credit markets for three years)  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par    -By Agnes T. Crane, Dow Jones Newswires, 201-938-2122; \par agnes.crane@dowjones.com \par    \f28 \par \par \uc2 Dokument DJI0000020040211e02b000u1\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw184\pich22\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2760\pichgoal330
