\par\par \b \uc2 Dollar Falls On Greenspan Comments; Intraday Lows Vs Euro  \b0\par\par\uc2 By Jamie McGeever and Tom Barkley  \par \uc2 Of DOW JONES NEWSWIRES  \par \uc2 1,056 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 11 Februar 2004\par 17:01\par \uc2 Dow Jones Capital Markets Report\par \uc2 CM\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 2004 Dow Jones & Company, Inc.  \par \par \uc2 NEW YORK (Dow Jones)--The dollar is falling sharply across the board Wednesday, reacting negatively to comments from Federal Reserve Chairman Alan Greenspan that the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2 's recent decline has been "gradual" and has had no "material side affects" on the U.S. economy.  \par \par \uc2 In congressional testimony, Greenspan also struck a slightly less hawkish tone on interest rates than many observers had expected, despite his more upbeat outlook for economic growth.  \par \par \uc2 "Although the \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  value of the dollar has fallen over the past year, the decline generally has been gradual, and no material adverse side effects have been visible in U.S. capital markets," Greenspan said in testimony prepared for the U.S. House of Representatives' Committee on Financial Services.  \par \par \uc2 This was enough to send the dollar plunging, as it indicates a lack of concern from the Fed about the potential impact of the weaker dollar on investment in the U.S.  \par \par \uc2 "The \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  market is reacting to his dovish comments on the exchange rate," said Tim Stewart, head of global \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  strategy at Morgan Stanley in New York.  \par \par \uc2 Steven Englander, chief \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  strategist for North America at Barclays Capital, said a combination of comments from Greenspan's speech is weighing on the dollar.  \par \par \uc2 "He's not saying anything particularly hawkish about the economy, and that's taking down yields," Englander said. "And it doesn't sound like the Fed is concerned about the dollar."  \par \par \uc2 Among the big gainers are sterling, which soared to a fresh 11-year high close to $1.8800, higher than the previous high of $1.8730 reached earlier Wednesday and the Australian dollar, which hit a fresh six and a half-year high of $0.7848.  \par \par \uc2 At 11:25 a.m. EST (1625 GMT), the euro was at $1.2775, up from $1.2675 moments before Greenspan's headlines hit the wires. Sterling was up a full cent at $1.8792, while the dollar fell to CHF1.2315 from CHF1.2410 and Y105.37 from Y105.65.  \par \par \uc2 The dollar also hit a fresh three-year low at about Y105.20, but bounced back almost immediately to Y105.37, still below Y105.65 just prior to Greenspan's remarks.  \par \par \uc2 Traders said they suspected that the Bank of \b \highlight22\uc2 Japan\b0 \highlight\uc2 , which \b \highlight22\uc2 intervenes\b0 \highlight\uc2  on behalf of \b \highlight22\uc2 Japan\b0 \highlight\uc2 's \b \highlight22\uc2 Ministry of Finance\b0 \highlight\uc2 , provided some covert support at the lows through Japanese agent banks.  \par \par \uc2 "People were playing with the BOJ and trying to see if they were really committed to capping the yen, and so far it looks like they have," said a market source.  \par \par \b \highlight22\uc2 Currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  market participants had been keenly awaiting Greenspan's views on the economy and more specifically signals on when the Fed will start to raise interest rates in response to strong economic growth.  \par \par \uc2 He gave an upbeat assessment of the economy, telling lawmakers that the odds of "sustained economic growth are good," while a separate Fed report raised the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2 's 2004 U.S. growth forecast to 4.5%-5.0% from an estimated 4.3% last year.  \par \par \uc2 But Greenspan pointed out that inflationary pressures are still weak, which will allow the Fed to remain "patient" in keeping its loose monetary policy and avoid rapid rate hikes.  \par \par \uc2 He also said the falling value of the dollar should ease the U.S. current account gap, which ballooned to $550 billion in 2003. He said the dollar's "adjustment" in \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  markets has been offset by \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  and continued demand for U.S. assets.  \par \par \uc2 This, according to Adam Cole, senior economist at Credit Agricole Indosuez in London, will be interpreted by the market as "Fed endorsement of the dollar fall and a green light for further selling."  \par \par \uc2 And the selling is gathering pace Wednesday, with the euro pushing up through $1.28 to a new month-high of $1.2821. This is within less than a cent of its lifetime high of $1.2898 and will surely test the resolve of the European \b \highlight22\uc2 Central Bank\b0 \highlight\uc2 , which last month warned against "brutal" moves in the euro.  \par \par \uc2 At 12:13 p.m. EST (1713 GMT), the euro was at $1.2815, up from $1.2675 moments before Greenspan's headlines hit the wires. Sterling surged more than a full cent at $1.8838, while the dollar fell to CHF1.2307 from CHF1.2410.  \par \par \uc2 The Group of Seven richest nations last weekend warned against "excess volatility and disorderly movements in exchange rates," another sign of European officials' discontent with the euro's appreciation.  \par \par \uc2 Having gone into the G7 meetings seeking more burden-sharing on the dollar's decline, "they're not going to be happy if the euro (rise) outpaces the yen again," said John McCarthy, director of \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  at ING Capital Markets in New York.  \par \par \uc2 But verbal \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  is one thing, actual \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  another. And \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  dealers and investors generally don't believe the ECB will \b \highlight22\uc2 intervene\b0 \highlight\uc2  selling euros, not before the single \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  rises as far as $1.30 anyway.  \par \par \uc2 By 12:30 p.m. EST (!&30 GMT), however, the dollar's bloodletting had eased as the market paused for breath after posting huge moves.  \par \par \uc2 With the dollar at these low levels, dealers short of the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  are taking profits and will also become more aware of the threat of \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  from central banks around the world keen to prevent their own \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2  from appreciating to fast too soon.  \par \par \uc2 Neal Kimberley, head of \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  sales at Bank of Tokyo Mitsubishi n London, said that in addition to the ECB and Reserve Bank of New Zealand, which have both expressed dissatisfaction recently with \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  market swings, the Reserve Bank of Australia might also start cranking up the rhetoric.  \par \par \uc2 But with the U.S. seemingly relaxed about the dollar - Treasury Secretary John Snow is an advocate of exchange rate "flexibility" and has also shrugged off the dollar's depreciation of recent months - there's growing skepticism over the likelihood of \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  and its effectiveness.  \par \par \uc2 "You've got to be buying euros on dips here," Kimberley said, adding that buying high-yielding \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2  such as sterling is a good way of trading dollar weakness.  \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par    -Jamie McGeever and Tom Barkley; Dow Jones Newswires; 201 938 2096; jamie.mcgeever@dowjones.com \par    \f28 \par \par \uc2 Dokument CM00000020040211e02b0001q\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw172\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2580\pichgoal600
