\par\par \b  \highlight22\uc2 FOREX \highlight\uc2 -Dollar drops on Greenspan current account remark.  \b0\par\par\uc2 By Kyle Peterson  \par \uc2 475 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 11 Februar 2004\par 17:40\par \uc2 Reuters News\par \uc2 LBA\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 2004 Reuters Limited  \par \par \uc2 CHICAGO, Feb 11 (Reuters) - The dollar fell against the euro on Wednesday after Federal Reserve Chairman Alan Greenspan said the U.S. \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2 's decline should help narrow the gaping U.S. current account gap.  \par \par \uc2 The Fed chief, in his semi-annual report on monetary policy to the U.S. House of Representatives Financial Services Committee, said the economy has turned the corner to vigorous growth but policymakers can be patient about raising interest rates.  \par \par \uc2 It was his comment about the dollar's impact on the current account gap, however, that sent the euro to a fresh session high against the dollar and sterling to a new 11-year high.  \par \par \uc2 A weaker dollar makes exports cheaper and imports more expensive, which should narrow the U.S. trade deficit, a major component of the current account.  \par \par \uc2 "That is the most direct comment that I've seen Greenspan make in a very long time," said Bob Priore, desk manager at Carr Futures in Chicago. "It takes the place of any G7 statement. This will force the hand of the European community to tell us what they really think."  \par \par \uc2 Last weekend, finance ministers from the Group of Seven major economic powers denounced excessive volatility in \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  markets as an obstacle to global growth. Many in the market took that statement as criticism of dollar weakness. But analysts said the ministers appeared unwilling to take aggressive action to prevent further weakness.  \par \par \uc2 Some economists think the Fed will raise rates from the current 1958 low of 1 percent around mid-year. Others think the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  will leave rates steady until after the November U.S. presidential election.  \par \par \uc2 The Fed sparked speculation of an earlier-than-expected rate hike in its last policy statement. But a weak January U.S. jobs report undermined that speculation, even though other data have shown the economic recovery is holding up.  \par \par \uc2 Yield-boosting rate rises are key to ending the dollar's two-year decline, especially after the G7 finance ministers made no promises to support the dollar. The G7 statement warned against "disorderly movements in exchange rates."  \par \par \uc2 In mid-morning U.S. trade, the euro was up 0.54 percent at $1.2757. The dollar fell 0.32 percent to 1.2313 Swiss francs.  \par \par \uc2 The dollar was down 0.09 percent at 105.39 yen. The dollar has been lingering just above three-year lows against the yen, helped by expectations of Bank of \b \highlight22\uc2 Japan\b0 \highlight\uc2  yen-selling \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2 .  \par \par \uc2 Sterling was up 0.44 percent to $1.8788.  \par \par \uc2 Lawmakers were likely to grill the Fed chief during a question-and-answer session on U.S. fiscal policy.  \par \par \uc2 The Bush administration has forecast a record $521 billion deficit this year, adding to the dollar's woes, but has vowed to halve that by 2009.   \par \par \uc2 Dokument LBA0000020040211e02b003vb\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw172\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2580\pichgoal600
