\par\par \b  \highlight22\uc2 FOREX \highlight\uc2 -Dollar steady ahead of Greenspan testimony.  \b0\par\par\uc2 By Kyle Peterson  \par \uc2 588 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 11 Februar 2004\par 15:21\par \uc2 Reuters News\par \uc2 LBA\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 2004 Reuters Limited  \par \par \uc2 (Recasts, adds comment, updates prices, changes byline, dateline; previous LONDON)  \par \par \uc2 CHICAGO, Feb 11 (Reuters) - The dollar hunkered in tight ranges against major \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2  on Wednesday as markets awaited clues from Federal Reserve Chairman Alan Greenspan on the timing of a potential U.S. interest rate increase.  \par \par \uc2 The Fed chief is scheduled to deliver his semiannual report on monetary policy to the U.S. House of Representatives' Financial Services Committee at 11 a.m. EST (1600 GMT). Analysts believe Greenspan will offer a bright economic outlook, while underscoring the Fed's willingness to be patient in deciding when to lift rates from the current 1 percent.  \par \par \uc2 Some economists think the Fed will raise rates around mid-year. Others think the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  will leave rates steady until after the November U.S. presidential election.  \par \par \uc2 "I presume just given the recent economic developments ... that the market is anticipating a relatively dovish leaning from Greenspan," said Robert Lynch, senior \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  strategist at BNP Paribas in New York. "If that is the case, that's potentially a little bit negative for the dollar. (But) the worst of that is priced in."  \par \par \uc2 The Fed sparked speculation of an earlier-than-expected rate hike in its last policy statement. But a weak January U.S. jobs report undermined that speculation, even though other data have shown the economic recovery is holding up.  \par \par \uc2 Yield-boosting rate rises are key to ending the dollar's two-year decline, especially after finance ministers from the Group of Seven industrialized nations made no promises at a weekend meeting to support the dollar. The G7 warned against "disorderly movements in exchange rates."  \par \par \uc2 In early U.S. trade the euro was 0.17 percent lower at $1.2665. The dollar gained 0.49 percent to 1.2416 Swiss francs.  \par \par \uc2 The dollar was up 0.11 percent at 105.56 yen. The dollar has been lingering just north of three-year lows against the yen, helped by expectations of Bank of \b \highlight22\uc2 Japan\b0 \highlight\uc2  yen-selling \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2 .  \par \par \uc2 Sterling dipped 0.03 percent to $1.8701 just below the previous session's 11-year high of $1.8734.  \par \par \uc2 In a quarterly report, the Bank of England said it expected inflation to pick up sharply this year, spurring expectations for a British rate hike.  \par \par \uc2 GREENSPAN IN VIEW  \par \par \uc2 Analysts expect Greenspan to assure lawmakers that inflation is unlikely to flare up soon, given a weak job market and lots of spare productive capacity.  \par \par \uc2 They said that while dollar weakness was a function of foreign investors' unwillingness to fund the U.S. current account deficit, the dollar could still get a short-term boost if Greenspan gave a robust outlook for the economy.  \par \par \uc2 "Although we are still in a structurally bearish trend, and the G7 outcome obviously reinforced that, we are perhaps looking at a pause in the long dollar decline as Greenspan delivers a relatively upbeat testimony," said Adam Cole, senior \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  strategist at Credit Agricole Indosuez in London.  \par \par \uc2 "Equally there is probably a risk that he talks about some return of pricing power to the corporate sector in the U.S., i.e. further diminishing deflation fears. Again, that should be relatively dollar supportive."  \par \par \uc2 Lawmakers are also likely to take the opportunity to grill the Fed chief on U.S. fiscal policy.  \par \par \uc2 The Bush administration has forecast a record $521 billion deficit this year, adding to the dollar's woes, but has vowed to halve that by 2009. (Additional reporting by Justyna Pawlak in London).   \par \par \uc2 Dokument LBA0000020040211e02b003g2\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw172\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2580\pichgoal600
