\par\par \b \uc2 INTERVIEW:Korea Govt/ \highlight22\uc2 FX \highlight\uc2  Stresses No Change In Policy  \b0\par\par\uc2 586 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 10 Februar 2004\par 04:51\par \uc2 Dow Jones International News\par \uc2 DJI\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 2004 Dow Jones & Company, Inc.  \par \par \uc2 SEOUL (Dow Jones)--The South Korean government will be more patient in its actions to stem the volatility in the won as it's confident the dollar's current weakness will be short lived, according to an official at the \b \highlight22\uc2 Ministry of Finance\b0 \highlight\uc2  and Economy Tuesday.  \par \par \uc2 Seoul's recently introduced restrictions on trading in the nondeliverable forwards market and the post-Group of Seven rise in the dollar-yen will play a part in re-energizing the U.S. unit in the domestic \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  market, the official said Tuesday.  \par \par \uc2 "We are firm in our stance that the dollar-won exchange rate should help sustain exports until a recovery in domestic consumption. The strategy remains the same, but you could say we can change the tactics a little bit," the official who declined to be named told Dow Jones Newswires.  \par \par \uc2 "We don't need to scurry to rescue every time the dollar falls. We now have room to be more relaxed in operating \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  policy," he said.  \par \par \uc2 His comments indicate the government won't likely resort to aggressive defense of the dollar Tuesday even as the won jumped to a four-month high on offshore fixing dollar sales related to tne NDF market and equity-related selling.  \par \par \uc2 At 0349 GMT, the dollar was at 1,162.3 won, down from Monday's close of KRW1,166.3 and its lowest since ending at a three-year low of KRW1,147.2 Oct. 13.  \par \par \uc2 Greater government tolerance for the won's rise Tuesday is in stark contrast to its recent actions to defend the dollar, which have included direct dollar buying and verbal \b \highlight22\uc2 interventions\b0 \highlight\uc2  since last month.  \par \par \uc2 But the official said the standoffishness could translate into more "effective" government action in the \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  market.  \par \par \uc2 "Let's say we will sacrifice small battles to concentrate our energy on the bigger war," he said.  \par \par \uc2 His remarks come after a series of government measures to reverse the won's strength failed.  \par \par \uc2 In January the government introduced a limit on offshore investors' bets on a stronger won. Korean banks cannot buy dollars for won from offshore players in the NDF market that are greater than 10% of their purchases made mid-January, regardless of their long or short positions. Penalties will be applied after Feb. 19.  \par \par \uc2 "The NDF regulations will be powerful as there is now a limit to dollar selling," he said.  \par \par \b \highlight22\uc2 Japan\b0 \highlight\uc2 's escape from criticism over its \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  policy at the G7 meeting also lends some weight to Seoul's \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  policy, which is aimed at keeping the won weak to support exporters.  \par \par \uc2 The G7 meeting in Florida criticized "excess volatility and disorderly movements" in the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  markets and sought "more flexibility in exchange rates" from economies "that lack such flexibility."  \par \par \uc2 "Japanese authorities look very pleased about the G7 result. They virtually received de facto approval from other G7 members to go on with \b \highlight22\uc2 interventions\b0 \highlight\uc2 ," the official said.  \par \par \uc2 "There is no reason for the won to rise more than the yen," he said. The dollar is currently at Y105.60, up from its intraday low of Y105.58.  \par \par \uc2 He also predicted that the U.S. government cannot afford to maintain a weak-dollar policy for long as higher raw material prices spurred by a soft dollar could generate inflation in the U.S. ahead of the presidential election.  \par \par \uc2 -By Hye-Seung Seo, Dow Jones Newswires; 822-732-2165; hyeseung.seo@dowjones.com  \par \par \uc2 -Edited by Simon Harvey  \par \par \uc2 Dokument DJI0000020040210e02a0005l\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw184\pich22\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2760\pichgoal330
