\par\par \b \uc2 Japan MOF Mizoguchi:No Change To Japan  \highlight22\uc2 FX \highlight\uc2  Stance Post G7  \b0\par\par\uc2 368 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 10 Februar 2004\par 01:29\par \uc2 Dow Jones International News\par \uc2 DJI\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 2004 Dow Jones & Company, Inc.  \par \par \uc2 TOKYO (Dow Jones)--Japan will stick to its policy of fighting sharp exchange rate moves following the weekend Group of Seven meeting, the \b \highlight22\uc2 Ministry of Finance\b0 \highlight\uc2 's \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  pointman said Tuesday, adding he believed the G7's call for more-flexible exchange rates wasn't aimed at Tokyo.  \par \par \uc2 "There is no change from our previous stance," Vice-Minister for International Affairs Zembei Mizoguchi told reporters.  \par \par \uc2 Mizoguchi declined to comment on the market's mild reaction to the G7 joint statement, which added a warning against "disorderly" exchange rate movements to its previous call for "flexibility."  \par \par \uc2 At 0040 GMT the dollar was trading at Y105.81, up slightly from Y105.70 in New York late Monday and Y105.45 late Friday.  \par \par \uc2 In addition to cautioning against excessive volatility, the G7 narrowed the focus of its call for more-flexible rates, which the group initially made at its previous finance chiefs' meeting in September.  \par \par \uc2 This time the G7 specified that it was seeking flexibility from economies "that lack such flexibility" - a thinly-veiled reference to China and other Asian countries that keep their \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2  closely tied to the dollar.  \par \par \uc2 Despite dissatisfaction in Europe and elsewhere over Tokyo's massive market \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  to curb the yen's rise, Japanese Finance Minister Sadakazu Tanigaki said immediately following the G7 talks that Japan was not one of the countries that the G7 thinks keeps too tight a grip on its \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2 .  \par \par \uc2 Mizoguchi echoed that view Tuesday, saying, "It's just as the minister said."  \par \par \uc2 The remarks suggest Tokyo feels it has a free hand to use \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  to fight sharp rises in the yen, which it worries could derail \b \highlight22\uc2 Japan\b0 \highlight\uc2 's export-led economic turnaround.  \par \par \b \highlight22\uc2 Japan\b0 \highlight\uc2 's yen-selling \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  totaled Y20 trillion last year and a record Y7.15 trillion in January alone.  \par \par \uc2 European authorities complain that by keeping a lid on the yen, Japan has forced the euro to absorb more of the dollar's broad-based decline, accelerating the single \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2 's rise against the dollar.  \par \par \uc2 -By Jonathan Soble, Dow Jones Newswires; 813-5255-2929; jonathan.soble@dowjones.com  \par \par \uc2 -Edited by John Gibson, Alastair Gale and Kirsty Mackenzie  \par \par \uc2 Dokument DJI0000020040210e02a0000m\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw172\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2580\pichgoal600
