\par\par \b \uc2 Dollar bears rush to UK, Aussie for less risky gains.  \b0\par\par\uc2 By Justyna Pawlak  \par \uc2 740 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 10 Februar 2004\par 16:33\par \uc2 Reuters News\par \uc2 LBA\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 2004 Reuters Limited  \par \par \uc2 LONDON, Feb 10 (Reuters) - Want to sell the dollar, now the Group of Seven meeting is over, but too afraid that euro zone policymakers will try to stall its descent against the euro?  \par \par \uc2 Then buy high-yielding \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2  such as the British pound and the Australian dollar, analysts say, since their gains are unlikely to face any aggressive official resistance while still earning the additional return of high interest rates.  \par \par \uc2 Sterling shot up this week to new 11-year highs versus the dollar, gaining 1.5 percent to $1.8734 since G7 finance ministers failed to offer the falling U.S. \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  any strong support when they met in Florida last weekend.  \par \par \uc2 The Australian dollar surged to its highest level since May 1997 at US$0.7832 on Tuesday.  \par \par \uc2 And analysts say there are more gains to come.  \par \par \uc2 "There is a lot of disappointment for dollar bulls after the G7. Given that dollar/yen is not going anywhere you have to express dollar weakness against \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2  that will move," said Paul Mackel, \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  strategist at ABN AMRO in London.  \par \par \uc2 "There is policy risk in euro/dollar, not in the sense that they (European \b \highlight22\uc2 Central Bank\b0 \highlight\uc2 ) will \b \highlight22\uc2 intervene\b0 \highlight\uc2  but they are always talking about it... Why not hold other \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2  so that you do not have such comments popping up on the screens all the time?" European and Japanese officials have voiced concern about the dollar's weakness against their \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2 , which can hurt their export competitiveness, making markets nervous.  \par \par \b \highlight22\uc2 Japan\b0 \highlight\uc2  is widely expected to continue \b \highlight22\uc2 intervening\b0 \highlight\uc2  heavily to limit any yen gains versus the dollar.  \par \par \uc2 Europe is still expected to refrain from \b \highlight22\uc2 currency intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  but the constant rumble of concern about the euro's rise is taking a toll on euro bulls, analysts say.  \par \par \uc2 This leaves some of the high yielding majors as the \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2  in which gains may be made most easily.  \par \par \uc2 NONCHALANT POLITICIANS  \par \par \uc2 Unlike the euro, sterling's rise on the dollar has drawn little response from politicians in Britain. The UK conducts 60 percent of its trade with the European Union and 15 percent with the United States, meaning a weak dollar has little impact on its export competitiveness.  \par \par \uc2 "The market likes sterling as a good vehicle to sell dollars since it has low policy risk. Cable looks ripe for testing $1.90," said Mackel.  \par \par \uc2 Similarly in Australia the risk of aggressive political outcry about a rising Aussie is low, even though some politicians may express worries ahead of elections later in 2004.  \par \par \uc2 "Markets are accustomed to the fact the Aussie dollar is free floating...and \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  talk is not on the horizon," said Tim Fox, strategist at National Australia Bank in London.  \par \par \uc2 "Politicians may get nervous about Aussie dollar strength but there doesn't seem to be any expression of intent regarding \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2 ."  \par \par \uc2 Analysts also said anecdotal evidence points to \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2 -supportive demand for Australian dollar bonds.  \par \par \uc2 Demand is largely driven by prospects of higher yields, which got a boost on Monday after the Reserve Bank of Australia affirmed a tightening bias in its quarterly policy statement.  \par \par \uc2 The RBA's official interest rate is 5.25 percent and Britain's four percent, while the Federal Reserve's main rate is one percent and the ECB's two percent.  \par \par \uc2 "People want to sell the dollar, the G7 has changed nothing. Then they see sterling - a major \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  having just hiked rates," said Peter Fontaine, \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  strategist at KBC in Brussels.  \par \par \uc2 "A few weeks ago you had Bank of England Governor King saying he was not worried about sterling strength. So it is a path of least resistance."  \par \par \uc2 Chartists back the positive fundamental views with evidence of technical support.  \par \par \uc2 "While the pound remains above $1.8500 its uptrend is healthy," Barclays Capital said, noting the next sterling/dollar target is $1.8925.  \par \par \uc2 Meanwhile, Tuesday's break of last month's 6 1/2-year high in the Aussie dollar signalled that a range-trading phase was over and the medium-term uptrend had resumed, Barclays said.  \par \par \uc2 "You can participate in the Asian growth story through the Aussie. There is some indication the market is long but maybe it will still test US$0.8210," said Mackel at ABN.  \par \par \uc2 "High yielders are definitely an attractive opportunity now rather than just being long euros. You need to diversify and why not get paid by holding them."   \par \par \uc2 Dokument LBA0000020040210e02a0025k\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw172\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2580\pichgoal600
