\par\par \b \uc2 Dollar hits 11-yr low on pound in G7 aftermath.  \b0\par\par\uc2 By Hideyuki Sano  \par \uc2 614 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 10 Februar 2004\par 08:30\par \uc2 Reuters News\par \uc2 LBA\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 2004 Reuters Limited  \par \par \uc2 TOKYO, Feb 10 (Reuters) - The dollar slid to an 11-year low against the pound and fell within sight of a two-week low versus the euro on Tuesday as the market concluded the Group of Seven nations were unlikely to jointly \b \highlight22\uc2 intervene\b0 \highlight\uc2  to halt its decline.  \par \par \uc2 Although G7 financial chiefs warned against excessive volatility in exchange rates at their weekend meeting, traders think the chance of joint dollar-selling \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  by the G7 is close to zero. Many believe Washington is content with the dollar's depreciation, which makes U.S. exporters competitive.  \par \par \uc2 "Unlike after the Plaza or Louvre accords, the G7 nations are unlikely to conduct joint \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2 ," said Fumihiko Kawano, a \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  manager at Nomura Securities.  \par \par \uc2 "So the market is shifting its focus back to fundamentals such as the U.S. current account deficit and low interest rates," he added.  \par \par \uc2 The pound spiked to a new 11-year high of $1.8665, a rise of 0.4 percent on the day and nearly 0.9 percent after the G7 meeting.  \par \par \uc2 The euro also edged up about 0.4 percent to around $1.2750, closing in on its two-week high of $1.2765 hit on Monday.  \par \par \uc2 The dollar's fall against the yen was smaller, due to wariness about \b \highlight22\uc2 Japan\b0 \highlight\uc2 's yen-selling \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2 . The greenback stood around 105.45 yen, down 0.2 percent on the day.  \par \par \uc2 Japan sold a record 20 trillion yen ($189.2 billion) in \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  last year to curb the yen's rise, and a further seven trillion yen last month.  \par \par \uc2 Japanese officials on Tuesday expressed satisfaction with the G7's warning against excessive volatility in \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  markets. The dollar fell sharply after the previous G7 meeting in September, when the group's call for flexibility in \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2  was interpreted as a criticism of \b \highlight22\uc2 Japan\b0 \highlight\uc2 's \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2 .  \par \par \uc2 Finance Minister Sadakazu Tanigaki said G7 countries had made great efforts to make sure their final statement on \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2  would not cause misunderstanding in the financial markets.  \par \par \uc2 "The Japanese government appears to think it won G7 endorsement for its \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2 ," said Kota Kimura, an assistant manager at Shinkin \b \highlight22\uc2 Central Bank\b0 \highlight\uc2 . "So I'm sure the authorities are full of confidence right now. And few players are willing to take (yen-long) positions."  \par \par \uc2 RATE OUTLOOK  \par \par \uc2 Traders expect the dollar, already falling against major \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2  for two years, to continue to weaken as investors shy away from U.S. assets on worries that a substantial fall in the dollar may be needed to reduce the United States' huge current account deficit.  \par \par \uc2 The spectre of continued low interest rates in the United States is also weighing on the dollar, especially against high-yielding \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2  such as sterling and the Australian dollar.  \par \par \uc2 The Australian dollar rose to around 78 U.S. cents, nearing its six-and-a-half year high of 78.13 hit last month.  \par \par \uc2 The expectations for low U.S yields could change if Federal Reserve Chairman Alan Greenspan hints at credit tightening in the future at semiannual testimony on the economy before a House of Representatives panel on Wednesday.  \par \par \uc2 "If he talks about raising interest rates, that'll put a brake on the dollar's fall," said a senior manager at the \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  and treasury division at Bank of Tokyo-Mitsubishi.  \par \par \uc2 "But with the (presidential election) coming up, I don't think that's very likely," he said.  \par \par \uc2 Many traders are now expecting the euro to test the record high of around $1.29 it hit last month, possibly even to reach $1.30 before European policy-makers run out of patience and \b \highlight22\uc2 intervene\b0 \highlight\uc2 .   \par \par \uc2 Dokument LBA0000020040210e02a0013s\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw172\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2580\pichgoal600
