\par\par \b \uc2 Dollar hits 11-yr low vs pound on lack of G7 help.   \b0\par\par\uc2 By Hideyuki Sano   \par \uc2 595 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 10 Februar 2004\par 07:02\par \uc2 Reuters News\par \uc2 LBA\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 2004 Reuters Limited   \par \par \uc2 TOKYO, Feb 10 (Reuters) - The dollar hit an 11-year low against the pound on Tuesday as the market acted on the G7's failure to agree coordinated action to stem its decline.    \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par The pound spiked to a new 11-year high of $1.8635  while the euro edged\par up about 0.4 percent to around $1.2735       . It hit a two-week high\par of $1.2765 on Monday on a perception that the Group of Seven nations\par are unlikely to make any joint efforts to curb the dollar's fall\par against the euro.\par    \f28 \par \par \uc2 Against the yen, however, the dollar drew support from wariness about \b \highlight22\uc2 Japan\b0 \highlight\uc2 's yen-selling \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  as Japanese officials expressed satisfaction with a statement issued after the weekend meeting of G7 finance ministers and central bankers warning against excessive volatility in \b \highlight22\uc2 forex\b0 \highlight\uc2  markets.   \par \par \uc2 "The Japanese government appears to think it won G7 endorsement for its \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2 ," said Kota Kimura, assistant manager at Shinkin \b \highlight22\uc2 Central Bank\b0 \highlight\uc2 . "So I'm sure the authorities are full of confidence right now. And few players are willing to take (yen-long) positions."   \par \par \uc2 Japan sold a record 20 trillion yen ($189.2 billion) in \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  last year to curb the yen's rise, and a further seven trillion yen last month.   \par \par \uc2 Japanese Finance Minister Sadakazu Tanigaki said on Tuesday that G7 countries had made great efforts to make sure their final statement on \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2  would not cause misunderstandings in financial markets.   \par \par \uc2 "We would like the communique to be read without misunderstandings," Tanigaki told reporters.   \par \par \uc2 "We paid great attention to making sure the communique accurately reflected our views, but of course we can't be sure how the market will take it."   \par \par \uc2 As of 0300 GMT, the dollar held steady from late New York levels around 105.70 yen .   \par \par \uc2 G7 STATEMENT   \par \par \uc2 The G7's "please all" statement, which included both a warning against excess volatility in \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  markets and a call for more flexibility in exchange rates, left room for the market to ponder.   \par \par \uc2 "I think the G7's message was that they are happy with the current \b \highlight22\uc2 forex\b0 \highlight\uc2  levels but they don't want to see a further rapid decline in the dollar," said Satoshi Tokuda, \b \highlight22\uc2 forex\b0 \highlight\uc2  manager at Sumitomo Corp.   \par \par \uc2 Although euro zone finance ministers said after they met on Monday that they did not discuss \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  in \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  markets, some think European policy-makers' patience may soon run out as the euro soars.   \par \par \uc2 "Some people are considering the chance of European \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2 ," said Kimura of Shinkin Bank. "So they are buying the British pound and the Australian dollar instead."   \par \par \uc2 Others think the dollar will continue to fall given the huge U.S. current account deficit and the prospect of continued low interest rates in the United States.   \par \par \uc2 "The G7 didn't provide any clear direction. In other words, the gradual decline in the dollar is likely to continue," said Kenji Kobayashi, a senior manager at the \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  and treasury division at Bank of Tokyo-Mitsubishi.   \par \par \uc2 With the G7 meeting out of the way, the market's focus was shifting to Federal Reserve Chairman Alan Greenspan's semiannual testimony on the economy before a House of Representatives panel on Wednesday.   \par \par \uc2 He is also scheduled to appear before the Senate Banking Committee on Thursday.   \par \par \uc2 "If he talks about raising interest rates, that'll put a brake on the dollar's fall. But with the (presidential election) coming up, I don't think that's very likely," said Tokyo-Mitsubishi's Kobayashi.    \par \par \uc2 Dokument LBA0000020040210e02a000wq\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw172\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2580\pichgoal600
