\par\par \b \uc2 G7 pact won't halt slow dlr fall-ex-MOF Sakakibara.   \b0\par\par\uc2 By Mari Terawaki and Tamawa Kadoya   \par \uc2 707 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 10 Februar 2004\par 06:08\par \uc2 Reuters News\par \uc2 LBA\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 2004 Reuters Limited   \par \par \uc2 TOKYO, Feb 10 (Reuters) - A G7 statement warning against excessive exchange rate volatility will do little to change the trend of a gradual decline in the dollar, a former top Japanese financial diplomat said on Tuesday.   \par \par \b \highlight22\uc2 Japan\b0 \highlight\uc2  also needed to reconsider its \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  stance to more effectively curb the yen's rise, allowing the market at some point to take its own course, said Eisuke Sakakibara, former vice finance minister for international affairs.   \par \par \uc2 "The (G7) communique is deliberately vague but well-done given that everyone holds divergent views. It has not caused confusion in the market, so I think everyone is satisfied," he told Reuters in an interview.   \par \par \uc2 But it would not reverse the trend set before the G7 meeting. "It merely confirmed the current situation."   \par \par \uc2 In addition to their standard line that rates should reflect fundamentals, Group of Seven finance ministers and \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  governors issued a communique at a weekend meeting in Florida saying: "Excess volatility and disorderly movements in exchange rates are undesirable for economic growth."   \par \par \uc2 Sakakibara, now a professor at Tokyo's Keio University, said it showed the G7 was in agreement that a rapid decline in the dollar after the previous G7 meeting in Dubai was undesirable, although G7 nations still differed in their views on \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2 .   \par \par \uc2 "The United States seeks a gradual fall in the dollar. The Europeans think it's a bit too fast. Japan does not want a dollar decline," he said.   \par \par \uc2 The G7 also called for \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  flexibility by countries "that lack such flexibility".   \par \par \uc2 Sakakibara said that was aimed at China and other Asian nations, and for Japan it was a "delicate situation".   \par \par \uc2 "Japan says it is not a target, the others are not commenting. I think that was what was agreed upon," he said.   \par \par \uc2 On \b \highlight22\uc2 Japan\b0 \highlight\uc2 's policy of actively \b \highlight22\uc2 intervening\b0 \highlight\uc2  in the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  market, Sakakibara said Europe remained critical, while the United States was tolerant as long as it was "defensive" and not aimed at pushing up the dollar.   \par \par \uc2 EXIT POLICY   \par \par \uc2 Dubbed "Mr Yen" for his influence in \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  markets, Sakakibara himself orchestrated massive \b \highlight22\uc2 currency intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  during his tenure, first to stem a fall in the yen in 1998 and then to halt the yen's rise the following year. Sakakibara defended Japan's huge yen-selling in January, saying the dollar would have fallen below 100 yen had the authorities not \b \highlight22\uc2 intervened\b0 \highlight\uc2 . \b \highlight22\uc2 Japan\b0 \highlight\uc2  sold over seven trillion yen ($66 billion) in the market in January to curb the yen's rise, a record for a single month.   \par \par \uc2 Last year it sold a record 20.4250 trillion yen. The previous yearly record was 7.6411 trillion yen in 1999.   \par \par \uc2 But Sakakibara said the policy could not be sustained.   \par \par \uc2 "The current \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  policy is abnormal and the authorities know that," he said. "I think they need to think over the next three to six months about exiting such a policy."   \par \par \b \highlight22\uc2 Currency intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  is conducted by the Bank of \b \highlight22\uc2 Japan\b0 \highlight\uc2 , which acts as the government's agent in the market. Policy is strictly controlled by the \b \highlight22\uc2 Ministry of Finance\b0 \highlight\uc2 .   \par \par \uc2 "It will lead to major distortions," he said, pointing out that Japan's foreign reserves ballooned to a record $741.246 billion at the end of January.   \par \par \uc2 Asked what he would do in the current situation, Sakakibara said he would let the market take its own course at some point, although that would not necessarily rule out \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2 .   \par \par \uc2 "The current \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  is not working. Authorities need to get the market on their side and there needs to be an element of surprise," he said, adding he did not think the dollar would slide sharply beyond 90 yen even if authorities let it fall.   \par \par \uc2 Most major Japanese companies could withstand the dollar at around 100 yen, he added.   \par \par \uc2 On Tuesday in Tokyo the dollar hit an 11-year low against the pound and fell within sight of a two-week low versus the euro as the market acted on what it saw as a lack of agreement by the G7 on coordinated action to stem its decline.   \par \par \uc2 But it was was little changed from before the G7 meeting at around 105.60 yen.    \par \par \uc2 Dokument LBA0000020040210e02a000pt\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw172\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2580\pichgoal600
