\par\par \b \uc2 Dollar Weakness Resumes As Market Unfazed By G7 Warning  \b0\par\par\uc2 By Adriana Arai  \par \uc2 Of DOW JONES NEWSWIRES  \par \uc2 747 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 9 Februar 2004\par 12:59\par \uc2 Dow Jones Emerging Markets Report\par \uc2 EM\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 2004 Dow Jones & Company, Inc.  \par \par \uc2 LONDON (Dow Jones)--Investors are continuing to sell the dollar Monday after a weekend meeting of policymakers from the world's richest nations produced no expressions of discomfort over the U.S. \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2 's weak level.  \par \par \uc2 While top economic officials from the Group of Seven leading industrialized nations warned in a joint statement Saturday that "excess volatility and disorderly movements in exchange rates are undesirable for economic growth," they made no reference to \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  valuation.  \par \par \uc2 They added the G7 "would continue to monitor exchange markets closely and cooperate as appropriate" and reiterated a call for "flexibility" in exchange rates.  \par \par \uc2 It was yet another vaguely worded statement that made no direct threat to \b \highlight22\uc2 intervene\b0 \highlight\uc2  to stop the dollar from falling further. Unlike the 1985 Plaza Accord, which launched a concerted effort to weaken a strong dollar, the communique from the meeting in Boca Raton, Fla., wasn't specific enough to have a real impact on \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  trading.  \par \par \uc2 Market players have interpreted the communique to mean that G7 officials would, in fact, be untroubled if the dollar, already down 30% since 2002 versus its main rivals, declines even further - as long as it's not too fast.  \par \par \uc2 "G7 still wants the dollar to fall. All that has changed is that it does not want the dollar to fall in a volatile way," wrote analysts with Bear Stearns in a Monday report.  \par \par \uc2 As global trading got underway Monday in Asia, the G7 outcome initially sent the dollar as high as Y105.87, from Y105.45 Friday afternoon in New York, as some investors were impressed by the warning against excess volatility. The euro dropped to a low of $1.2575 from $1.2697.  \par \par \uc2 But it didn't take long for markets to realize the G7 statement brought no real news, and investors resumed selling the greenback. By around 1130 GMT, the dollar had slipped to Y105.64 while the euro had bounced to $1.2746.  \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par            Consensus Has Dollar Falling Further \par    \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 The consensus market view remains that the dollar is set to drop further against the euro and the yen in the coming months, or at least until the Federal Reserve raises interest rates. A rate cut by the European \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 Central Bank\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2 , for its part, would put a drag on the euro, but ECB President Jean-Claude Trichet suggested Saturday such a move is not in the cards.  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 In \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 Japan\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2 , authorities are expected to continue \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 intervening\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  in the market to smooth the appreciation of the yen. Even though the G7 communique called for flexibility in \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currency\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  regimes, analysts think the statement is mostly targeted at China and South Korea, since Japanese \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 intervention\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  has not been enough to stop the yen from gradually rising.  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 The introduction of the warning against excess \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currency\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  volatility in the G7 communique follows European demands for a coordinated verbal effort to stem the dollar's sharp decline against the euro, which has risen 20% in the past year alone and is slowing Europe's economic recovery.  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Spearheaded by Trichet, European policymakers had embarked on a campaign to talk the euro down a few weeks before the G7 meeting in Boca Raton.  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Many economists saw the volatility warning as a European victory, because it meant that they had managed to persuade U.S. officials to risk slowing a dollar depreciation that has boosted U.S. corporate earnings and helped job creation in an election year.  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Yet large \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currency\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  risks may be looming in the euro zone.  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Shahab Jalinoos, a senior \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currency\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  strategist with ABN Amro in London, thinks European policymakers have made a mistake by preempting the G7 statement with verbal \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 intervention\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  before the meeting. He argues the G7 would have had a greater impact on the market had Trichet and his colleagues stayed quiet and let the communique first bring up official concern against abrupt moves in \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currencies\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2 .  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 This is not yet the consensus view, but it makes sense: The risk is that investors who pared positions ahead of the G7 meeting because of Trichet's warnings may now feel encouraged to challenge Europe's resolve in curbing the euro's rise, now that Europe seems to have played its full hand.  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 "Europe overplayed," said Jalinoos. "They have shown the market their limits, and now the market will feel encouraged to take them on," he said.  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 -By Adriana Arai, Dow Jones Newswires, 44 207 842 9483 adriana.arai@dowjones.com  \f28 \par \par \uc2 Dokument EM00000020040209e0290000g\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw200\pich25\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal3000\pichgoal375
