\par\par \b \uc2 GLOBAL YIELD: Second-Guessing G7 Gives Edge To US Bonds  \b0\par\par\uc2 930 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 9 Februar 2004\par 22:11\par \uc2 Dow Jones International News\par \uc2 DJI\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 2004 Dow Jones & Company, Inc.  \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par    By Brian Blackstone \par    A DOW JONES NEWSWIRES COLUMN \par    \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 NEW YORK (Dow Jones)--Euro-zone officials seemed to score a victory at the latest Group of Seven meetings by securing a warning against volatile \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currency\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  moves.  \f28 \par \par \uc2 That's until financial markets second-guessed them on Monday, giving the nod to the U.S., whose bond market and economy continue to reap the rewards of a weak dollar.  \par \par \uc2 By continuing to exert downward pressure on the U.S. \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2 , investors ensured that foreign-central banks will remain heavy buyers of U.S. Treasurys, meaning the U.S. gets a three-legged recovery with a soaring budget deficit stimulating growth, a weak \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  that spurs exports and low market interest rates that keep the consumer humming.  \par \par \uc2 "The U.S. is definitely winning - no doubt about it," said Richard Gilhooly, senior bond strategist at BNP Paribas in New York.  \par \par \uc2 That wasn't expected to be the reaction after finance ministers added a wrinkle to a communique following weekend meetings in Florida.  \par \par \uc2 They stated, in what appeared to address European complaints about the magnitude of the euro's rise against the dollar, that "excess volatility and disorderly movements in exchange rates are undesirable for economic growth."  \par \par \uc2 And if successful, that call would likely have been a negative for Treasurys and disrupt the delicate balance of \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  and bond-market forces that are expected to fuel 4% to 4.5% gross domestic product growth in the U.S. - twice the rates hoped for in Japan and Europe.  \par \par \uc2 Yet the dollar saw no reprieve, and fetched close to historic lows against the euro and three-and-a-half year lows against the yen Monday, ensuring that the U.S.'s virtuous dollar and fixed-income cycle will continue.  \par \par \uc2 The benchmark 10-year Treasury yield fell three basis points Monday, and it fetched eight basis points below its euro-zone counterpart despite growth and monetary policy factors that would seem to favor European fixed income.  \par \par \uc2 "I think people have been trying to buy (German) bunds versus Treasurys" on the theory that economic fundamentals favor European debt, but "it seems that the outperformance is coming from countries with looser monetary policy," said Gilhooly.  \par \par \uc2 The G7 communique, which also stressed the importance of flexible \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  markets, was "an indication that (Asian central banks) should continue (to buy Treasurys), and it helps hold down yields," Gilhooly added.  \par \par \uc2 The Bank of Japan spent a record $67 billion to support the dollar last month in order to shield its export-dependent economy. Much of those proceeds are recycled into Treasurys.  \par \par \uc2 Foreign central banks "are the biggest marginal buyer (of Treasurys), and in many cases it's the marginal buyer that has the swing vote in prices," said Robert Gay, global strategist at Commerzbank Securities in New York.  \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par    Not Just Asian Central Banks Anymore? \par    \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Along with Japan, Asian countries with pegs to the dollar such as China have been forced to buy U.S. bonds to keep their \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currencies\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  in line with the dollar. Judging by overnight \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 intervention\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  by monetary authorities in \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 Japan\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2 , China, Taiwan and South Korea - on the first trading day after the G7 gathering, there's little hint that this policy will lose its popularity.  \f28 \par \par \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 Intervention\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  could even spread beyond Asia to Europe. With initial jawboning attempts having failed, Europeans are left with two options to boost the dollar versus the euro - cut rates or \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 intervene\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2 .  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 The former would have the most durable effect, say analysts at French investment bank CDC IXIS. They see the G7 statement as "euro positive," removing the last obstacle to the euro reaching $1.30. It fetched just under $1.27 Monday.  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 That would in turn be a plus for euro-zone debt if it spurred a half-point reduction in ECB rates, CDC IXIS said.  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Still, rate cuts seem unlikely in the near term. European \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 Central Bank\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  officials have showed no desire to cut rates from 2% - which is twice the level set by the Federal Reserve - despite the rising euro. The ECB has stressed that the recovery is underway and that rising global growth will benefit the region's economy.  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 "All experience shows that the dynamic of world trade and the world economy has a far bigger influence than the level of the exchange rate," Bundesbank President and ECB Governing Council Member Ernst Welteke said Monday.  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Yet ECB dollar buying seems back on the table as an option, which if it materializes would give still another lift to Treasurys and the U.S. economy via rate-sensitive sector like housing.  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Belgian Finance Minister Didier Reynders, part of the Eurogroup of euro-zone finance ministers, said Monday that \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 intervention\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  to stem to the euro's rise was "possible."  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 "This is something that needs to be resolved with the cooperation of all the central banks," Reynders said. "This won't happen today or tomorrow, but it's possible one day."  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 "It seems it would make the most sense to hold the course on what you think is the right (monetary) policy and squash \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 foreign-exchange\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  speculation by \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 intervening\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2 ," said Gay.  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 "None of this bodes for much higher U.S. long rates anytime soon," he added.  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 (Brian Blackstone writes about U.S. and global markets for Dow Jones Newswires. Previously, he had covered the Federal Reserve and European economics and politics for Dow Jones in New York and Paris)  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 -By Brian Blackstone; Dow Jones Newswires; 201-938-2137; brian.blackstone@dowjones.com  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par    (John Miller in Brussels and Ana Campoy in Frankfurt contributed to this article) \par    \f28 \par \par \uc2 4083  \par \par \uc2 Dokument DJI0000020040209e029000v8\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw184\pich22\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2760\pichgoal330
