\par\par \b \uc2 WRAP: Asia Central Banks Defy G7, Keep Supporting Dlr  \b0\par\par\uc2 1,192 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 9 Februar 2004\par 09:34\par \uc2 Dow Jones International News\par \uc2 DJI\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 2004 Dow Jones & Company, Inc.  \par \par \uc2 SINGAPORE (Dow Jones)--China and other Asian countries, rebuffing a call by the industrial powers to let the region's \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2  rise, Monday bought dollars and signaled they remain intent on curbing their \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2 ' gains.  \par \par \uc2 Finance chiefs of the Group of Seven nations called Saturday for more flexibility in exchange rates - a veiled push for China, Taiwan and South Korea in particular to let their \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2  rise against the dollar and take some pressure off the surging euro.  \par \par \uc2 But Taipei and Seoul followed G7 member \b \highlight22\uc2 Japan\b0 \highlight\uc2  in \b \highlight22\uc2 intervening\b0 \highlight\uc2  again in the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  markets Monday, traders said, and indicated they don't intend to let rising local \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2  price their exporters out of competition. China's \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  strongly denied a report that it will alter the yuan's peg to the dollar in the next couple of months to let the Chinese \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  appreciate.  \par \par \uc2 "It's a fantasy to believe they (Asian central banks) will change policies overnight and decide to stay out of the \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  markets after the G7," said an American bank trader in Taipei.  \par \par \uc2 Investors will likely continue pushing Asian \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2  higher in the coming days and weeks to test Asian central banks' willingness to defy the G7, traders said. Market participants expect central banks and finance ministries throughout the region to continue putting up a fight, but they largely predict the region's \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2  will keep grinding higher against the beleaguered dollar.  \par \par \uc2 The dollar rallied briefly in Asia after G7 finance ministers and central bankers, meeting in Boca Raton, Fla., criticized "excess volatility and disorderly movements" in the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  markets and sought "more flexibility in exchange rates."  \par \par \uc2 Market participants view the statement to mean the G7 - the U.S., Japan, Germany, France, the U.K., Italy and Canada, as well as the European \b \highlight22\uc2 Central Bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  - think the euro has risen enough and want Asian authorities to stop \b \highlight22\uc2 intervening\b0 \highlight\uc2  to keep their \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2  weak, and thus their exports cheap.  \par \par \uc2 But Tokyo made clear any hands-off message didn't apply to it. Senior Finance Ministry official Zembei Mizoguchi said Japan "will continue to take appropriate action" to temper the yen's rise, and the Bank of Japan was believed to have sold yen for dollars early in the Asian session. Other regional authorities followed suit.  \par \par \uc2 "We don't expect significant changes in the \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  market due to the G7," said Lee Jae-Ouk, assistant governor at the Bank of Korea. "But Japan's moves will likely influence other Asian countries, including us."  \par \par \uc2 While the G7's call for freer exchange rates was meant to chasten Asia's \b \highlight22\uc2 interventionist\b0 \highlight\uc2  central banks, the latter group has countered that they are merely fighting \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  volatility by trying to smooth out market movements.  \par \par \uc2 Over the past year, the dollar has fallen 16% against the euro and 13% against the yen, despite \b \highlight22\uc2 Japan\b0 \highlight\uc2 's record yen-selling \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  campaign. In the same period, the greenback, essentially fixed against the yuan, has slipped just 4.4% against the New Taiwan dollar and a mere 2.2% against the Korean won. Indeed, it's now a tad higher against the Korean \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  than in the aftermath of the September G7 meeting, which first called for more exchange-rate flexibility.  \par \par \uc2 Washington is widely believed to be happy with the dollar's long decline as it helps U.S. manufacturers in an election year. But the Europeans fear the euro's surge to last month's record high against the dollar could derail the eurozone's recovery. They are annoyed, too, that the common \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  has gained more than Asian units. \b \highlight22\uc2 Japan\b0 \highlight\uc2 , the only overt \b \highlight22\uc2 interventionist\b0 \highlight\uc2  in the G7, has sold Y27 trillion, mostly for dollars, over the past 13 months to keep the nation's export-reliant recovery going.  \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par          China Firm, Taiwan Mum, Korea Adamant \par    \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 The G7's weekend call for more \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currency\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  flexibility from economies "that lack such flexibility" was seen as chiefly targeting China, which pegs the yuan to a tight trading band less than half a percent above or below 8.2770 yuan to the dollar. U.S. Treasury Secretary John Snow has for months urged Beijing to ease restrictions on the yuan, which is convertible only on the current account. China has rejected any calls for quick action, reiterating only that it aims - eventually - at a fully convertible yuan, or renminbi.  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Just as the market was digesting the G7 statement Monday, China's \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 central bank\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  denied a report in the unofficial China Business Post that the authorities would revalue the yuan next month by widening the existing trading band to 5% and then to 10% by the end of 2005.  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 "This is the newspaper's opinion - it isn't a \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 central bank\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  decision," a spokesman for the People's Bank of China told Dow Jones Newswires. "Currently the bank has no specific plans for renminbi changes. There is no timetable for renminbi reform."  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 China's leadership has told the U.S. that a market-determined exchange rate is the country's long-term aim but also argues that it must first complete reforms to its financial system before exposing the yuan to a free float.  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 For the moment, investors appear to believe the change isn't imminent. The market for nondeliverable yuan forward contracts, where investors can bet on a future change in the spot rate, Monday was pricing in a 5.1% rise in the yuan against the dollar over the next 12 months but at most a 1% rise over the next three months.  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 "If the markets really thought this was going to happen next month, then the one-month and three-month China NDFs would be trading where the one-year is right now," said a trader at a U.S. bank in Singapore.  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Across the Taiwan Strait, authorities in Taipei were selling the New Taiwan dollar, despite the G7 statement, traders said. But the \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 central bank\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2 's U.S. dollar-buying was apparently aimed at slowing, not reversing the local \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currency\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2 's climb to 18-month highs, they said.  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 The \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 central bank\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  doesn't comment on its actions in the market. But a person familiar with the situation said the bank views the G7 statement as "neutral."  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 U.S. fundamentals argue for the dollar to rise against the euro and yen, but the gaping U.S. budget and current-account deficits suggest otherwise, this person told Dow Jones Newswires. "Therefore, how the statement will affect the New Taiwan dollar will depend on which side of the argument the market focuses on," he said.  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 In Seoul, the authorities were more adamant, with a government official telling Dow Jones Newswires the G7 statement will actually ease upward pressure on the won.  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 "We think the meeting results could reverse the dollar's weakening trend," said Yoon Yeo-Kwon, director of the finance ministry's \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 foreign exchange\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  market division.  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par    -By William Mallard, Dow Jones Newswires; 65-6415-4041; billy.mallard@dowjones.com \par    (Perris Lee in Taipei, Hye-Seung Seo in Seoul, Owen Brown with Steven Yang in Beijing, Leika Kihara in Tokyo and Benjamin Pedley in Singapore, contributed to this item.) \par    \f28 \par \par \uc2 Dokument DJI0000020040209e029000bv\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw184\pich22\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2760\pichgoal330
