\par\par \b \uc2 Asia  \highlight22\uc2 Forex \highlight\uc2 : Dollar Falls Vs Euro After Brief G7 Rally  \b0\par\par\uc2 By Leika Kihara  \par \uc2 Of DOW JONES NEWSWIRES  \par \uc2 799 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 9 Februar 2004\par 06:42\par \uc2 Dow Jones International News\par \uc2 DJI\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 2004 Dow Jones & Company, Inc.  \par \par \uc2 TOKYO (Dow Jones)--A brief dollar rally against the euro in Asia Monday quickly fizzled as traders concluded that the Group of Seven's warning against "excess volatility" in \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  rates doesn't change the overall weak-dollar outlook.  \par \par \uc2 In a joint communique issued after their meeting in Boca Raton, Fla., over the weekend, the G7 finance officials added a new phrase warning that "excess volatility" and "disorderly movements" in exchange rates were "undesirable for economic growth."  \par \par \uc2 The phrase ignited euro selling for dollars, as market players had little doubt it was aimed at taking pressure off the euro, which had soared around 14% against the dollar since the previous G7 meeting in Dubai last September.  \par \par \uc2 After falling as low as $1.2575, however, the euro climbed back above $1.2700 on buybacks by market players who had reduced euro long-positions ahead of the G7 meeting.  \par \par \uc2 Some Asian central banks were rumored to be buying euros for dollars after confirming the single \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2 's downside was solid, traders said.  \par \par \uc2 The dollar saw a similar pattern against the yen - rising as high as Y105.90 on buybacks but then losing steam on heavy offers by Japanese exporters.  \par \par \uc2 The change in the G7 communique's wording caught some market players by surprise, as the dominant pre-G7 view was for no major change in its reference on \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  matters.  \par \par \uc2 But the statement didn't send a strong enough message to turn the current dollar-bearish tide around and eliminate market wariness over the huge U.S. budget and trade deficits, traders said.  \par \par \uc2 "For the dollar-bearish trend to change, the U.S. government needs to send a clearer message it's modifying its \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  policy," said Yasunari Ueno, chief market economist at Mizuho Securities.  \par \par \uc2 "But that's unlikely to come soon since the U.S. government seems to be sticking with its stance of tolerating the dollar's gradual decline" caused by market forces, Ueno said.  \par \par \uc2 At 0515 GMT, the dollar was quoted at Y105.60, above Y105.45 late Friday in New York. The dollar was at Y106.08 late Friday in Tokyo.  \par \par \uc2 The euro was at $1.2748, higher than $1.2697 late Friday in New York. The euro was at $1.2554 in Tokyo Friday.  \par \par \uc2 Against the yen, the euro was quoted at Y134.59, up from Y133.94 in New York late Friday. The euro was at Y133.15 in Tokyo late Friday.  \par \par \uc2 Sterling was at $1.8542, up from $1.8350 in New York and higher than $1.8350 in Tokyo Friday.  \par \par \uc2 Japan's monetary authorities appeared keen to avoid a recurrence of the Dubai G7 experience, when the communique's call for "flexibility" in exchange rates was widely interpreted as criticism of \b \highlight22\uc2 Japan\b0 \highlight\uc2 's yen-selling \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  and caused the yen to surge against the dollar.  \par \par \uc2 The Boca Raton statement retained the call for flexibility and added a portion saying \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  flexibility was desirable "for major countries or economic areas that lack such flexibility" in order to promote smooth adjustments in the international finance system.  \par \par \uc2 Hiroshi Watanabe, head of the \b \highlight22\uc2 Ministry of Finance\b0 \highlight\uc2 's international bureau, chose to highlight the phrase warning against "excess volatility," saying it applied not only to euro/dollar rates but also to dollar/yen movements.  \par \par \uc2 "The phrase means if there is excess volatility or inappropriate moves in the market, we will take necessary action," Watanabe said, suggesting the MOF's determination to keep acting to prevent the yen's sharp rise from hurting Japan's export-led economic recovery.  \par \par \uc2 Tomoko Fujii, strategist at Nikko Citigroup, expects \b \highlight22\uc2 Japan\b0 \highlight\uc2 's \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  stance to remain largely unchanged from that before the G7 meeting.  \par \par \uc2 "\b \highlight22\uc2 Japan\b0 \highlight\uc2  will \b \highlight22\uc2 intervene\b0 \highlight\uc2  only up to a level where it won't be interpreted as intentionally guiding the yen lower, so that the United States can tolerate it," Fujii said.  \par \par \uc2 Fujii believes the MOF will try to keep defending the Y105 line, where many Japanese export firms have set their in-house exchange rate forecasts.  \par \par \uc2 But the dollar/yen's line-in-the-sand may be shifted lower, or toward further yen strength, "if Japan's economic indicators, exporters' earnings and stock prices show firmness" and lead to a higher yen, she said.  \par \par \uc2 -By Leika Kihara, Dow Jones Newswires; 813-5255-2929; leika.kihara@dowjones.com  \par \par \uc2 -Edited by Alastair Gale  \par \par \uc2 The chart of late Tokyo \b \highlight22\uc2 forex\b0 \highlight\uc2  rates that accompanied Asia \b \highlight22\uc2 Forex\b0 \highlight\uc2  has been replaced with an enhanced \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign-exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  fixture that is updated hourly.  \par \par \uc2 Subscribers can find this table:  \par \par \uc2 -On Telerate pages 4046 and 1087.  \par \par \uc2 -On Dow Jones Newswires by searching the code N/IFX  \par \par \uc2 -On Reuters by entering FRX or INT  \par \par \uc2 -On Bloomberg by entering NI FRX  \par \par \uc2 1074  \par \par \uc2 